  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-14",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Volatility Surface & Correlation Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Source, construct, validate, and publish the volatility and correlation parameters that option valuation and risk measurement depend on — by commodity, location, and tenor per approved methodology — so valuations and measures rest on governed market parameters, not ad hoc inputs."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). The market-data sibling to curves; same ownership pattern. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.14"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Volatilities And Correlations"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers volatility and correlation ownership: sourcing implied and historical inputs, constructing surfaces and matrices per approved methodology, validating and handling exceptions, and publishing the official parameter set to valuation (CM-1-2-2-3-15) and risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3-7). Methodology approval and independent validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions; curves are the sibling at CM-1-2-2-3-3.\nIn scope: Source implied and historical volatility and correlation inputs | construct surfaces and matrices per approved methodology | validate parameters and handle exceptions | publish the official parameter set\nOut of scope: Methodology approval and independent validation (designated functions) | curve construction (CM-1-2-2-3-3) | option trading decisions (front office)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "implied and historical market data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "parameter methodologies"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "published official volatility and correlation set"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Governed parameters behind every option value and correlated measure."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-15",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12953"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002b"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Mark-to-Market Management"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trading Book Valuation"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Produce and control the official valuations of the trading book — marking positions to market or, where approved, to model per approved methodology from the official curves and parameters, applying approved valuation adjustments, investigating exceptions, and publishing the valuations that P&L, risk, exposure, and reconciliation run on."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Adjustment ownership clarified at the decision owner's direction: this process executes approved valuation-control procedures — adjustment categories apply only where relevant to the portfolio and approved methodology, and policy, accounting classification, and model methodology are owned elsewhere; which adjustments are material for the portfolio is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. The valuation node batches 08 and 11 point at. Model-risk boundaries per CCRO Model Risk practice as evidence. APQC 12953 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.15"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Commodity Valuations"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers valuation ownership: running mark-to-market and approved mark-to-model valuations from the official curves (CM-1-2-2-3-3) and parameters (CM-1-2-2-3-14), applying approved valuation adjustments, independent price verification against external sources where required, exception investigation, and publication to position and P&L (CM-1-2-2-3-9), risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3-7), and credit exposure (CM-1-2-2-1-4). Valuation adjustments may include approved liquidity, model, credit, funding, or other adjustments where applicable to the portfolio and approved methodology; this process executes the approved valuation-control procedures — it does not approve valuation policy, accounting classification, or model methodology, which sit with the designated governance, Accounting (Manage Accounting Treatments, CM-1-2-4-2-1), and validation functions. Structured-deal representation fidelity is the capture side's duty (CM-1-2-1-3-8) with valuation representation coordinated here.\nIn scope: Run mark-to-market and approved mark-to-model valuations | apply approved valuation adjustments per procedure | perform independent price verification where required | investigate exceptions and publish official valuations\nOut of scope: Valuation policy, methodology approval, and independent model validation (designated governance and validation functions) | accounting classification and valuation treatments (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | structured-deal capture representation (CM-1-2-1-3-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-14"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "official curves and parameters"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "validated positions"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "valuation methodologies and approved adjustments"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "published official trading-book valuations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One official value per position, methodologically defensible, every day."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16385"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trading Delegation of Authority Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Establish and maintain the trading delegation of authority — who may trade what products, instruments, tenors, and sizes, and which approvals are required beyond each authority — keeping it current with roles, strategies, and governance decisions so every trade's authorization has a defined basis."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Set here, applied at CM-1-2-1-3-2 — closing the set-versus-apply loop recorded in batches 07-09. Which body owns risk appetite and final limit-exception approval is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. APQC 16385 is a generic framework candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Establish & Maintain Delegation Of Authority (DOA)"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the authority framework: defining and documenting delegated trading authorities by role, product, instrument, tenor, and size; maintaining them as people, strategies, and appetite change; and publishing them to the processes that apply them. The DOA is applied — never set — by Obtain Trade Approval (CM-1-2-1-3-2) and aligned to by Trading Strategy Management (CM-1-2-1-4), per the batch 07-08 decisions; authority levels derive from governance decisions owned by the designated risk-governance body. Excludes HR role administration; excludes system access provisioning (Process Excellence & IT).\nIn scope: Define and document delegated authorities by role, product, instrument, tenor, and size | maintain authorities as roles, strategies, and appetite change | publish the DOA to the applying processes | keep authority history auditable\nOut of scope: Applying the DOA to individual trades (CM-1-2-1-3-2) | risk-appetite decisions (designated risk-governance body) | HR role administration | system access provisioning (Process Excellence & IT)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governance and appetite decisions"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "role and strategy changes"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "current, published, auditable delegation of authority"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Every trade's authorization has a defined, current, auditable basis."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Forward Curve Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Construct and maintain the forward price curves the organization values and measures risk with — sourcing market data, building and validating curves by commodity, location, and tenor per approved methodology, and publishing the official curve set on the control calendar."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). The curves consumed by batches 07 and 11 are owned here; methodology-qualified per the batch 11 discipline. Market-data vendor and licensing ownership is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Setup And Manage Curves"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers curve ownership: market-data sourcing and quality checks, curve construction per approved methodology including illiquid-tenor and basis treatment, validation and exception handling, and publication of the official daily curve set consumed by valuation, risk measurement, credit exposure (CM-1-2-2-1-4), and revenue projection (CM-1-2-1-1-5). Curve methodology approval and independent validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions — this process constructs and publishes, it does not approve its own methodology. Excludes market-data vendor selection and licensing, which sit with the applicable procurement and Process Excellence & IT processes per the operating model; excludes volatilities and correlations (CM-1-2-2-3-14).\nIn scope: Source and quality-check market data | construct curves per approved methodology | validate curves and handle exceptions | publish the official curve set on the control calendar\nOut of scope: Curve methodology approval and independent validation (designated governance and validation functions) | market-data vendor selection and licensing (procurement, Process Excellence & IT) | volatilities and correlations (CM-1-2-2-3-14) | trading views on prices (front office)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "control calendar"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "curve methodologies"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market prices and broker quotes"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "published official curve set"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One official, defensible curve set that everything values against."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19568"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Daily Risk Control Cycle"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate and evidence the daily trading-risk control cycle — performing or obtaining capture-completeness checks, freezing the official market-data set, running or obtaining valuation, position, P&L, and risk-measure outputs, investigating material exceptions, and obtaining the designated control approvals for the daily controlled result."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Reworded at the decision owner's direction: the cycle is coordinated and evidenced with designated control approvals rather than a single universal sign-off — different functions may attest different elements per the operating model. An orchestration process over sibling-owned outputs. APQC 19568 is a process-control candidate; false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform End-of-day (EOD) Process"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the end-of-day control cycle as coordinated orchestration: capture-completeness checks performed or obtained from their owners, the official market-data and curve freeze, valuation and risk runs, position and P&L production, material-exception investigation, and the designated control approvals — which may sit with different functions (front-office attestation of completeness, risk sign-off of control results, operations validation of settlement status) per the operating model; no single universal sign-off authority is asserted. Orchestrates outputs owned by the sibling processes (curves at CM-1-2-2-3-3, valuations at CM-1-2-2-3-15, measures at CM-1-2-2-3-7, position and P&L at CM-1-2-2-3-9) without owning their methodologies. Excludes the monthly cycle (CM-1-2-2-3-5); excludes accounting close (CM-1-2-4-2).\nIn scope: Perform or obtain capture-completeness checks | freeze the official market-data set | run or obtain valuation, position, P&L, and risk outputs | investigate material exceptions and obtain designated control approvals\nOut of scope: Methodology and output ownership (sibling processes) | monthly cycle (CM-1-2-2-3-5) | accounting close (CM-1-2-4-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-15"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "captured trades"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "exception reports"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market data and curves"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "evidenced daily controlled result with designated approvals"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Every trading day closes once, evidenced, with its designated approvals obtained."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19568"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monthly Risk Control Cycle"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate and evidence the month-end trading-risk control cycle — reconciling and reviewing the month's daily controlled results, producing month-end economic valuation, position, P&L, and risk outputs, resolving or formally carrying forward approved exceptions — and provide the controlled economic inputs required by Accounting's reconciliation and close processes."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Reworded at the decision owner's direction: coordinated and evidenced with designated approvals, and framed as the economic-input provider to Accounting's reconciliation and close — supply, never ownership, of CM-1-2-4-2-6's economic side. APQC 19568 false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the end-of-month control cycle: month-end valuation and risk runs, reconciliation and review of daily controlled results across the month, resolution or documented carry-forward of approved exceptions, provision of the controlled economic inputs to Reconcile Economic PNL to Accounting PNL (CM-1-2-4-2-6) and to reporting, and the designated month-end control approvals per the operating model. Excludes the accounting close and postings, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); excludes reconciliation ownership itself — CM-1-2-4-2-6 owns the economic-to-accounting reconciliation and this process supplies the economic side, a boundary the Settlements & Accounting batch inherits. When the report includes the corresponding month-end controlled outputs, Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process (CM-1-2-2-3-5) supplies controlled month-end economic valuation, position, profit-and-loss, and risk outputs that Manage Risk Reporting (CM-1-2-2-3-13) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Run month-end valuations and risk measures | reconcile and review the month's daily controlled results | resolve or formally carry forward approved exceptions | provide controlled economic inputs to Accounting's reconciliation and close\nOut of scope: Accounting close and postings (CM-1-2-4-2) | economic-to-accounting reconciliation ownership (CM-1-2-4-2-6) | financial reporting (Finance)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "accounting calendar"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "evidenced daily results"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "month-end market data"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "evidenced month-end economic result set with controlled inputs to Accounting"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The month closes once, reconciled, with risk and accounting telling one story."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11211"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Market Risk Limit Administration"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Establish, maintain, publish, and adjust market-risk limits — by book, strategy, commodity, tenor, and measure — derived from approved risk appetite; administer documented temporary-excess and exception decisions under delegated authority; and tighten or suspend limits when conditions or policy triggers require it."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Mirrors the batch 11 credit-limits rule exactly: establish, publish, administer; enforcement through connected controls; appetite above. APQC 11211 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Risk Limits"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the market-risk limit lifecycle: establishing limits from appetite across the measures the approved framework uses (such as value-at-risk, position, stop-loss, and tenor limits), publishing them to the applying and monitoring processes, administering and recording temporary-excess approvals under the delegation of authority, and adjusting limits on regime change or deterioration. Enforcement occurs through connected controls — pre-trade checks, monitoring and escalation (CM-1-2-2-3-8), and policy-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10). Risk appetite itself is set by the designated risk-governance body. Excludes credit limits (CM-1-2-2-1-2). Trading Strategy Management (CM-1-2-1-4) aligns each strategy within the published market-risk limits set by Manage Risk Limits (CM-1-2-2-3-6); the limits bound strategy choices but do not give the limit process strategy ownership.\nIn scope: Establish and structure market-risk limits from approved appetite | publish limits to applying and monitoring processes | administer and record temporary-excess decisions under delegated authority | tighten or suspend limits on triggers\nOut of scope: Risk-appetite setting (designated risk-governance body) | limit enforcement through connected controls (CM-1-2-2-3-8, CM-1-2-2-3-10, pre-trade checks) | credit limits (CM-1-2-2-1-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-11"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-12"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "approved risk appetite"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governance decisions"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "risk measures and utilization"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "current, approved, published market-risk limits and exception decisions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Market exposure capacity always matches approved appetite."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/triggeredBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-8"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-7",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16436"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Market Risk Measurement"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Compute the organization's market-risk measures — value-at-risk, sensitivities, position and tenor exposures, and the other measures the approved framework requires — from validated positions, curves, and volatilities, publishing them on the control calendar so limits, monitoring, and reporting run on one measured picture."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Methodology-qualified per the batch 11 discipline: the measures the approved framework requires, never an asserted universal metric set. APQC 16436 is a generic benchmarking candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.7"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Measure Risk"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers risk measurement: running the approved measures per methodology against validated inputs, quality-checking and explaining day-on-day moves, and publishing results to monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-8), limits (CM-1-2-2-3-6), and reporting (CM-1-2-2-3-13). Which measures apply is set by the approved framework; measure methodologies and their independent validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions. Excludes scenario and stress analysis (CM-1-2-2-3-11); excludes credit exposure measurement (CM-1-2-2-1-4).\nIn scope: Run approved risk measures per methodology | quality-check and explain measure movements | publish measures on the control calendar | maintain measurement input traceability\nOut of scope: Measure methodology approval and independent validation (designated functions) | scenario and stress analysis (CM-1-2-2-3-11) | credit exposure (CM-1-2-2-1-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "approved measure methodologies"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "official curves and volatilities"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "validated positions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "published market-risk measures"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One measured, methodology-true risk picture per control cycle."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-8",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20729"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Market Risk Surveillance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Continuously watch market risk against limits — detecting breaches and near-breaches, escalating them per policy, tracking them to resolution, and watching concentration and regime signals — so no excursion goes unseen or unescalated."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). The batch 04 detect-versus-decide pattern, as applied to credit in batch 11. APQC 20729 is an IT-risk candidate; false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.8"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor Risk"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers market-risk surveillance: measure-versus-limit monitoring, breach and near-breach detection, escalation and resolution tracking, and watching for concentrations and market-regime changes that warrant limit or appetite review. The detect-versus-decide split holds: this process surfaces; limit actions are taken at Manage Risk Limits (CM-1-2-2-3-6) and appetite questions go to the risk-governance body. Excludes credit surveillance (CM-1-2-2-1-7); excludes risk-policy compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10). When Monitor Risk (CM-1-2-2-3-8) identifies a limit breach, or a near-breach meeting the approved escalation criteria, it refers the finding to Manage Risk Limits (CM-1-2-2-3-6) for assessment and an authorized limit-management decision. The referral does not automatically authorize a temporary excess, limit change, or suspension.\nIn scope: Monitor measures against limits | detect and escalate breaches and near-breaches | track breaches to resolution | watch concentration and regime signals\nOut of scope: Limit decisions (CM-1-2-2-3-6) | appetite decisions (risk-governance body) | credit surveillance (CM-1-2-2-1-7) | policy-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "current limits"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "escalation policy"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "published risk measures"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "escalated, tracked limit excursions and regime alerts"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "No limit excursion goes unseen or unescalated."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-9",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Closes the batch 07 deferral: projected economics at CM-1-2-1-1-5, official controlled results here, accounting results at CM-1-2-4-2, reconciled at CM-1-2-4-2-6. Business-facing label 'Perform Position & P&L Analysis' queued for the naming pass per the decision owner — 'PNL' retained in the technical label, 'P&L' used in prose. APQC 11783 is the competitive-analysis false match batch 02 already rejected. Step 3d naming pass — normalization-only mechanical Batch 6 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Perform Position & PNL Analysis' to 'Perform Position & P&L Analysis'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11783"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "P&L Attribution"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Position & P&L Control"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Produce and analyze the official, controlled position and realized and unrealized P&L picture — explaining daily P&L by its drivers (price, position, new deals, amendments, and other effects), investigating unexplained amounts, and delivering the validated P&L that management, risk, and the accounting reconciliation consume."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.2.3.9"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Position & P&L Analysis"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers official position and P&L production and attribution: daily P&L explanation by driver, unexplained-P&L investigation and thresholds, position validation against capture, and delivery of the controlled result to management, reporting, and Reconcile Economic PNL to Accounting PNL (CM-1-2-4-2-6). This is the official, controlled figure that batch 07's Forecast Revenue explicitly defers to — the forward-looking projection lives there (CM-1-2-1-1-5); the governed result lives here. Excludes accounting P&L and postings, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); excludes valuation methodology (CM-1-2-2-3-15).\nIn scope: Produce the official controlled position and P&L | attribute P&L to drivers and investigate unexplained amounts | validate positions against capture | deliver controlled results to management, reporting, and reconciliation\nOut of scope: Forward revenue projection (CM-1-2-1-1-5) | accounting P&L and postings (CM-1-2-4-2) | valuation methodology (CM-1-2-2-3-15)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "deal activity"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "prior-day results"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "validated positions and valuations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "official, explained daily position and P&L"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One official P&L per day, explained to its drivers."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/priorPreferredLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Position & PNL Analysis"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualizations is performed by scheduler who assigns the final ticket volumes to the trades according to the preset priority."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualizations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 3
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Deal Actualization"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Movement Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Update trading deal, movement, and entitlement records with contractually applicable actual quantities, qualities, dates, statuses, and charge determinants from execution, custody-transfer, registry, and other agreed evidence, replacing planned or estimated values so settlement, economic P&L, risk, and downstream reporting use controlled actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). 'Actualization' is energy-trading (ETRM/CTRM) practice terminology: replacing planned or estimated deal and movement values with measured actuals. Concept-type: process per the batch 08 variant test — one per-movement lifecycle discipline applied across mode variants, like Trade Capture. 'Entitlement' covers commingled stock, exchanges, storage, and instrument arrangements where the commercial fact is not a uniquely identifiable physical parcel. Q2 decision (the decision owner, batch 13): charge-determinant actualization, not universal cost actualization — Actualizations records the verified operational and contractual facts needed to determine charges (actual quantity, quality, duration, route, capacity use, tariff/fee basis, dates, loss/gain treatment, and other rate inputs); Settlements applies rates, calculates settlement amounts, invoices, pays or receives, accrues, nets, and resolves financial disputes. Trading-record versus stock-record boundary: the same custody-transfer documents feed this process (trading deal, movement, and entitlement records) and the SCM inventory processes (physical stock and scheduling records); each updates its own record. Measurement authority: API MPMS (tank gauging Ch. 3; marine cargo Ch. 17; lease-tank custody transfer Ch. 18). 'P&L' prose convention per batch 12. APQC 19948 is an unrelated candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualizations"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the per-movement actualization lifecycle across transport and instrument modes: matching execution evidence to the captured deal and scheduled movement, recording actual quantities, qualities, dates, and statuses on the contractual measurement basis, recording verified charge determinants, and closing the movement in the trading record. Actualizations may record or validate charge determinants — such as tariff basis, freight basis, throughput quantity, storage days, accessorial events, loss allowances, quality adjustments, and contractual rate references — and may calculate provisional amounts where the controlled trading-system design assigns that function; it does not own invoice generation, final settlement calculation, payment, accounting accrual or posting, or financial dispute resolution. Actualization updates the trading system's deal, movement, and entitlement records; it consumes measurement results and does not perform physical measurement, which is executed at custody transfer by operating personnel and independent inspectors under the applicable measurement standards (API MPMS). Excludes trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3); confirmation of agreed terms (CM-1-2-2-2); settlement and invoicing (CM-1-2-4-1); per-movement closure in distribution scheduling and inventory records and network book-to-physical stock reconciliation, owned by Discharge & Reconcile Load (CM-1-1-3-6-4) and Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) under the locked SCM boundary; demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4). For each physical delivery or settlement period, Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1) records and validates the relevant actual quantities and movement facts before Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1) uses those inputs to calculate or validate the corresponding charges. Settlement preparation and contractually permitted provisional billing may proceed before every input is final; subsequent corrections are handled through controlled true-ups. For trade-linked physical movements, Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1) follows initial Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3), using the captured trade and associated movement references to record and validate actual quantities and other applicable movement facts. Subsequent amendments and corrections remain controlled.\nIn scope: Match execution and measurement evidence to deals and scheduled movements | record actual quantities, qualities, dates, and statuses on the contractual measurement basis | record verified charge determinants and, where the trading system is configured to do so, provisional or contractually derived charge amounts for downstream settlement validation | close movements in the trading record for settlement\nOut of scope: Physical measurement at custody transfer (operations and independent inspection per API MPMS) | trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2) | invoice generation, final settlement calculation, payment, and accrual (CM-1-2-4-1, Accounting) | distribution movement closure and stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-6-4, CM-1-1-3-7-5) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Captured deal and scheduled movement records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "carrier, terminal, and registry statements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "inspection and outturn reports"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "meter tickets, bills of lading, and run tickets"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized deal, movement, and entitlement records ready for settlement"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure the trading record reflects what was actually delivered, received, and measured, so settlement, accounting, and risk run on controlled actuals rather than estimates."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20042"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-doe-rppl-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualize pipeline movements of crude, feedstocks, and products — recording actual receipt and delivery volumes, qualities, and dates from pipeline meter tickets, carrier statements, and shipper reports, applying pipeline loss allowance, quality-bank, proration, or other carrier and tariff adjustments where applicable, and reconciling actuals against nominated and scheduled volumes — so pipeline transactions settle on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Pipeline loss allowance (PLA), quality banks, and proration are tariff mechanisms recorded at actualization only where the governing tariff provides them; the tariff itself is administered by the carrier under the applicable regulatory regime. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied: this process records determinants and validates charges; Settle Pipelines owns settlement. APQC 20042 'Monitor pipeline' is a WEAK operational-monitoring candidate, not this accounting activity."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Pipeline Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers matching pipeline carrier evidence to deals and scheduled movements, recording actual volumes on the tariff measurement basis, recording applicable carrier and tariff entitlement and quality-adjustment results, recording actual pipeline tariff and charge determinants and validating applicable transportation charges for settlement, and surfacing discrepancies between carrier-reported and scheduled volumes. Loss allowance, quality-bank, and proration mechanisms are tariff- and carrier-specific and apply only where the governing tariff provides them; a quality bank may adjust an entitlement or economic balance rather than a physical quality measurement. Excludes pipeline nomination and scheduling (the owning transportation and scheduling processes); pipeline settlement and invoicing (Settle Pipelines, CM-1-2-4-1-12); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9); actualization disputes (Manage Actualization Disputes, CM-1-2-3-1-8).\nIn scope: Match carrier tickets and statements to deals and movements | record actual volumes, qualities, and dates per tariff basis | record applicable carrier and tariff entitlement and quality-adjustment results where applicable | record actual pipeline tariff and charge determinants and validate applicable transportation charges for settlement\nOut of scope: Nomination and scheduling (owning transportation processes) | pipeline settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-12) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pipeline meter tickets and carrier statements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "scheduled movements and nominations"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "shipper reports"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "tariff terms"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-12"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized pipeline movement records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make pipeline movement records reflect carrier-measured actuals under the governing tariff terms."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10954"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualize truck movements of crude, feedstocks, and products — recording actual loaded and delivered volumes, qualities, and dates from bills of lading, terminal lifting records, and truck tickets, recorded on the contractually applicable measurement and temperature/volume basis — so truck transactions settle on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). The governing unit-of-measure basis may be gross, net, observed, standard, mass, or another contractual basis; not every truck transaction involves a temperature correction. Boundary to CM-1-2-3-1-6: mode-based rows partition by evidence and contract context — lease run tickets and first-purchase terms belong to Lease Actualization even though a truck performs the movement. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC 10954 'Perform abandonment' is a false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Truck Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers matching truck shipment evidence — rack lifting records, bills of lading, delivery tickets — to deals and scheduled movements, recording actual volumes and qualities on the contractually applicable measurement basis, recording verified freight-charge determinants and validating charge inputs for settlement, and surfacing load-versus-delivery discrepancies. Crude gathered from lease tanks by truck actualizes under Perform Lease Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6) with its run-ticket and contract-terms context. Excludes truck dispatch and scheduling (the owning logistics processes); truck settlement (Settle Trucks, CM-1-2-4-1-4); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).\nIn scope: Match bills of lading and lifting records to deals and movements | record actual volumes, qualities, and dates on the contractually applicable measurement basis | record verified freight-charge determinants and validate charge inputs for settlement | surface load-versus-delivery discrepancies\nOut of scope: Lease-tank truck gathering (CM-1-2-3-1-6) | truck dispatch and scheduling (logistics processes) | truck settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-4) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Bills of lading and delivery tickets"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "freight terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "scheduled movements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "terminal rack lifting records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized truck movement records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make truck movement records reflect measured lifted and delivered actuals on the contractual basis."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10954"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualize railcar movements of crude, feedstocks, and products — recording actual loaded and received quantities, weights, qualities, and dates from railcar shipping documents, scale weights, and gauging records on the contractually applicable basis — so rail transactions settle on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Rail quantities may be determined by scale weight, gauging, or mass balance according to commodity, equipment, carrier practice, and contract; the contractual measurement basis governs. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC 10954 is a false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Railcar Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers matching rail shipment evidence — bills of lading, railcar weight and volume determinations, inspection records — to deals and scheduled movements, recording actuals per railcar and shipment on the contractual basis, recording verified rail freight and accessorial charge determinants for settlement, and surfacing origin-versus-destination discrepancies. Quantities may be weight-, volume-, or mass-balance-based according to commodity, equipment, carrier practice, and contract. Excludes railcar fleet management and rail logistics scheduling (the owning logistics processes); rail settlement (Settle Rail, CM-1-2-4-1-13); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).\nIn scope: Match rail shipping documents to deals and movements | record actual quantities, weights, qualities, and dates per railcar on the contractual basis | record verified rail freight and accessorial charge determinants for settlement | surface origin-versus-destination discrepancies\nOut of scope: Railcar fleet management and rail scheduling (logistics processes) | rail settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-13) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Railcar bills of lading and shipping documents"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "freight terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "scale weights and gauging records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "scheduled movements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-13"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized rail movement records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make rail movement records reflect measured shipped and received actuals on the contractual basis."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10954"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Marine Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualize marine movements by vessel and barge — recording actual loaded and outturn quantities, qualities, and dates from bills of lading, independent inspection reports, and ship and shore figures per marine measurement practice, and identifying apparent voyage gains, losses, and quantity differences according to the contractual measurement basis — so marine transactions settle on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Marine measurement per API MPMS Ch. 17; whether a cargo settles on bill-of-lading or outturn quantity is a contractual term captured with the deal — actualization applies the contractual basis, it does not choose it. Load/discharge differences are recorded as apparent gains or losses: they may reflect measurement, temperature, density, timing, sampling, or contractual-settlement effects rather than physical loss. Actualization supplies time, notice, and event facts but does not make the demurrage determination (CM-1-2-4-4). Receipt-side outturn confirmation for distribution movements is owned by Discharge & Reconcile Load (CM-1-1-3-6-4); this row owns the trading-record actualization. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers matching marine cargo evidence — bill of lading and outturn quantities, load- and discharge-port inspection reports, ship and shore measurements, timesheets and statements of fact for date actuals — to deals and scheduled voyages, following marine cargo measurement practice (API MPMS Ch. 17); recording actuals on the contractual bill-of-lading or outturn basis; identifying and recording apparent voyage gains, losses, and quantity differences according to the contractual measurement basis, agreed measurement hierarchy, and applicable claim process; and recording verified freight, laytime, and voyage-cost determinants for settlement. Relevant time, notice, and event facts may be made available to Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4); that process determines potential demurrage under the applicable charter party, contract, or terminal terms. Excludes vessel chartering and voyage operations (the owning chartering and marine logistics processes); marine settlement (Settle Vessels & Barge, CM-1-2-4-1-5); demurrage claim identification and management (CM-1-2-4-4); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8). Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) supplies actualized marine time, notice, and event facts that Identify Potential Demurrage (CM-1-2-4-4-1) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Match bills of lading, outturn, and inspection reports to deals and voyages | record actuals on the contractual bill-of-lading or outturn basis | identify and record apparent voyage gains, losses, and quantity differences per the agreed measurement hierarchy | record verified freight, laytime, and voyage-cost determinants for settlement\nOut of scope: Chartering and voyage operations (marine logistics processes) | marine settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-5) | demurrage determination and claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Bills of lading and outturn reports"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "independent inspection reports"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "statements of fact and timesheets"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "voyage and freight terms"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized marine movement records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make marine movement records reflect inspected load and outturn actuals and make apparent voyage differences visible."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10954"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualize terminal and storage transactions — recording transaction-level receipt, delivery, throughput, and contractually relevant storage-balance actuals for the associated deal or capacity arrangement from terminal operator statements, tank gauging records, and throughput reports, and recording verified throughput, storage, blending, and handling charge determinants and applicable contractual bases — so terminal and storage transactions settle on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Trading-record versus stock-record boundary applied: terminal statements actualize transaction-level actuals and charge determinants for the associated deal or capacity arrangement here, while periodic book-to-physical stock reconciliation across the network remains with CM-1-1-3-7-5 (EI HM 32 practice). Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC 10954 is a false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Terminal/Storage Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers matching terminal operator evidence — receipt and delivery tickets, tank gauging and inventory statements, throughput reports — to deals, scheduled movements, and capacity arrangements; recording transaction-level receipt, delivery, throughput, and contractually relevant storage-balance actuals per movement and period; recognizing terminal gains and losses per the agreement's loss-allowance terms; and recording verified throughput, storage, blending, and handling charge determinants and applicable contractual bases. Storage-deal terms come from capture (Capture Storage Deals, CM-1-2-1-3-5). Excludes terminal operations and physical measurement (terminal operator); terminal and storage settlement (Settle Terminal/Storage, CM-1-2-4-1-14); network book-to-physical stock reconciliation, owned by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).\nIn scope: Match terminal statements and tickets to deals and movements | record transaction-level receipt, delivery, throughput, and contractually relevant storage-balance actuals | recognize terminal gains and losses per agreement terms | record verified throughput, storage, blending, and handling charge determinants and applicable contractual bases\nOut of scope: Terminal operations and physical measurement (terminal operator) | terminal and storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Terminal receipt and delivery tickets"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "tank gauging and inventory statements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "terminal and storage agreement terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "throughput reports"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-14"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized terminal and storage transaction records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make terminal and storage transaction records reflect operator-measured actuals and contractual charge determinants."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10954"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "First-Purchase Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualize crude and condensate lease purchases — recording actual volumes, gravities, and qualities from run tickets and LACT meter records taken at lease-tank custody transfer, applying the contractual gravity, quality, and deduction adjustments and division-of-interest references — so first-purchase transactions settle on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). 'Lease' in the crude-marketing first-purchase sense per batch 09; the batch 09 follow-up on whether lease-purchase activity is genuinely downstream-owned still stands and is carried in the batch 13 PR. Run-ticket measurement per API MPMS Ch. 18.1 (manual gauging, lease tanks to truck); LACT (lease automatic custody transfer) metering where installed. APQC 10954 is a false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform Lease Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers matching lease custody-transfer evidence — run tickets from manual gauging or LACT meter records — to lease purchase contracts; recording actual volumes and qualities per lease and run; applying gravity and quality adjustments, deductions, and division-of-interest references maintained under Manage Lease Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-8); and closing lease runs for settlement. Measurement procedures at the lease tank follow API MPMS Ch. 18 custody-transfer practice, performed by the gathering party. Included only where the downstream organization purchases at the lease/wellhead, per the batch 09 scope caveat. This process does not include upstream leasehold administration, land management, production allocation, royalty calculation policy, or exploration-and-production accounting. Excludes lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8); first-purchaser settlement (Settle First Purchaser/Leases, CM-1-2-4-1-6); royalty and interest payment execution (Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments, CM-1-2-4-2-3); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8). Perform Lease Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6) follows establishment of the applicable effective lease-contract terms and required division-of-interest references maintained under Manage Lease Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-8), applying them to the relevant delivery or production period. Subsequent changes and corrections are handled through controlled updates.\nIn scope: Match run tickets and LACT records to lease contracts | record actual volumes, gravities, and qualities per lease and run | apply contractual adjustments, deductions, and division-of-interest references | close lease runs for settlement\nOut of scope: Upstream leasehold administration, land management, production allocation, royalty calculation policy, and exploration-and-production accounting (outside scope) | lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | first-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | royalty and interest payments (CM-1-2-4-2-3) | physical gauging and metering (gathering party per API MPMS) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-1-2-8"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Run tickets and LACT meter records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "lease contract terms and division-of-interest references (CM-1-2-1-2-8)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "scheduled lease runs"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized lease purchase records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make lease purchase records reflect run-ticket and meter actuals under contractual terms."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-7",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10954"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-emts-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-rec-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-rfs-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Environmental Instrument Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Update environmental-attribute and renewable-instrument trading records from authoritative registry lifecycle events — including issuance or generation, separation where applicable, transfer, acquisition, sale, retirement, and cancellation — and reconcile internal instrument positions to the applicable registry or regulatory system of record."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Q3 decision (the decision owner): approve operational registry-event processing where the function is authorized — this process records, submits, receives, reconciles, and evidences transaction-level registry lifecycle events where the organization has assigned registry-account authority and the event is part of the transaction lifecycle; EHS & Government Reporting retains regulatory policy, compliance applicability, periodic compliance demonstrations, attestations, regulatory reporting, and final compliance accountability. Registry events are not necessarily submitted by this process — they may be initiated by a compliance team, producer, obligated party, registry administrator, or delegated service provider per the operating model (organizational neutrality, batch 10). EMTS is the RFS database of record for RIN transactions (40 CFR 80.1452); RECs are tracked in regional energy-attribute tracking systems whose specific operating rules are registry-specific — EPA material is context, not a universal registry-operating standard. Report Trades to SDR precedent (batch 08) applied. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.7"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Perform RINs/REC Actualization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers transaction-level recording, controlled submission or instruction of registry events where authorized, receipt of registry confirmations, internal-to-registry position reconciliation, exception investigation, and evidence retention for RIN, REC, and other in-scope environmental instruments. For US RFS transactions, EMTS transaction reporting is subject to the applicable EPA rules and account-holder and delegation model (40 CFR 80.1452). For RECs, registry actions are subject to the applicable regional tracking system and program rules; program applicability is jurisdiction-specific. Excludes regulatory-policy interpretation, compliance-applicability and compliance-accountability decisions, periodic regulatory filing, attestation, audit response, renewable-volume-obligation demonstration, and broader EHS compliance reporting, owned by EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule; position forecasting (Forecast Renewables, CM-1-2-1-1-3); deal capture (Capture Environmental/Renewable Deals, CM-1-2-1-3-6); environmental instrument settlement (Settle Environmental Instruments, CM-1-2-4-1-8); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).\nIn scope: Record registry lifecycle events against deals and positions | submit or instruct registry events where the organization has assigned registry-account authority | reconcile internal instrument positions to EMTS and registry records | investigate exceptions and retain registry evidence\nOut of scope: Regulatory policy, compliance accountability, periodic filings, attestations, audit response, and RVO demonstrations (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3) | deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Captured environmental and renewable deals (CM-1-2-1-3-6)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "EMTS transaction records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "blending and production data for RIN separation events"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "registry statements and confirmations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-8"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized instrument positions reconciled to EMTS and registries"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep instrument positions verified against the applicable regulatory and registry systems of record."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-8",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage disputes over what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed — logging and classifying quantity, quality, and measurement discrepancies, investigating against custody-transfer evidence and independent inspection, coordinating investigation, evidence exchange, and agreement or adjudication of corrected actuals with the relevant external parties under the governing agreement and applicable measurement hierarchy, and closing disputes through corrected actualized records."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Dispute partition rule (the decision owner, batch 10) applied verbatim: confirmation disputes answer 'what did the parties agree the terms were'; actualization disputes answer 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed' (here); settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'; demurrage claims answer 'was delay or capacity usage compensable'; legal proceedings belong to Legal & Corporate Communications. The requested 'may-inform: Manage Settlement Disputes' relation has no controlled relationship_type; per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent the rule is recorded here verbatim — an actualization dispute may inform, coexist with, or generate a settlement dispute — and the reciprocal 'informed-by: Manage Actualization Disputes' is queued for CM-1-2-4-1-17 in the Settlements batch. This process coordinates evidence and controlled correction; it does not claim unilateral power to settle every dispute — some require independent surveyor findings, contractual claims handling, or legal escalation. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.3.1.8"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Actualization Disputes"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the actualization dispute lifecycle across all modes: discrepancy logging and classification; investigation against tickets, inspection reports, and statements; coordination of investigation, evidence exchange, and agreement or adjudication of corrected actuals with counterparties, carriers, inspectors, and terminal operators under the governing agreement and applicable measurement hierarchy — some disputes resolve through independent surveyor findings, contract-defined measurement hierarchies, or contractual claims handling; escalation of aging or material disputes; and closure through corrected actualized records. Under the batch 10 dispute partition, this process answers 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed'. An actualization dispute may inform, coexist with, or give rise to a settlement dispute; it is not universally a predecessor of one. Excludes confirmation disputes over agreed terms (Manage Confirm Disputes, CM-1-2-2-2-5); settlement disputes over amounts owed and paid (Manage Settlement Disputes, CM-1-2-4-1-17); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); financial recovery of carrier claims, which follows the relevant commercial agreements through settlement; legal proceedings, owned by Legal & Corporate Communications.\nIn scope: Log and classify quantity, quality, and measurement discrepancies | investigate against custody-transfer and inspection evidence | coordinate evidence exchange and agreement or adjudication of corrected actuals under the governing agreement and measurement hierarchy | escalate and close disputes through corrected records\nOut of scope: Confirmation disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | carrier claim recovery (commercial agreements via settlement) | legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualization discrepancies from the mode processes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty, carrier, inspector, and operator communications"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "custody-transfer documents and inspection reports"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Closed disputes with corrected actualized records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Resolve physical-delivery and measurement disagreements so the actualized record is correct and settlement proceeds on agreed actuals."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settlements and Accounting process covers deal attributes and deal terms at time of trade execution with counterparty through netting, invoicing and settlement post trade expiry and various accounting postings throughout deal lifecycle."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settlements & Accounting"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 3
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial Settlement"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trade Settlement"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Determine and complete the financial settlement of trading transactions — calculating amounts owed and due from actualized quantities, contractual prices, and verified charge determinants; generating and validating invoices; netting where agreements provide; preparing, validating, and submitting authorized settlement instructions or payment requests to the designated Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, Treasury, banking, or shared-services process; tracking payment or receipt status; and retaining settlement evidence — so every transaction closes financially on verified actuals and contractual terms."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process per the batch 08/13 variant test — one settlement lifecycle across commodity, channel, and logistics-service variants. Q2 decision (the decision owner): commodity-value settlement (value of the commodity or instrument, with trade counterparties) and logistics-service settlement (freight, tariff, charter hire, throughput, storage, handling, with carriers and operators) are distinct; one physical delivery may legitimately produce both; bundled delivered-price contracts are allocated per the scope-note rule. Q3 decision (the decision owner): Settlements determines contractual amounts, validates invoices and statements, manages netting, and prepares/submits authorized settlement instructions; Accounting owns booking, cash application, accruals, and GL; Treasury/banking executes payment release and bank movement; Finance owns write-offs and accounting policy (locked rule); the operating model locates these authorities (organizational neutrality, batch 10). Dispute partition (batch 10): settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. APQC 19948 is an unrelated candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settlements"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the per-transaction settlement lifecycle across commodity, market-channel, and logistics-service variants: assembling actualized quantities and charge determinants, applying contractual and market prices and rates, calculating settlement amounts, generating outbound and validating inbound invoices and statements, netting under the governing agreements, preparing and submitting authorized settlement instructions and payment requests to the designated finance processes, tracking payment and receipt status, and retaining settlement evidence. Under the batch 10 partition, settlement answers 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. Consumes actualized records and charge determinants from Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1) per the batch 13 charge-determinant rule. Where a contract bundles commodity value and logistics charges into one commercial settlement, the settlement process retains the contractual charge components and allocates them to the appropriate commodity and service settlement records where the accounting, tax, operational, or control model requires that distinction. Settlement does not independently approve payment release, initiate an unauthorized bank transfer, post accounting entries, apply cash, determine accounting treatment, or perform treasury cash management unless those authorities are expressly delegated in the enterprise operating model. Excludes actualization of quantities and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1); AR/AP booking, cash application, accruals, sub-ledger, general ledger, and tax, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); payment release and bank execution (Treasury and banking processes per the enterprise operating model); financial write-offs and valuation (Finance, locked rule); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1). When the accounting entry records the corresponding validated settlement or invoice, Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1) supplies authorized settlement calculations, validated invoices, and adjustment references that Manage AR/AP & Actuals (CM-1-2-4-2-4) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Calculate settlement amounts from actualized quantities, prices, and charge determinants | generate and validate invoices and settlement statements | net obligations under governing agreements | prepare, validate, and submit authorized settlement instructions and payment requests to the designated finance processes | track payment and receipt status and retain settlement evidence\nOut of scope: Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | AR/AP booking, cash application, accruals, and GL (CM-1-2-4-2) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking processes) | write-offs and valuation (Finance, locked rule) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/assuredBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-7-1-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized deal, movement, and entitlement records (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contractual prices, rates, and netting terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty, carrier, exchange, broker, ISO, and registry invoices and statements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled transactions with validated invoices, netting, and authorized settlement instructions evidenced"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Close every trading transaction financially on verified actuals and contractual terms, with a controlled and evidenced trail from actual to authorized settlement instruction."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12901"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle physical crude oil and condensate purchases and sales — applying contractual pricing, including provisional pricing, deferred pricing, or subsequent price determination where the contract provides, and contractually defined quality adjustments to actualized volumes, issuing and validating invoices, netting where agreements allow, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so crude transactions complete financially on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Provisional/final invoicing applies only where the contract provides for provisional pricing, deferred pricing, or subsequent price determination. Quality adjustments follow the contractually defined specifications (gravity, sulfur, water and sediment, contaminants, volume-temperature basis, and other contract terms) — the contract governs, not a fixed list. Q2 commodity-value reading applied. APQC 12901 is an unrelated forecasting candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Crude Oil"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers crude and condensate commodity-value settlement: assembling actualized volumes and qualities, applying contract price formulas, differentials, escalators, and contractually defined quality adjustments, managing provisional-to-final invoice cycles where the contract provides for provisional pricing, deferred pricing, or subsequent price determination, validating inbound invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. Lease/first-purchase crude settles under Settle First Purchaser/Leases (CM-1-2-4-1-6). Transportation charges for the movements settle under the mode processes (CM-1-2-4-1-4/-5/-12/-13); bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); exchange-cleared transactions (CM-1-2-4-1-3); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Apply contractual pricing and quality adjustments to actualized volumes | manage provisional and final invoice cycles where the contract provides | validate counterparty invoices and net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: First-purchase/lease settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | transportation-charge settlement (mode processes) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | exchange-cleared settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized crude movements (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract pricing terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty invoices"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "price indices and quotations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled crude transactions with validated invoices and authorized payment instructions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete crude purchases and sales financially on actualized volumes and contractual pricing."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-10",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-naesb-wgq-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle natural gas purchases, sales, transportation, and storage — applying index and contract pricing to scheduled and flowing-gas quantities, validating pipeline and counterparty invoices per the applicable business-practice standards, settling imbalances and cash-outs per tariff and contract, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so gas transactions complete financially on verified quantities."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q5: pipeline allocation, flow, imbalance, and invoice statements provide the applicable quantity and charge basis under the relevant tariff, contract, and allocation methodology — pipeline tariffs and bilateral agreements govern the specific transaction and take precedence; NAESB WGQ standards are US industry-practice context, jurisdiction-specific elsewhere. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Natural Gas"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers natural gas settlement: applying first-of-month and daily index and contract pricing to confirmed scheduled and allocated flowing-gas quantities, validating transporter and counterparty invoices following the applicable wholesale gas business-practice standards (NAESB WGQ invoicing standards in US interstate practice), settling transportation, fuel, and imbalance charges and cash-outs per tariff and contract, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. Pipeline allocation, flow, imbalance, and invoice statements provide the applicable quantity and charge basis under the relevant tariff, contract, and allocation methodology (no separate actualization sibling, per the batch 14 Q5 note); the specific pipeline's tariff and the bilateral contracts govern the transaction. Gas scheduling and nominations are owned by the gas scheduling processes. Excludes gas trading and capture (CM-1-2-1-3); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Apply index and contract pricing to allocated gas quantities | validate transporter and counterparty invoices per applicable standards | settle imbalances and cash-outs per tariff and contract | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Gas nominations and scheduling (gas scheduling processes) | gas deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Confirmed nominations and allocated flowing-gas statements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "index prices and contract terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "transporter and counterparty invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled gas transactions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete gas transactions financially on allocated quantities, applicable indices, and tariff terms."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-11",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/17058"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-isda-ma-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle bilateral financial over-the-counter derivatives — determining settlement amounts from contractual fixings and floating-price observations per the confirmed terms, exchanging and validating settlement calculations with counterparties, netting payments under the governing master agreements, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so financial trades complete per their documented terms."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Payment netting (routine settlement of offsetting amounts, applied only where legally enforceable and operationally configured) is distinguished from close-out netting (default remedy) per ISDA master agreement structure. APQC 17058 is an unrelated finance candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.11"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Financial OTC Trades"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers financial OTC settlement: observing fixings and floating prices per the confirmed trade terms, calculating and validating settlement amounts, agreeing amounts with counterparties, applying payment netting under the governing master agreements (ISDA or equivalent), preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes, and evidencing completion. Cleared OTC positions settle through the clearing channel (CM-1-2-4-1-3, CM-1-2-4-1-16). Collateral and credit-support administration is owned by Manage Collateral (CM-1-2-2-1-3); close-out netting on default is executed under the master agreement with Legal and Credit Risk. Excludes trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-7); confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17). Settle Financial OTC Trades (CM-1-2-4-1-11) follows initial Capture Financial (OTC) Trades (CM-1-2-1-3-7), using the captured transaction terms and applicable settlement-period inputs to calculate and validate payment obligations, subject to the required confirmation, reconciliation, and authorization controls.\nIn scope: Determine settlement amounts from fixings per confirmed terms | agree and validate amounts with counterparties | apply payment netting under master agreements | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Cleared settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3, CM-1-2-4-1-16) | collateral administration (CM-1-2-2-1-3) | close-out netting on default (master agreement, Legal/Credit Risk) | capture and confirmation (CM-1-2-1-3-7, CM-1-2-2-2) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Confirmed financial trades (CM-1-2-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "master agreement netting terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "price fixings and observations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled financial OTC payments, netted per agreements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete financial OTC trades exactly per documented terms and netting provisions."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-12",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-doe-rppl-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle pipeline transportation charges with carriers — validating pipeline invoices for tariff transportation, fuel, loss allowance, and related charges against actualized pipeline movements and verified tariff determinants, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so pipeline services complete financially on verified movement actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied; tariff mechanisms (PLA, quality bank, proration) are recorded at actualization and validated here per the batch 13 charge-determinant rule; the governing tariff takes precedence for the specific transaction. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.12"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Pipelines"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers logistics-service settlement for pipeline movements: validating carrier invoices for transportation, fuel and power, loss allowance, quality bank, and related tariff charges against actualized pipeline movements and the tariff and charge determinants recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-1), resolving rate and volume variances with carriers, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. The governing tariff and transportation agreements determine the applicable charges. The commodity value of the moved product settles under the commodity processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes pipeline actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-1); capacity contracting and nominations (transportation processes); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate pipeline tariff invoices against actualized movements and determinants | resolve rate and volume variances with carriers | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Commodity-value settlement (commodity processes) | pipeline actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-1) | capacity contracting and nominations (transportation processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized pipeline movements and tariff determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "carrier invoices"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "tariff rate references"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled pipeline transportation charges"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay pipeline carriers correctly for verified transportation services under tariff terms."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-13",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12931"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle rail transportation charges with railroads and rail service providers — validating freight and accessorial invoices against actualized rail movements and verified charge determinants, applying contracted and tariff rates, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so rail services complete financially on verified movement actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied. Rail car-hire/detention charges settle here when the rail agreement governs them; marine and terminal demurrage remains with CM-1-2-4-4 per the batch 10 partition. APQC 12931 'Schedule rail' is a scheduling candidate, not settlement."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.13"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Rail"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers logistics-service settlement for rail movements: validating railroad freight invoices and accessorial charges (car-hire and detention charges included where the rail agreement governs them) against actualized rail movements and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-3), applying contracted and tariff rates, resolving variances, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Railcar lease and fleet costs follow their governing agreements through the owning fleet processes. The commodity value settles under the commodity processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes rail actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-3); rail contracting and fleet management (logistics processes); marine/terminal demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate rail freight and accessorial invoices against actualized movements | apply contracted and tariff rates | resolve variances with rail providers | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Commodity-value settlement (commodity processes) | rail actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-3) | rail contracting and fleet management (logistics processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized rail movements and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contracted and tariff rates"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "railroad invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled rail transportation charges"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay rail providers correctly for verified rail services."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-14",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19558"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle terminal and storage service charges — validating operator invoices for throughput, storage, blending, handling, and ancillary services against actualized terminal transactions and verified charge determinants and contractual bases, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so terminal and storage services complete financially on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied. Minimum-throughput and capacity-reservation charges settle per the agreement even when unused; determinants come from actualization per batch 13. APQC 19558 is a storage-constraints candidate, not settlement."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.14"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Terminal/Storage"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers logistics-service settlement for terminal and storage arrangements: validating terminal and storage operator invoices against actualized transaction-level actuals and the charge determinants and contractual bases recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-5), applying agreement rates including minimum-throughput and capacity-reservation provisions, resolving variances, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Storage-deal commodity economics settle under the commodity and channel processes; the deal terms come from Capture Storage Deals (CM-1-2-1-3-5); bundled arrangements follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes terminal actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-5); terminal contracting (commercial/logistics processes); network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate terminal and storage invoices against actualized transactions and determinants | apply agreement rates including minimum-throughput and capacity provisions | resolve variances with operators | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Storage-deal commodity economics (commodity/channel processes) | terminal actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-5) | terminal contracting (commercial/logistics processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized terminal transactions and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-5)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "operator invoices"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "terminal and storage agreement rates"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled terminal and storage charges"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay terminal and storage operators correctly for verified services under agreement terms."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-15",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ferc-rto-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle bilateral power transactions outside ISO markets — applying contract and index pricing to scheduled and delivered quantities, validating counterparty invoices and settlement data, netting where agreements provide, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so bilateral power transactions complete financially on verified quantities."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Boundary to -7: bilateral contract settlement here; ISO statement settlement there; one transaction may touch both. Delivered-quantity sources are contractually agreed and market-design-specific (meter data, scheduling coordinator records, bilateral schedules, ISO/RTO data, or other agreed sources). APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.15"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Power"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers bilateral (non-ISO-cleared) power settlement: applying contract and index pricing to confirmed scheduled and delivered quantities, validating counterparty invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. Delivered quantities may be derived from meter data, scheduling coordinator records, bilateral schedules, ISO/RTO data, or other contractually agreed sources (batch 14 Q5 note). ISO/RTO-cleared activity settles under Settle ISO/Power Pools (CM-1-2-4-1-7); a bilateral transaction scheduled through an ISO may have both a bilateral settlement here and ISO charges there. Excludes power trading and capture (CM-1-2-1-3); transmission deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Apply contract and index pricing to scheduled and delivered power quantities | validate counterparty invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: ISO-cleared settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-7) | power deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | transmission capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Confirmed schedules and metering data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract and index prices"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled bilateral power transactions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete bilateral power transactions financially on verified scheduled and delivered quantities."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-16",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle broker and clearing-member account activity — reconciling broker statements against internal trade, position, and margin records, validating commissions, fees, and charges, preparing, submitting, tracking, and reconciling authorized broker-facing payment or collateral-transfer instructions, including margin transfers, through the designated Treasury, banking, or payment-operations process, and resolving statement discrepancies — so broker accounts remain accurate, funded, and evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (the decision owner) applied: broker-facing account relationship here; trade-level cleared settlement at -3; margin requirement control at CM-1-2-2-1-5. The batch 11 binding is preserved as the operational broker-account side of margin cash movements — this process prepares, submits, tracks, and reconciles the authorized instructions; the designated Treasury, banking, or payment-operations process executes the bank movement. Whether this process submits payment instructions or only requests them is operating-model-specific (open question carried in the batch 14 PR). APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.16"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Broker"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the broker-facing account relationship for executing and clearing brokers: daily reconciliation of broker statements against internal trades, positions, and cash and margin balances; validation of commissions, exchange and clearing fees, and other charges against agreed schedules; preparation, submission, tracking, and reconciliation of authorized broker-facing payment and collateral-transfer instructions, including margin transfers, through the designated Treasury, banking, or payment-operations process, per the batch 11 binding and the batch 14 Q3 payment-authority rule; and resolution of statement discrepancies with brokers. Margin requirement validation and calls are owned by Perform Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5); trade-level exchange settlement values by Settle Exchange Trades (CM-1-2-4-1-3). Excludes broker relationship management and selection (commercial processes); margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5); bank payment release and execution (Treasury/banking processes); settlement disputes escalated beyond broker statement resolution (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Reconcile broker statements against internal trade, position, and margin records | validate commissions, fees, and charges | prepare, submit, track, and reconcile authorized broker-facing payment and margin-transfer instructions through the designated process | resolve statement discrepancies\nOut of scope: Margin requirement validation and calls (CM-1-2-2-1-5) | trade-level exchange settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3) | bank payment release and execution (Treasury/banking processes) | broker selection and relationship management (commercial processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Broker and clearing statements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "commission and fee schedules"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "internal trade, position, and margin records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "margin calls validated by CM-1-2-2-1-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Reconciled broker accounts with authorized broker-payment and margin-transfer instructions, status, and evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep broker accounts reconciled, correctly charged, and funded through authorized, tracked instructions with an evidenced trail."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-17",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage disputes over what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied — logging and classifying settlement discrepancies, investigating against actualized records, contractual terms, and settlement calculations, coordinating resolution with counterparties, carriers, brokers, and operators under the governing agreements, escalating aging or material disputes, and closing them through corrected settlements and evidenced adjustments."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Dispute partition rule (the decision owner, batch 10) applied verbatim: confirmation disputes answer 'what did the parties agree the terms were'; actualization disputes answer 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed'; settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied' (here); demurrage claims answer 'was delay or capacity usage compensable'; legal proceedings belong to Legal & Corporate Communications. Invoice-calculation rule (the decision owner, batch 14): a dispute about the invoice calculation, not the underlying actual or agreed terms, remains a settlement dispute. The requested 'may-be-informed-by' relations have no controlled relationship_type; per the batch 10/13 conditional-relation precedent, informed-by is used with the conditional rule recorded verbatim: a settlement dispute may be caused by an unresolved actualization dispute or a confirmation mismatch, may be purely financial (rate, tax, invoice, netting, fee, timing, or duplicate-charge issues), and may continue after the underlying physical or confirmation question is settled — no strict lifecycle sequencing is implied. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.17"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Settlement Disputes"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the settlement dispute lifecycle across all settlement processes: discrepancy logging and classification; investigation against actualized quantities and determinants, contractual prices and rates, and settlement calculations; coordination of resolution with the relevant external parties under the governing agreements; escalation of aging or material disputes; and closure through corrected settlements, credit notes, or evidenced adjustments. Under the batch 10 dispute partition, this process answers 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. Where a dispute concerns an invoice calculation but not the underlying actual or agreed terms, it remains a settlement dispute; a dispute whose root cause is a physical or measurement disagreement is redirected to Manage Actualization Disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8). Excludes confirmation disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); ISO statement disputes within ISO processes (CM-1-2-4-1-7); financial write-offs (Finance, locked rule); legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications).\nIn scope: Log and classify settlement discrepancies | investigate against actuals, terms, and calculations | coordinate resolution under governing agreements | escalate and close through corrected settlements and evidenced adjustments\nOut of scope: Confirmation disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | ISO statement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-7) | write-offs (Finance, locked rule) | legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settlement discrepancies from the settlement processes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "actualized records and determinants"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contractual terms and calculations"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty communications"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Closed settlement disputes with corrected, evidenced settlements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Resolve financial-settlement disagreements so transactions close on correct, evidenced amounts."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-18",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle purchases and sales of refinery intermediates and blend components — applying contractual and index-based pricing to actualized volumes and qualities, generating and validating invoices, netting where agreements provide, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so intermediate transactions complete financially on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Product-versus-intermediate partition rule (the decision owner, batch 14): follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not simply the physical location or refinery use; matters for blendstocks, refinery intermediates, bio-components, and imported feedstocks. Consistent with the batch 04 finished-products-plus-intermediates material scope. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.18"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Intermediates"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers commodity-value settlement for intermediates and blend components (for example vacuum gasoil, naphtha, reformate, alkylate, blendstocks): applying contract and index pricing with quality adjustments to actualized volumes, generating and validating invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. The product-versus-intermediate boundary follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not the physical location or whether a stream is used inside a refinery; finished refined products settle under Settle Products (CM-1-2-4-1-2). Transportation and terminal charges settle under the mode processes. Excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Apply contractual and index pricing with quality adjustments to actualized intermediate volumes | generate and validate invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Finished-product settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-2) | transportation and terminal charges (mode processes) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized intermediate movements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract and index pricing"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled intermediate transactions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete intermediate and blend-component transactions financially on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-19",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle purchases and sales of other commodities traded by the organization — such as NGLs, petrochemicals, sulfur, petroleum coke, and other by-products or ancillary commodities — applying contractual pricing to actualized quantities, generating and validating invoices, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions on the same settlement discipline as the named commodity processes."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Controlled residual category (the decision owner, batch 14): membership requires that no named sibling owns the commodity's commercial settlement and that its mechanics need no distinct regulated, market-channel, or logistics-service process; growth routes through taxonomy governance, not scope creep. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.19"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Other Commodities"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "This is a controlled residual category, not a default dumping ground: a commodity belongs here only if no named sibling owns its commercial settlement and its settlement mechanics do not require a distinct regulated, market-channel, or logistics-service process. Covers commodity-value settlement for such commodities: applying contract and index pricing to actualized quantities, generating and validating invoices, netting where agreements provide, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. If trading volume or control needs justify it, a dedicated row should be proposed through taxonomy governance rather than broadening this row. Transportation and terminal charges settle under the mode processes. Excludes crude (CM-1-2-4-1-1), products (CM-1-2-4-1-2), intermediates (CM-1-2-4-1-18), gas (CM-1-2-4-1-10), power (CM-1-2-4-1-7/-15), environmental instruments (CM-1-2-4-1-8); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Apply contractual pricing to actualized quantities of unlisted commodities | generate and validate invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Commodities with named settlement rows (CM-1-2-4-1-1/-2/-7/-8/-10/-15/-18) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized movements of unlisted commodities"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract pricing"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled other-commodity transactions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Give every traded commodity a settlement owner without diluting the named processes."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12914"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle physical refined product purchases and sales — applying contractual and index-based pricing to actualized volumes on the contractual measurement basis, generating and validating invoices, netting where agreements provide, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so product transactions complete financially on verified actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 commodity-value reading applied. Product-versus-intermediate partition rule (the decision owner, batch 14): follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not simply the physical location or refinery use; matters for blendstocks, refinery intermediates, bio-components, and imported feedstocks. APQC 12914 'Sell by-products' is a WEAK sales candidate, not settlement."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Products"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers refined-product commodity-value settlement: assembling actualized volumes and qualities, applying contract and index pricing with differentials and adjustments, generating outbound and validating inbound invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. The product-versus-intermediate boundary follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not the physical location or whether a stream is used inside a refinery; intermediates settle under Settle Intermediates (CM-1-2-4-1-18). Product exchange agreements settle per their agreement terms with imbalance treatment administered under Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1). Transportation and terminal charges settle under the mode processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Apply contractual and index pricing to actualized product volumes | generate and validate invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Intermediates settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-18) | exchange-agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | transportation and terminal charges (mode processes) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized product movements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract and index pricing terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled product transactions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete refined product purchases and sales financially on actualized volumes and contractual pricing."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19582"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle exchange-traded and cleared transactions — validating and allocating daily settlement variation and final settlement amounts to trades and positions against exchange and clearinghouse values, settling expiries, exercises, and deliveries per contract specifications, and reconciling trade-level settlement outcomes — so cleared positions complete financially per exchange rules."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (the decision owner): trade/position-level settlement under exchange and clearing rules here; the broker/clearing-member account relationship at CM-1-2-4-1-16; margin requirement control at CM-1-2-2-1-5. Daily settlement variation is often handled operationally through the broker/clearing account rather than as a standalone trade-level payment — the split is validate-and-allocate here, reconcile-and-coordinate-cash there. Settlement values are determined by the exchange/clearinghouse; this process validates and applies them, consistent with the batch 11 validate-not-recalculate pattern. APQC 19582 is an FX-execution candidate, not settlement."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Exchange Trades"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers trade-level settlement of exchange-traded and cleared instruments: validating daily settlement prices and variation amounts against clearinghouse determinations and allocating them to trades and positions, settling contract expiries, option exercises and assignments, and physically delivered exchange contracts per contract specifications, and reconciling settlement outcomes at the trade and position level. Daily settlement variation may operationally flow through the broker or clearing account: this process validates and allocates the exchange and clearing settlement values; Settle Broker (CM-1-2-4-1-16) reconciles the broker account and coordinates the associated cash movement. Margin requirement validation and calls are owned by Perform Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5). Excludes exchange trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-4); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate and allocate daily settlement variation against clearinghouse values | settle expiries, exercises, assignments, and deliveries per contract specifications | reconcile trade-level settlement outcomes\nOut of scope: Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5) | broker account reconciliation and associated cash movement (CM-1-2-4-1-16) | exchange trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Captured exchange trades (CM-1-2-1-3-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract specifications"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "exchange and clearinghouse settlement values"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-16"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled exchange transactions reconciled to clearinghouse values"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure cleared transactions settle exactly per exchange and clearinghouse determinations, with discrepancies surfaced."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle truck transportation charges with carriers — validating freight invoices against actualized movements and verified freight-charge determinants, applying contracted rates and accessorials, resolving invoice variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so trucking services complete financially on verified movement actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied: this row settles the trucking service charge; the product value settles under the commodity rows; one delivery may produce both settlement streams. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Trucks"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers logistics-service settlement for truck movements: validating carrier freight invoices against actualized truck movements and the freight-charge determinants recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-2), applying contracted rates, fuel surcharges, and accessorial charges, resolving rate and quantity variances with carriers, netting where agreements provide, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. The commodity value of the moved product settles under the commodity processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes truck actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-2); carrier contracting (the owning logistics and procurement processes); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate carrier freight invoices against actualized movements and determinants | apply contracted rates, surcharges, and accessorials | resolve carrier invoice variances | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Commodity-value settlement (commodity processes) | truck actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-2) | carrier contracting (logistics/procurement processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized truck movements and freight determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "carrier invoices"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contracted rates"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled truck freight charges"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay carriers correctly for verified trucking services."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12927"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle marine transportation charges — validating freight, charter hire, and voyage-related invoices against actualized voyages and verified freight, laytime, and voyage-cost determinants, applying charter party and freight-agreement terms, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so marine services complete financially on verified voyage actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied. Agreed freight and voyage-service charges settle here; delay and capacity compensation (demurrage, detention, despatch) stays wholly with CM-1-2-4-4 — not all maritime claims are demurrage, and the governing agreement decides the claim type. APQC 12927 'Qualify vessels' is a vetting candidate, not settlement."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Vessels & Barge"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers logistics-service settlement for marine movements: validating owner and operator invoices for freight, charter hire, deadfreight, and other agreed voyage-service charges against actualized voyages and the determinants recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4), applying charter party and contract-of-affreightment terms, resolving variances, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Freight and agreed voyage-service charges are settled here; demurrage, detention, despatch, and delay compensation follow the governing charter party, terminal agreement, or transport contract and are handled by Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4) where that process is in scope, per the batch 10 partition. The cargo value settles under the commodity processes. Excludes marine actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4); chartering and voyage operations (marine logistics processes); demurrage, detention, despatch, and delay compensation (CM-1-2-4-4); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate freight, charter hire, and voyage-charge invoices against actualized voyages | apply charter party and freight-agreement terms | resolve variances with owners and operators | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Cargo-value settlement (commodity processes) | marine actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | demurrage, detention, despatch, and delay compensation (CM-1-2-4-4) | chartering (marine logistics processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized voyages and voyage-cost determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "charter party and freight terms"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "owner and operator invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled marine transportation charges"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay marine service providers correctly for verified voyage services."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle crude and condensate first-purchase transactions — calculating amounts due from actualized lease runs under contractual pricing, gravity and quality adjustments, and deductions, producing settlement statements consistent with the division-of-interest references, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so lease purchases complete financially on verified run actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Conditional downstream scope: 'first purchaser' per the batch 09 crude-marketing reading; the standing batch 09 follow-up on whether lease-purchase activity is genuinely downstream-owned or a cross-segment/integrated-company exception applies here and is carried in the batch 14 PR. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle First Purchaser/Leases"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Included only where the downstream Supply & Trading organization purchases crude or condensate at lease/wellhead or administers first-purchaser arrangements, per the batch 09 scope caveat. Covers first-purchaser settlement: assembling actualized lease runs (Perform Lease Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-6), applying contract pricing, gravity and quality adjustments, and deductions maintained under Manage Lease Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-8), producing lease settlement statements consistent with division-of-interest references, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Royalty and interest payment execution to owners is owned by Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments (CM-1-2-4-2-3). This process does not absorb upstream land and lease administration, production allocation, royalty policy, exploration-and-production accounting, owner-payment entitlement calculation policy, or statutory first-purchaser compliance obligations not delegated to downstream. Excludes lease actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6); lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Calculate first-purchase amounts from actualized runs and contract terms | produce lease settlement statements consistent with division-of-interest references | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status | synchronize with royalty-payment execution\nOut of scope: Royalty and interest payment execution (CM-1-2-4-2-3) | lease actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6) | lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | upstream land/lease administration, production allocation, royalty policy, E&P accounting, owner-payment entitlement policy, and non-delegated statutory first-purchaser compliance (outside scope) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-1-2-8"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized lease runs (CM-1-2-3-1-6)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "division-of-interest references"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "lease contract pricing and deduction terms (CM-1-2-1-2-8)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled first-purchase transactions with statements for payment execution"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete lease purchases financially on verified runs and contractual terms, feeding correct interest-level payment execution."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-7",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19948"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ferc-rto-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle transactions with independent system operators, regional transmission organizations, and power pools — validating ISO settlement statements across the day-ahead and real-time settlement cycles and their subsequent true-up versions, performing independent shadow-settlement checks, disputing discrepancies within ISO timelines, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so ISO-cleared activity completes financially per market rules."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q5: no actualization sibling — the applicable ISO/RTO settlement statement is the governing market settlement record under that market's tariff, subject to validation and dispute rights; shadow settlement is the internal verification and control step. ISO/RTO applicability is jurisdiction-specific (US market design per FERC; other jurisdictions differ), and each ISO's tariff and business-practice manuals govern its settlement calendar, billing determinants, and dispute procedures. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.7"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle ISO/Power Pools"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers ISO/RTO and power-pool settlement: receiving and validating settlement statements for energy, capacity, ancillary services, transmission, and associated charge types under the market's two-settlement (day-ahead/real-time) design and multi-version true-up calendar; performing shadow settlement against internal scheduling and metering data; raising statement disputes within the ISO's dispute windows; and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. The applicable ISO/RTO settlement statement is the governing market settlement record under that market's tariff, subject to the organization's validation and dispute rights; the ISO tariff is authoritative for charge types, timing, dispute windows, and true-up treatment. Bilateral (non-ISO) power settles under Settle Power (CM-1-2-4-1-15). Excludes market participation strategy and bidding (trading processes); transmission deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11); settlement disputes outside ISO statement processes (CM-1-2-4-1-17). Where ISO/power-pool settlement includes items arising from a captured transmission arrangement, initial capture of the relevant arrangement under Capture Transmission Deals (CM-1-2-1-3-11) precedes validation of those items under Settle ISO/Power Pools (CM-1-2-4-1-7). Other settlement activities may proceed independently.\nIn scope: Validate ISO settlement statements across versions and charge types | perform shadow settlement against internal data | raise statement disputes within ISO timelines | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status\nOut of scope: Bilateral power settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-15) | bidding and market participation strategy (trading processes) | transmission deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11) | non-ISO settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "ISO settlement statements and true-ups"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "internal scheduling and metering data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market tariff and charge-type references"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated and settled ISO statements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure ISO-cleared activity settles exactly per validated market statements, with discrepancies disputed in time."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-8",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Row name reads 'Emissions' but the settled scope spans the environmental-instrument families handled by capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6) and actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-7): RINs, allowances, RECs, and similar program instruments; 'Settle Environmental Instruments' queued for the naming pass. Payment-versus-transfer linkage is contract-specific. APQC 19511 is a vehicle-testing candidate, unrelated. Step 3d naming pass — scope-ambiguity Batch 1 (A+B+C, 2026-09-21): renamed from 'Settle Emissions' to 'Settle Environmental Instruments'. Prior label(s) retained as altLabel(s): 'Settle Emissions'."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19511"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-rec-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-rfs-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Emissions"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle environmental and renewable instrument transactions — RINs, allowances, RECs, and similar program instruments — calculating amounts due from actualized registry transfers and contractual pricing, generating and validating invoices, and submitting authorized payment instructions against confirmed instrument delivery — so instrument trades complete financially on verified registry actuals."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.8"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settle Environmental Instruments"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers financial settlement of environmental-instrument transactions: assembling actualized registry lifecycle events (Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7), applying contract pricing, generating and validating invoices, preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes against confirmed registry transfer or retirement where the contract links payment to delivery, and evidencing completion. Program applicability is jurisdiction-specific. Excludes instrument actualization and registry-event processing (CM-1-2-3-1-7); deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6); compliance filings and RVO demonstrations (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); allowance-position economics (CM-1-1-7-3-3); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Calculate amounts due from actualized registry events and contract pricing | generate and validate instrument invoices | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status | evidence completion against confirmed transfers\nOut of scope: Registry-event processing and reconciliation (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6) | compliance filings (EHS & Government Reporting) | allowance-position economics (CM-1-1-7-3-3) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized registry events (CM-1-2-3-1-7)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contract pricing"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled environmental-instrument transactions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete environmental-instrument trades financially against verified registry delivery."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1-9",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19601"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage the controlled working and evidentiary document set used by trading settlement — generating, receiving, validating, distributing, and retaining settlement statements, payment instructions, supporting actualization evidence, and applicable invoices — so every settlement is executed and evidenced from controlled, complete documents."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Document-control process serving the settlement family, parallel to Manage Confirmation Documentation (batch 10); scope is the trading-settlement working and evidentiary set, not the enterprise document system of record. Record-retention requirements are jurisdiction- and instrument-specific. APQC 19601 is an HSSE-records candidate, not trading settlement."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.1.9"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Settlement Documents"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the settlement document working set across all settlement processes: outbound invoice and statement generation and distribution; inbound document receipt, registration, and completeness checking; association of supporting evidence (tickets, inspection reports, statements) with the settlement record; netting statement documentation; and retention and retrieval of the settlement working set per the applicable requirements. This process controls settlement documents for trading settlement purposes; it is not the legal system of record for every enterprise invoice and does not own enterprise records-retention policy. Excludes confirmation documentation (Manage Confirmation Documentation, CM-1-2-2-2-2); contract and legal documents (Contract Management, CM-1-2-1-2; Legal & Corporate Communications); accounting records and financial-reporting documentation and workpapers (CM-1-2-4-2-7); enterprise records-retention policy (the accountable governance owner); the settlement calculations themselves (the owning settlement processes).\nIn scope: Generate and distribute outbound invoices and statements | receive, register, and completeness-check inbound documents | associate supporting evidence with settlement records | retain and retrieve the settlement working set per applicable requirements\nOut of scope: Confirmation documents (CM-1-2-2-2-2) | contract and legal documents (CM-1-2-1-2, Legal & Corporate Communications) | accounting and financial-reporting records and workpapers (CM-1-2-4-2-7) | enterprise records-retention policy (governance owner) | settlement calculation (owning settlement processes)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settlement calculations and statements from the settlement processes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "inbound counterparty and carrier documents"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "retention requirements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Controlled, retained settlement documentation linked to settlement records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep settlement execution running on complete, controlled, retrievable documents."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10774"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-fasb-asc815-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Product Control"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trading Accounting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The capability to record, value, reconcile, and report the financial effects of supply and trading activity by maintaining controlled sub-ledger and general-ledger records; booking authorized settlement outcomes, receivables, payables, actuals, accruals, taxes, and interest obligations; reconciling economic P&L to accounting P&L; and producing controlled financial evidence and reporting within the enterprise accounting-policy framework."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12 test — heterogeneous concurrent record-and-control functions, not one per-transaction lifecycle. Organizational neutrality (the decision owner, batch 15): responsible domain recorded as Finance-enabled Supply & Trading — a trading-accounting capability serving Supply & Trading, delivered through Product Control, Trading Accounting, Finance Operations, Accounting, Shared Services, or another designated function per the operating model; taxonomy branch placement under Commercial & Marketing does not prescribe the accountable owner. Execute-within-policy pattern: accounting executes within the Finance-owned policy framework, parallel to the batch 11 limits and batch 12 valuation-control precedents. 'P&L' in prose per the batch 12 convention. APQC 10774 is a WEAK financial-accounting candidate, comparative evidence only."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Accounting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the trading and supply accounting capability: transaction-level treatment assessment, sub-ledger and GL recording, AR/AP and actuals booking from authorized settlement outcomes (batch 14 Q3 chain), cash application, accruals, financial production and inventory accounting, royalty and interest disbursement execution, economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation using the controlled economic inputs delivered by Market Risk at end of month (batch 12), transaction and commodity tax execution, financial documentation and reporting, financial system and ledger-structure administration, and trading-specific accounting procedures. This capability supports Supply & Trading and may be operated by Product Control, Trading Accounting, Finance Operations, Accounting, Shared Services, or another designated accounting function; the taxonomy placement does not prescribe the accountable legal, finance, or organizational owner. Operates within the enterprise accounting policy framework: financial write-off approval, valuation policy, material technical-accounting conclusions, and enterprise accounting policy are owned by Finance under the locked Finance rule; payment release and bank execution by Treasury and banking processes; settlement calculation and invoice validation by Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1); the refinery operational material balance by Perform Production Accounting (CM-1-1-7-1-3); network book-to-physical stock reconciliation by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5).\nIn scope: Book, value, and reconcile trading activity in sub-ledger and GL | apply cash, accrue, and post actuals from authorized settlement outcomes | reconcile economic P&L to accounting P&L | assess treatments, execute transaction taxes, and produce controlled financial documentation\nOut of scope: Enterprise accounting policy, valuation policy, material technical-accounting conclusions, and write-off approval (Finance, locked rule) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking) | settlement calculation and invoicing (CM-1-2-4-1) | refinery material balance (CM-1-1-7-1-3) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/capability"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/governedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-12"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Authorized settlement outcomes (CM-1-2-4-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "controlled economic P&L inputs at EOM (CM-1-2-2-3-5)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "enterprise accounting policy (Finance)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "operational production and inventory balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3, CM-1-1-3-7-5)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Complete, reconciled trading financial records, postings, and controlled financial reporting"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Give supply and trading activity a complete, controlled, and reconciled financial record within enterprise policy."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10731"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-fasb-asc815-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess, document, and apply the accounting treatment of trading transactions under approved enterprise policy — derivative versus normal purchase/normal sale scope, own-use, lease, storage, and exchange treatments, and gross-versus-net presentation — obtaining Finance/controller approval or escalation for material, new, complex, or policy-sensitive conclusions, so transactions are recorded under the correct, evidenced treatment."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Assess-document-apply with escalation (the decision owner, batch 15): treatment work is performed under approved policy with Finance/controller approval for material conclusions — delegated technical-accounting authority is operating-model-specific and not assumed. Batch 12 exclusion on CM-1-2-2-3-15 honored: valuation execution does not approve accounting classification; classification assessment lands here, policy and material conclusions with Finance. ASC 815 supports the accounting concepts, not the organization's approval design. Standards are jurisdiction-specific: ASC 815 under US GAAP; IFRS 9 and related standards elsewhere. APQC 10731 is a fixed-asset candidate, unrelated."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Accounting Treatments"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers transaction-level treatment assessment and documentation: assessing new deal types, structures, and amendments against the applicable accounting standards and enterprise policy (for US GAAP, ASC 815 derivative scope, the normal purchases and normal sales election, and hedge accounting requirements are the recurring assessments), documenting the treatment conclusion and its evidence, supporting hedge designation documentation, and maintaining the treatment reference used by posting and reconciliation processes. Material, new, complex, or policy-sensitive conclusions require Finance/controller approval or escalation; hedge designation approval follows the approved hedging policy through its accountable owners (Finance, Treasury, or Controller per the operating model), not this process. Treatment assessments apply enterprise accounting policy owned by Finance under the locked rule. Excludes enterprise accounting policy and material technical-accounting conclusions (Finance, locked rule); hedge designation approval (per approved hedging policy); valuation adjustment execution (Manage Commodity Valuations, CM-1-2-2-3-15, batch 12); trading-procedure documentation (CM-1-2-4-2-12); posting execution (the recording processes).\nIn scope: Assess deal types and structures against standards and approved policy | document treatment conclusions with evidence | escalate material, new, complex, or policy-sensitive conclusions to Finance | maintain the treatment reference and support hedge designation documentation\nOut of scope: Enterprise accounting policy and material technical-accounting conclusions (Finance, locked rule) | hedge designation approval (per approved hedging policy) | valuation adjustments (CM-1-2-2-3-15) | procedure documentation (CM-1-2-4-2-12) | posting execution (recording processes)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-12"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/governedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-12"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "New and amended deal types and structures (CM-1-2-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "accounting standards references"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "enterprise accounting policy (Finance)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Documented treatment assessments and the treatment reference, with material conclusions Finance-approved"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure every transaction is recorded under a correct, documented, policy-compliant treatment with material conclusions properly approved."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-10",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Calculate, post, and true up accruals for trading activity — un-invoiced delivered or received volumes, expected fees and charges, and estimated prices pending final determination — per the enterprise accrual methodology, so each period's books are complete for activity not yet settled."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Execute-within-policy: methodology is Finance-owned, execution here. Accrual bases (the decision owner, batch 15) span actualized-but-uninvoiced, policy-permitted estimated unactualized activity, expected service charges, and deferred pricing — not only delivered/received-not-invoiced. Accrual-to-actual analysis feeds estimation improvement. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Accruals"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the accrual cycle: identifying accruable activity from actualized movements and open settlement items, estimating accrual values per the enterprise methodology, posting accruals at cut-off, reversing or truing up against actual settlements, and analyzing accrual accuracy. Accruals may be based on actualized but uninvoiced activity, estimated unactualized activity where policy permits, expected service charges, or deferred pricing, depending on the approved close methodology (including provisional-price estimates pending final price determination per the batch 14 crude pricing pattern). Accrual methodology and materiality policy are owned by Finance under the locked rule; this process executes them. Excludes accrual methodology and policy (Finance, locked rule); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); AR/AP booking of settled items (CM-1-2-4-2-4). Manage Accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10) supplies accrual, reversal, and true-up postings with period references that Manage Trading Sub-Ledger (CM-1-2-4-2-5) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Identify accruable activity per the approved close methodology | estimate accrual values per enterprise methodology | post at cut-off and true up against settlements | analyze accrual accuracy\nOut of scope: Accrual methodology and policy (Finance, locked rule) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | booking of settled items (CM-1-2-4-2-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized movements not yet settled (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "enterprise accrual methodology (Finance)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "open settlement items (CM-1-2-4-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Posted, evidenced, trued-up accruals"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make each period's books complete for real activity that has not yet settled."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-11",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10736"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-irs-p510-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Execute transaction- and commodity-tax accounting for supply and trading activity by applying approved tax rules and tax-determination data to transactions, recording tax amounts, maintaining required transaction-level evidence, and preparing controlled tax-accounting and filing-support outputs for the accountable enterprise Tax function."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision (the decision owner): transaction- and commodity-tax accounting execution under approved rules — tax-determination data, licenses, exemption certificates, and classifications are consumed from their accountable owners (Tax, Legal, EHS, or master-data governance per the operating model), validated for presence and applicability, never owned here; separation-of-duties in downstream fuels taxation. Motor-fuels excise is jurisdiction-specific; IRS Publication 510 covers the US federal regime as context, not tax advice; state and non-US regimes differ. APQC 10736 'Manage taxes' scored 1.0 at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; that candidate is the corporate tax function, broader than this execution row."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.11"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Taxes"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers transaction-tax execution for trading activity: applying approved tax rules and tax-determination data to purchases, sales, blends, and movements (in the United States, federal motor-fuels excise taxes under the IRS Publication 510 regime, plus state motor-fuel, sales and use, and similar taxes; other jurisdictions per their regimes); recording and accruing tax amounts; maintaining required transaction-level evidence; and preparing controlled tax-accounting and filing-support outputs for the accountable enterprise Tax function. This process consumes approved tax rules, jurisdiction and product tax classifications, licenses, exemption certificates, and tax-determination master data from their accountable owners; it may validate their presence and applicability to an individual transaction but does not establish tax policy, determine legal tax positions, own tax registrations or licenses, or file tax returns unless expressly delegated. Excludes tax policy, strategy, legal tax positions, registrations and licenses, corporate income tax, and tax-return filing (enterprise Tax function/Finance and accountable owners); settlement of tax-inclusive invoices (CM-1-2-4-1); customs and trade compliance where separately owned. Manage Taxes (CM-1-2-4-2-11) supplies transaction-tax accounting amounts, accruals, and supporting posting references that Manage Trading Sub-Ledger (CM-1-2-4-2-5) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Apply approved tax rules and determination data to trading transactions | validate presence and applicability of licenses, exemptions, and classifications per transaction | record and accrue tax amounts with transaction-level evidence | prepare controlled tax-accounting and filing-support outputs for the Tax function\nOut of scope: Tax policy, strategy, legal positions, registrations, licenses, and return filing (enterprise Tax function/Finance and accountable owners) | corporate income tax (enterprise Tax function) | settlement of invoices (CM-1-2-4-1) | separately owned customs and trade compliance"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/assuredBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-12"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Captured and actualized transactions (CM-1-2-1-3, CM-1-2-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "approved tax rules, classifications, licenses, and exemption data (accountable owners)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "jurisdictional tax rates"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Recorded, accrued, evidenced transaction taxes with controlled filing-support outputs"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure every trading transaction carries correct, evidenced transaction-tax treatment under approved rules."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-12",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (the decision owner): despite the row name, this process develops procedures within — never policy above — the Finance-owned framework; the set-versus-apply pattern (batches 07/11) governs. The displayed label overstates the mandate: preferred normalized label 'Maintain Trading Accounting Procedures and Guidance' (alternative 'Manage Trading Accounting Procedures') recorded as alt labels and queued for the naming pass with priority. APQC 16592 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; it is a retail omni-channel candidate. Step 3d naming pass, authority-risk category (2026-09-21, the decision owner's approval): renamed 'Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures' -> 'Maintain Trading Accounting Procedures and Guidance' — the label overstated the mandate: the concept maintains trading accounting procedures and guidance, it does not develop accounting policy."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16592"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-fasb-asc815-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Trading Accounting Procedures"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop, maintain, version, and communicate trading-specific accounting procedures, close instructions, and implementation guidance that operationalize Finance-owned enterprise accounting policy for supply and trading activity."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.12"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Maintain Trading Accounting Procedures and Guidance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers trading accounting procedure management: developing and maintaining desk-level procedures, close instructions, and implementation guidance that operationalize enterprise accounting policy for trading transaction types; assessing new products, structures, and standards changes for procedural impact and coordinating with Manage Accounting Treatments (CM-1-2-4-2-1) on treatment consequences; escalating policy gaps and interpretation questions to Finance; and maintaining procedure currency and version control. Boundary rule: Finance/Controller establishes enterprise accounting policy and decides material new policy questions (supported by technical accounting where applicable); Manage Accounting Treatments determines transaction-level treatment under approved policy; this process maintains the procedures, close instructions, and operational guidance; the accounting processes execute them. This process implements policy and never sets it, on the set-versus-apply pattern established in batches 07 and 11. Excludes enterprise accounting policy and material policy interpretation (Finance, locked rule); transaction-level treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1); enterprise records-retention and governance policy (governance owners).\nIn scope: Develop, maintain, version, and communicate trading accounting procedures and close instructions implementing enterprise policy | assess new products and standards changes for procedural impact | escalate policy gaps and questions to Finance | maintain procedure currency and version control\nOut of scope: Enterprise accounting policy and material policy interpretation (Finance, locked rule) | transaction-level treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | enterprise governance and retention policy (governance owners)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Enterprise accounting policy (Finance)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "new products and structures (CM-1-2-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "standards changes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "treatment assessments (CM-1-2-4-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Current, version-controlled trading accounting procedures and guidance"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep trading accounting execution consistent, current, and provably within enterprise policy."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision: financial production and inventory accounting here; operational material balance and stock reconciliation with their batch 04/06 owners — the same operational source feeds each process, but each owns a different record and decision, extending the batch 13 record-partition principle into the accounting layer. Record-not-compute (the decision owner, batch 15): this process maintains the financial representation of verified balances; it does not compute inventory independently of operational systems. Preferred normalized label 'Manage Production & Inventory Accounting' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass (row names mirror the locked taxonomy). APQC 10224 is a scheduling candidate, unrelated. Step 3d naming pass — scope-ambiguity Batch 1 (A+B+C, 2026-09-21): renamed from 'Manage Production Accounting' to 'Manage Production & Inventory Accounting'. Prior label(s) retained as altLabel(s): 'Manage Production Accounting'."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10224"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm31-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Production Accounting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Maintain the financial accounting of production and inventory for supply and trading activity — recording and reconciling financial inventory balances and cost-of-sales effects under the enterprise-approved inventory valuation methodology, from verified operational balances — so the financial books carry a complete, correctly valued representation of physical activity."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Production & Inventory Accounting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the financial-accounting side of production and inventory: recording and valuing production receipts, inventory movements, transfers, and exchange imbalances into the sub-ledger and GL; recording and reconciling financial inventory balances and cost-of-sales effects under the enterprise-approved inventory valuation methodology; and reconciling the financial inventory records to the operational balances. Operational quantities arrive as controlled inputs: the refinery daily material balance is owned by Perform Production Accounting (CM-1-1-7-1-3, batch 06) and network book-to-physical reconciliation by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5, batch 04) — this process values what those processes have verified; it does not re-perform the operational balance. Financial reconciliation to operational balances may identify differences for investigation but does not authorize operational stock adjustments, production remeasurement, or physical loss determination. Inventory valuation methodology, write-off approval, and valuation entries policy are owned by Finance under the locked rule. Excludes the operational balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3, CM-1-1-3-7-5); operational stock adjustments and loss determination (operational owners); valuation policy and write-offs (Finance); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1). Manage Production & Inventory Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2-2) supplies valued production and inventory postings and reconciliation references that Manage Trading Sub-Ledger (CM-1-2-4-2-5) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Record and value production receipts, inventory movements, and exchanges into the accounting records | record and reconcile financial inventory balances and cost-of-sales effects per the approved methodology | reconcile financial records to operational balances and refer differences for investigation | post correcting entries per approved corrections\nOut of scope: Refinery material balance (CM-1-1-7-1-3) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | operational stock adjustments, remeasurement, and loss determination (operational owners) | valuation methodology and write-offs (Finance, locked rule) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-3-7-5"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-7-1-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Refinery accounted balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "actualized movements (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "enterprise valuation methodology (Finance)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "reconciled stock positions (CM-1-1-3-7-5)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Valued production and inventory accounting records reconciled to operational balances"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep the financial books correctly valued and complete against verified physical activity."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20008"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Execute royalty and interest disbursements arising from first-purchase and marketing arrangements — calculating owner-level distributions from settled lease transactions and division-of-interest references, managing suspense and unclaimed amounts per the applicable requirements, and submitting authorized disbursement instructions — so interest owners are paid correctly and on time."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Execution-only row completing the lease chain (contract b09, actualization b13, settlement b14, disbursement here); conditional downstream scope and the standing batch 09 ownership follow-up apply. Suspense and unclaimed-property requirements are jurisdiction-specific. APQC 20008 is a marketing-plans candidate, a false lexical match on 'marketing'."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Included only where the downstream organization purchases at lease/wellhead or administers first-purchaser arrangements, per the batch 09 caveat carried through lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8), lease actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6), and first-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6). This process is not in scope for ordinary downstream product marketing unless the organization is the first purchaser or contractually responsible disburser for lease/wellhead production. Covers disbursement execution: computing owner-level distributions from settled first-purchase transactions and current division-of-interest references, applying contractual deductions, managing suspense accounts and unclaimed-property handling per the applicable jurisdiction's requirements, responding to owner inquiries on payments, and preparing and submitting authorized disbursement instructions through the designated finance processes (batch 14 Q3 rule). Owner-payment entitlement calculation policy, upstream royalty policy, division-order legal determinations, and E&P accounting remain outside scope per batches 13-14. Excludes first-purchaser settlement calculation (CM-1-2-4-1-6); lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8); entitlement and royalty policy (outside scope); payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking).\nIn scope: Compute owner-level distributions from settled transactions and division-of-interest references | manage suspense and unclaimed amounts per applicable requirements | respond to owner payment inquiries | prepare and submit authorized disbursement instructions and track status\nOut of scope: First-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | entitlement policy, royalty policy, division-order legal determinations, and E&P accounting (outside scope) | ordinary downstream product marketing (not applicable) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Settled first-purchase transactions and statements (CM-1-2-4-1-6)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "division-of-interest references (CM-1-2-1-2-8)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "jurisdictional suspense and unclaimed-property requirements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Authorized, submitted, tracked, and evidenced royalty and interest disbursement instructions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay interest owners correctly from settled first-purchase transactions with a controlled, evidenced trail."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Record trading receivables, payables, and actuals in the accounting records — recording authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices in AR/AP, posting actualized transaction values, monitoring open items and aging, and keeping the payables and receivables position complete and current — so the financial position reflects settled and actualized activity."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the batch 14 Q3 chain: Settlements validates and submits, this process records. 'Actuals' means actualized transaction values posted to the financial records, consistent with batch 13 terminology. Whether AR/AP operations sit in the trading organization or a shared-services center is operating-model-specific (organizational neutrality, batch 10). APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage AR/AP & Actuals"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers AR/AP and actuals recording for trading activity: recording authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices in AR/AP (invoice validation itself remains with Settlements per the batch 14 Q3 chain), posting actualized values and settlement outcomes to the accounting records, monitoring open items, aging, and unmatched instructions, and coordinating overdue items with Settlements and Credit Risk through the accountable owners. Cash receipts are applied by Manage Cash Application (CM-1-2-4-2-9); accrual of un-invoiced activity by Manage Accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10); payment release and bank execution by Treasury and banking processes; collection risk decisions and credit holds by Credit Risk (CM-1-2-2-1) under the batch 11 boundary. Excludes settlement calculation and invoice validation (CM-1-2-4-1); cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-9); accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10); credit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1); payment execution (Treasury/banking). Manage AR/AP & Actuals (CM-1-2-4-2-4) follows the applicable settlement calculation and invoice validation under Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1) when posting the corresponding receivable, payable, or adjustment. Accruals and other financial-actuals activities may proceed independently; subsequent settlement changes are reflected through controlled adjustments.\nIn scope: Record authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices in AR/AP | post actualized values and settlement outcomes | monitor open items, aging, and unmatched instructions | coordinate overdue items with the accountable owners\nOut of scope: Settlement calculation and invoice validation (CM-1-2-4-1) | cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-9) | accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10) | credit decisions and holds (CM-1-2-2-1) | payment release (Treasury/banking)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-8"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices (CM-1-2-4-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "actualized transaction values (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-9"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Booked, monitored AR/AP and actuals records"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep the trading payables and receivables position complete, current, and evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Maintain the trading sub-ledger as the complete transactional accounting record — ensuring every captured, actualized, and settled transaction posts correctly under its determined treatment, reconciling the sub-ledger to the general ledger, and enforcing period cut-off — so the general ledger can rely on a complete, reconciled trading record."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). The sub-ledger is the transactional accounting record beneath the GL; its physical implementation (CTRM/ETRM, ERP, or integrated architecture) is system-design-specific and not assumed — relevant to the future system-integration workstream. This row owns the completeness and reconciliation control, not the structures or the source postings. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Trading Sub-Ledger"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers sub-ledger control, whether implemented in the CTRM/ETRM platform, an ERP sub-ledger, or an integrated accounting architecture: monitoring posting completeness from capture, actualization, settlement, and accounting sources; validating that postings follow the determined treatments (CM-1-2-4-2-1); reconciling sub-ledger balances to the general ledger and resolving breaks; enforcing period cut-off and controlled reopening; and evidencing the reconciliation. Ledger and account structures are administered by Manage Financial System/GL (CM-1-2-4-2-8, per the batch 12 binding); the postings themselves originate in the owning recording processes. Excludes GL and ledger-structure administration (CM-1-2-4-2-8); treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1); source postings (owning processes); financial reporting (CM-1-2-4-2-7).\nIn scope: Monitor posting completeness across sources | validate postings against determined treatments | reconcile sub-ledger to GL and resolve breaks | enforce period cut-off with evidence\nOut of scope: Ledger-structure administration (CM-1-2-4-2-8) | treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | source postings (owning processes) | financial reporting (CM-1-2-4-2-7)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-10"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-11"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-8"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "GL balances (CM-1-2-4-2-8)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Postings from capture, actualization, settlement, and accounting processes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "treatment reference (CM-1-2-4-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Reconciled sub-ledger with evidenced cut-off"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Guarantee the GL rests on a complete, reconciled, cut-off-controlled trading record."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the batch 12 EOM binding: Market Risk coordinates and evidences the month-end economic close and provides controlled economic inputs; this process owns the reconciliation to the books. Bridge-not-elimination (the decision owner, batch 15): expected differences (NPNS/own-use treatment, hedge-accounting timing, accrual timing, valuation basis, inventory and cost-allocation timing, realized-versus-unrealized, tax and statutory adjustments) are systematic and are explained, not defects to remove. 'PNL' retained in the technical label; 'P&L' in prose per the batch 12 convention, label queued for the naming pass. APQC 12940 'Reconcile inventories' is unrelated. Step 3d naming pass — normalization-only judgment Batch 7 (2026-09-22): renamed from 'Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL' to 'Reconcile Economic PNL to Accounting PNL'. Prior label NOT retained as skos:altLabel: the old label it differs from the new label by case only ('To' vs 'to'), which the validator flags as an altlabel-collision (queue rule: case/punctuation-only changes carry no alt label); it is preserved as prior_name in the identity/migration map."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12940"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-fasb-asc815-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Reconcile the economic profit and loss produced by the trading risk processes to the profit and loss recorded in the accounting books — identifying, quantifying, explaining, and evidencing expected and unexpected differences by cause — so management, risk, and financial views of trading results are provably consistent."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Reconcile Economic PNL to Accounting PNL"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation at each close: taking the controlled economic P&L inputs delivered by the end-of-month process (Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process, CM-1-2-2-3-5, per the batch 12 binding) and the accounting P&L from the reconciled sub-ledger and GL; identifying, quantifying, explaining, and evidencing expected and unexpected differences by cause — accounting treatment effects (hedge accounting, normal purchase/normal sale election, own-use), accrual and timing differences, valuation-basis differences, inventory valuation and cost-allocation timing, realized-versus-unrealized timing, valuation adjustments, tax and statutory adjustments, and scope differences; and escalating unexplained or out-of-tolerance differences. The reconciliation is a bridge: expected differences are explained and evidenced, not eliminated. Interim indicative reconciliations may run more frequently per local procedure. Excludes producing the economic P&L (Market Risk Management, CM-1-2-2-3, batch 12); producing the accounting P&L (the recording processes); valuation policy (Finance, locked rule).\nIn scope: Assemble economic and accounting P&L on a consistent basis | quantify and explain expected and unexpected differences by cause | evidence the reconciliation bridge | escalate unexplained or out-of-tolerance differences\nOut of scope: Economic P&L production (CM-1-2-2-3) | accounting P&L production (recording processes) | valuation policy (Finance, locked rule)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-2-3-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Controlled economic P&L inputs (CM-1-2-2-3-5)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "accounting P&L from the reconciled sub-ledger (CM-1-2-4-2-5)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "treatment reference (CM-1-2-4-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Evidenced economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation bridge"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Prove management and financial views of trading results agree, with every difference explained."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/priorPreferredLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-7",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Internal reports plus controlled inputs through accountable owners, per the batch 11 precedent; external reporting accountability stays with Finance. Normalized label 'Manage Financial Information Documentation and Reporting' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass — the 'Info.' abbreviation should not survive into the canonical glossary. APQC 20765 'Manage information' is a generic IT candidate. Step 3d naming pass — normalization-only mechanical Batch 6 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Manage Financial Info. Documentation & Reporting' to 'Manage Financial Information Documentation and Reporting'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20765"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Financial Info. Documentation & Reporting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Prepare and control the financial documentation and internal financial reporting for supply and trading activity — management reporting on financial results, accounting workpapers and reconciliation evidence, and controlled inputs to external financial reporting through the accountable owners — so financial information about trading is complete, evidenced, and consistently produced."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.7"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Financial Information Documentation and Reporting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers financial information control for trading: producing internal financial and management reporting on trading results; maintaining accounting workpapers, reconciliation evidence, and close documentation (including the settlement-accounting records excluded from the batch 14 settlement-documents working set); and preparing controlled inputs to external financial reporting, statutory filings, and audit requests through the accountable Finance owners, per the batch 11 internal-reports-and-controlled-inputs precedent. Excludes external financial reporting ownership and statutory filings (Finance); risk reporting (Market Risk and Credit Risk reporting processes, batches 11-12); the settlement working document set (CM-1-2-4-1-9); enterprise records-retention policy (governance owner).\nIn scope: Produce internal financial and management reporting on trading results | maintain accounting workpapers and close documentation | prepare controlled inputs to external reporting and audits through accountable owners\nOut of scope: External financial reporting and statutory filings (Finance) | risk reporting (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | settlement working documents (CM-1-2-4-1-9) | records-retention policy (governance owner)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "P&L reconciliation bridge (CM-1-2-4-2-6)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Reconciled sub-ledger and GL balances"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "reporting calendars and audit requests"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Internal financial reporting and controlled external-reporting inputs with evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make trading financial information complete, evidenced, and reliably reportable."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-8",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/17058"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Administer the financial system structures used by trading accounting — chart-of-accounts mapping, ledger and book structures, posting rules and business interface configuration from trading systems to the general ledger, and accounting master data — so postings land correctly, completely, and controllably in the financial records."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Business administration versus technical ownership (the decision owner, batch 15): this process owns accounting-specific configuration and business interface administration; technical development, platform operation, security, infrastructure, and deployment belong to the technology delivery function. Receiving end of the batch 12 book-structure partition: risk-reporting hierarchies at CM-1-2-2-3-1, ledger structures here. APQC 17058 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.8"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Financial System/GL"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers accounting-structure administration: maintaining ledger and book structures for trading accounting (the accounting counterpart of the batch 12 decision that risk book structures live with Market Risk and ledger structures here); chart-of-accounts mapping for trading transactions; posting rules and business-side interface configuration between trading, settlement, and financial systems; accounting master data within the accounting domain; and posting-interface error monitoring and business resolution. Changes follow the applicable change control. Enterprise master-data governance frameworks are excluded per the batch 05 precedent; ownership and operation of the enterprise GL platform itself follows the IT and Shared Services operating model. Excludes technical application development, integration-platform operation, identity and security administration, infrastructure, and release deployment, owned by Process Excellence & IT or the relevant technology delivery function; enterprise GL platform ownership (IT/Shared Services per operating model); enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion); risk book structures (CM-1-2-2-3-1, batch 12); sub-ledger reconciliation control (CM-1-2-4-2-5). Manage Financial System/GL (CM-1-2-4-2-8) maintains chart-of-accounts mappings, ledger structures, posting rules, and business interfaces that Manage AR/AP & Actuals (CM-1-2-4-2-4) needs to operate its defined process path.\nIn scope: Maintain ledger and book structures and chart-of-accounts mapping | configure posting rules and business-side interface settings | maintain accounting master data within the accounting domain | monitor posting-interface errors and drive business resolution\nOut of scope: Technical development, integration-platform operation, security administration, infrastructure, and release deployment (Process Excellence & IT / technology delivery) | enterprise GL platform ownership (IT/Shared Services) | enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion) | risk book structures (CM-1-2-2-3-1) | sub-ledger reconciliation (CM-1-2-4-2-5)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Approved structure and mapping change requests"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "accounting master data requirements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "interface specifications from trading and settlement systems"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Controlled ledger structures, mappings, and business interface configuration"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep the structural plumbing of trading accounting correct and controlled on the business side."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-9",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10759"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Apply cash receipts to open trading receivables — matching bank-reported receipts against invoices, settlement statements, and netting arrangements, investigating and coordinating resolution of unapplied, unidentified, short, and over payments, and evidencing application — so the receivables position is accurate and cash is accounted for daily."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Application-not-custody: this process accounts for cash received; Treasury owns the cash itself. Short payments or delinquency may trigger credit involvement, but the credit decision belongs to Credit Risk (batch 11 boundary). APQC 10759 'Manage cash' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; that candidate is treasury cash management, a false match for application."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.2.9"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Cash Application"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers cash application for trading receivables: matching bank-reported receipts against open items using invoice references, settlement statements, and netting agreements; applying receipts and posting application entries; investigating and coordinating resolution of unapplied, unidentified, short, and over payments with Settlements, AR, Credit, and counterparty contacts as applicable — Credit Risk makes any resulting credit decision under the batch 11 boundary, not this process; and evidencing daily application. Bank account operation, cash positioning, and funds movement are owned by Treasury and banking processes (batch 14 Q3 rule); whether cash application runs in the trading organization or a shared-services center is operating-model-specific. Excludes bank account operation and cash management (Treasury/banking); receivables booking (CM-1-2-4-2-4); settlement calculation (CM-1-2-4-1); credit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1).\nIn scope: Match bank-reported receipts to open items and netting arrangements | apply receipts and post application entries | investigate and coordinate resolution of unapplied, unidentified, short, and over payments | evidence daily application\nOut of scope: Bank operation and cash management (Treasury/banking) | receivables booking (CM-1-2-4-2-4) | settlement calculation (CM-1-2-4-1) | credit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Bank receipt reporting (Treasury/banking)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "netting agreements and settlement statements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "open receivables (CM-1-2-4-2-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Applied cash with resolved exceptions and evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Account for every receipt against the right open item, daily and evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16463"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-p180-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trading Compliance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The capability to keep supply and trading activity compliant with external law, regulation, and market rules and with the organization's own compliance framework — maintaining the trading compliance program's policies and procedures, monitoring external regulatory compliance, internal policy adherence, and the operation of compliance-supporting controls, assuring tax compliance, coordinating regulatory reporting obligations with their accountable owners, and managing compliance exceptions through to closure."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12/15 test. Monitor partition (the decision owner, batch 16): external rules (CM-1-2-4-3-2); internal compliance policies (CM-1-2-4-3-3); compliance-supporting control operation (CM-1-2-4-3-4); internal market-risk framework (CM-1-2-2-3-10) — the batch 12 dependency is resolved under Option A with reciprocal exclusions on both sides. Q5 decision: taxonomy placement retained under Commercial & Marketing with the mandatory independence and delivery-model statement in the scope note. APQC 16463 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Regulatory & Compliance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the trading compliance capability: trading-specific compliance policies and procedures, external trading compliance monitoring including market-conduct surveillance, internal compliance-policy adherence monitoring, monitoring of internally implemented controls that support external obligations, tax compliance monitoring and assurance, RFS/RINs reporting coordination, and compliance exception management. The taxonomy places this capability in the Supply & Trading branch because it governs trading activity. It is normally delivered with functional independence from trading decision-makers through an enterprise Compliance, Legal/Compliance, Risk/Compliance, or equivalent second-line function. Taxonomy placement does not establish operational reporting lines, approval authority, or organizational ownership. Boundary architecture: adherence to the internal market-risk framework is monitored by Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10) under the batch 12/16 reciprocal split; operational execution of regulatory reporting sits with its lifecycle owners (Report Trades to SDR, CM-1-2-1-3-10; Report Confirmation To SDR, CM-1-2-2-2-4; Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7); environmental, health, safety, and government reporting filings, attestations, and submissions are owned by EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule; legal interpretation, enforcement response, and proceedings by Legal & Corporate Communications; transaction-tax accounting execution by Manage Taxes (CM-1-2-4-2-11); enterprise tax policy and returns by the enterprise Tax function.\nIn scope: Maintain trading compliance policies and procedures | monitor external regulatory compliance and internal policy adherence | monitor compliance-supporting controls and assure tax compliance | coordinate reporting obligations with accountable owners and manage exceptions to closure\nOut of scope: Internal market-risk framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10) | operational regulatory reporting execution (CM-1-2-1-3-10, CM-1-2-2-2-4, CM-1-2-3-1-7) | EHS and government reporting filings and attestations (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | legal interpretation and proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications) | transaction-tax execution (CM-1-2-4-2-11) and enterprise tax policy/returns (Tax function)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/capability"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Applicable law, regulation, and market rules"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "enterprise compliance and legal frameworks"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "trading activity and control evidence from the lifecycle processes"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A monitored, evidenced trading compliance posture with exceptions managed to closure"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep trading activity provably compliant — with the law outside and the framework inside — and every compliance gap managed to closure."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/17469"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-p180-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop and maintain the trading compliance program's policies and procedures — market-conduct rules, information barriers, personal-account dealing, communications and conflicts requirements, regulatory-change management, and compliance monitoring and escalation procedures — within the enterprise compliance and legal governance framework, so trading activity operates under current, communicated compliance requirements."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Trading-program-within-enterprise-framework, parallel to the batch 15 Q4 pattern: this row owns the trading compliance program's documentation under enterprise delegation; enterprise policy and legal interpretation stay above it. APQC 17469 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers trading compliance program documentation: developing and maintaining trading-specific compliance policies and procedures; translating regulatory change into updated requirements through a managed regulatory-change process; defining monitoring, surveillance, escalation, and training requirements for the trading business; and communicating and version-controlling the program. It defines trading-program requirements within — never above — the enterprise framework: enterprise-wide compliance policy, legal interpretation, and regulatory legal positions are owned by the enterprise compliance function and Legal & Corporate Communications; the risk-policy framework is owned by risk governance (batches 11-12); this process holds no independent policy-setting authority outside that delegation. Excludes enterprise compliance policy and legal interpretation (enterprise compliance, Legal & Corporate Communications); risk policies and limits frameworks (risk governance, CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3); trading accounting procedures (CM-1-2-4-2-12); executing the monitoring the policies require (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4). Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1) maintains the approved trading-compliance policies, procedures, monitoring, and escalation rules within which Monitor Internal Trading Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3-3) operates; the recipient retains its other decisions and authorities.\nIn scope: Develop and maintain trading compliance policies and procedures within the enterprise framework | manage regulatory change into program requirements | define monitoring, escalation, and training requirements | communicate and version-control the program\nOut of scope: Enterprise compliance policy and legal interpretation (enterprise compliance, Legal & Corporate Communications) | risk-policy frameworks (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | accounting procedures (CM-1-2-4-2-12) | monitoring execution (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-7"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Applicable law, regulation, and market-rule changes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "enterprise compliance and legal frameworks"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "monitoring findings (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4) and exception patterns (CM-1-2-4-3-7)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Current, version-controlled trading compliance policies and procedures"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep the trading compliance program current, complete, and communicated."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10935"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-p180-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-p45-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor supply and trading activity for compliance with external law, regulation, and market rules — market-conduct and anti-manipulation surveillance, position-limit monitoring, exchange and market-rule adherence, licensing and registration status, and completion of regulatory reporting obligations by their owners — surfacing potential violations for assessment and escalation."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Monitor-not-execute: reporting obligations are executed by their lifecycle owners (batch 08/10/13 decisions); this process monitors completion. Compliance surveillance detects and escalates; legal interpretation and enforcement response belong to Legal — the surveillance-versus-legal distinction (the decision owner, batch 16). Applicable regimes are jurisdiction-specific; 17 CFR Part 180 (CFTC anti-manipulation/anti-fraud) and the FERC anti-manipulation rule (18 CFR 1c) anchor the US context; exchange and market rules are source-specific. Receives the Monitor Contracts exclusion (batch 09). APQC 10935 is a tax-compliance candidate, a false match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor External Trading Compliance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers external-compliance monitoring for trading: trade and communications surveillance for market conduct under the applicable anti-manipulation and anti-fraud regimes (in the United States, CEA section 6(c) as implemented by 17 CFR Part 180, and the FERC anti-manipulation rule for jurisdictional energy markets; other jurisdictions per their regimes); position-limit and accountability-level monitoring; exchange, clearinghouse, and market-rule adherence; licensing and registration status monitoring; and completion monitoring of regulatory reporting obligations executed by their lifecycle owners (SDR reporting at CM-1-2-1-3-10/CM-1-2-2-2-4, EMTS at CM-1-2-3-1-7) — this process checks that required reporting happened, it does not perform it. Potential violations are assessed and escalated per the compliance procedures; legal determination of liability, regulator interaction on enforcement, and proceedings are owned by Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes reporting execution (lifecycle owners); legal interpretation and enforcement response (Legal & Corporate Communications); internal-policy monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-3); control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4). Each potential external trading-compliance violation identified by Monitor External Trading Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3-2) invokes Manage Exceptions (CM-1-2-4-3-7) through a documented exception referral for intake and assessment, either opening a case or updating an existing case. Referral does not mean the violation has been substantiated. Monitor External Trading Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3-2) carries out its surveillance and escalation under the compliance monitoring and escalation procedures maintained by Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1).\nIn scope: Conduct trade and communications surveillance for market conduct | monitor position limits, market rules, and licensing status | monitor completion of regulatory reporting obligations by their owners | assess and escalate potential violations per procedure\nOut of scope: Regulatory reporting execution (CM-1-2-1-3-10, CM-1-2-2-2-4, CM-1-2-3-1-7) | legal interpretation and enforcement response (Legal & Corporate Communications) | internal-policy adherence (CM-1-2-4-3-3) | control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/governedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trade, order, and communications data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "applicable regime requirements (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "position and limit data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "regulatory reporting completion evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-7"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Surveillance results and escalated potential violations with evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Detect potential external-compliance violations early and route them to controlled assessment."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10935"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor adherence to the organization's internal trading compliance policies — conduct rules, information barriers, personal-account dealing, gifts and entertainment, communications requirements, and required compliance training — surfacing violations and adverse patterns for assessment and escalation."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). People-adherence monitor: this row watches whether personnel follow internal compliance policies; CM-1-2-4-3-4 watches whether compliance-supporting controls operate. Receives the Monitor Contracts exclusion (batch 09). APQC 10935 is a tax-compliance candidate, a false match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor Internal Trading Compliance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers internal compliance-policy adherence monitoring: checking trading personnel's adherence to the compliance policies maintained under Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1) — conduct rules, information barriers and need-to-know restrictions, personal-account dealing requirements, gifts and entertainment rules, communications and recordkeeping requirements, training completion, and required declarations and attestations — and escalating violations and adverse patterns per the compliance procedures. The partition with its siblings: external law and market rules are monitored by CM-1-2-4-3-2; the operation of compliance-supporting controls by CM-1-2-4-3-4; the internal market-risk framework by Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10). Excludes external-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-2); control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4); risk-framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10); risk-limit and credit-limit monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3, CM-1-2-2-1); disciplinary action (management, HR); policy authorship (CM-1-2-4-3-1). Each internal trading-compliance violation or adverse pattern identified as a compliance concern by Monitor Internal Trading Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3-3) invokes Manage Exceptions (CM-1-2-4-3-7) through a documented exception referral for intake and assessment, either opening a case or updating an existing case.\nIn scope: Monitor adherence to conduct, information-barrier, personal-dealing, and communications policies | monitor required training completion and declarations | detect and escalate violations and adverse patterns | evidence the monitoring performed\nOut of scope: External-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-2) | control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4) | risk-framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10) | risk and credit limit monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3, CM-1-2-2-1) | disciplinary action (management, HR) | policy authorship (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/governedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Compliance policies and requirements (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "declarations and attestations"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "personnel activity, communications, and training data per policy"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Adherence monitoring results and escalated violations with evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep internal compliance-policy adherence visible and violations escalated."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10735"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cftc-p45-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor the operation of internally implemented controls that support the organization's external trading obligations — verifying that required deal approvals are evidenced, regulatory reporting controls are complete and functioning, surveillance and recordkeeping controls operate as designed, and audit evidence exists — surfacing control failures and gaps to the accountable owners."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Batch 12 dependency resolved (the decision owner, Option A): this node monitors internally implemented controls supporting external obligations; Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10) monitors the internal market-risk framework; the reciprocal exclusion is recorded on both rows and neither tests the other's framework. Relationship note (the decision owner, batch 16): no typed relation to Monitor Risk Policy Compliance is recorded — 'related-to' is not a controlled relationship_type and no defined data dependency exists; the non-hierarchical boundary lives in the scope notes per the conditional-relation precedent (batches 10/13/14). Control-operation monitor, distinct from the people-adherence monitor (CM-1-2-4-3-3). APQC 10735 'Manage internal controls' is a WEAK generic candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor Internal Trade Controls"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers compliance-control monitoring: testing and monitoring that internally implemented controls supporting external obligations operate and produce evidence — required pre-trade and deal approvals evidenced in the transaction record, regulatory reporting controls (SDR, EMTS, and registry reporting completeness and error handling by their owners), surveillance and communications-recordkeeping controls, and audit-evidence availability — and escalating control failures, design gaps, and recurring weaknesses to the accountable control owners. Reciprocal boundary per the batch 12/16 decision: this process does not test the internal market-risk framework — risk appetite, approved limits, book mandates, delegation of authority, valuation and risk-control procedures, control-cycle evidence, and risk-exception approvals are monitored by Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10); neither node tests or owns the other's framework, and each may consume information from the other where relevant. Excludes the market-risk framework (CM-1-2-2-3-10, reciprocal exclusion); performing the controls themselves (control owners); external-rule surveillance (CM-1-2-4-3-2); internal-policy adherence (CM-1-2-4-3-3); internal audit's independent assurance (internal audit function). Each internal trade-control failure or design gap identified by Monitor Internal Trade Controls (CM-1-2-4-3-4) invokes Manage Exceptions (CM-1-2-4-3-7) through a documented exception referral for assessment and tracking of the applicable response, either opening a case or updating an existing case. Monitor Internal Trade Controls (CM-1-2-4-3-4) monitors the compliance-supporting controls under the policies and procedures maintained by Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1).\nIn scope: Verify required approvals are evidenced in transaction records | monitor regulatory reporting control completeness and error handling | test surveillance and recordkeeping control operation | escalate control failures and design gaps to accountable owners\nOut of scope: Internal market-risk framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10, reciprocal exclusion) | control execution (control owners) | external-rule surveillance (CM-1-2-4-3-2) | policy-adherence monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-3) | independent internal audit (internal audit function)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
