   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/17467"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-irs-p510-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor and assure compliance with transaction- and commodity-tax obligations affecting supply and trading activity by reviewing evidence of registration, licensing, exemption-certificate, filing-calendar, and tax-determination-control compliance; coordinating relevant tax-authority notices and examinations with the enterprise Tax function; and escalating material gaps."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (the decision owner): evidence-based monitoring and assurance, completing the batch 15 split (execute at CM-1-2-4-2-11; own policy, registrations, and filing at the Tax function and accountable owners; monitor here) — this process reviews evidence, it does not own the underlying data or renewal activity. Jurisdiction-specific regimes per batch 15; IRS Publication 510 anchors the US federal fuels context. APQC 17467 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Tax Compliance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers tax compliance monitoring and assurance for trading activity: reviewing evidence that licenses and registrations are current, exemption certificates valid, filing calendars met by the accountable filers, and transaction-tax determination controls operating; coordinating responses to tax-authority notices, information requests, and examinations touching trading transactions with the enterprise Tax function and the affected process owners; and escalating material gaps. Consumes status and evidence from the accountable registration, licensing, tax, commercial, and master-data owners; it does not register, license, renew, file, or determine the legal tax position itself. Completes the batch 15 three-way split: transaction-tax accounting execution is owned by Manage Taxes (CM-1-2-4-2-11); tax policy, legal positions, registrations, and return filing by the enterprise Tax function and accountable owners; monitoring and assurance here. Excludes transaction-tax execution (CM-1-2-4-2-11); tax policy, legal positions, registration, licensing, renewal, and return filing (enterprise Tax function and accountable owners); customs and trade compliance where separately owned; income tax (enterprise Tax function). Each material tax-compliance gap identified by Manage Tax Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3-5), assessed against the applicable approved materiality and escalation criteria, invokes Manage Exceptions (CM-1-2-4-3-7) through a documented exception referral for assessment and tracking of the applicable response. Required statutory reporting, correction, or payment obligations are not delayed by the internal exception workflow.\nIn scope: Review evidence of license, registration, and exemption-certificate compliance | monitor filing-calendar adherence by accountable filers | monitor tax determination control operation | coordinate tax-authority notices and examinations with the Tax function and escalate material gaps\nOut of scope: Transaction-tax accounting execution (CM-1-2-4-2-11) | tax policy, registration, licensing, renewal, and return filing (enterprise Tax function, accountable owners) | customs and trade compliance (separately owned) | income tax (enterprise Tax function)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Status and evidence from accountable registration, licensing, tax, commercial, and master-data owners"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "filing calendars and completion evidence"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "tax determination control evidence (CM-1-2-4-2-11)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "tax-authority correspondence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Tax compliance assurance results and coordinated authority responses"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assure that trading tax obligations are actually being met by their owners, with material gaps escalated."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10522"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-emts-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-epa-rfs-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate the trading organization's Renewable Fuel Standard reporting obligations — assembling and quality-assuring RIN transaction, position, and renewable-volume-obligation data from the trading systems and registries, reconciling it to EMTS, and preparing controlled reporting inputs and supporting evidence for the accountable filer — so RFS reports attributable to trading activity are complete, accurate, and evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision (the decision owner, Option A with tightened accountability): controlled-inputs coordination on the batch 11 precedent — this process supports the accountable filer and never becomes the de facto owner of regulatory filing; operational tasks require a documented delegation with the accountable filer retaining filing and attestation accountability; the batch 13 Q3 split (per-transaction EMTS at CM-1-2-3-1-7, periodic compliance at EHS&GR) is preserved exactly. EPA RFS/EMTS sources cover US RIN mechanics only — not internal ownership, delegation of account authority, EHS-versus-trading responsibilities, non-US programs, or other environmental-credit reporting. RFS mechanics per 40 CFR 80 subpart M (EMTS at 80.1452). APQC 10522 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; generic reporting candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage RINs Reporting"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the trading-side coordination of RFS reporting: assembling RIN generation, separation, trade, retirement, and position data from actualized registry records (Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7) and trading systems; reconciling reportable data to EMTS; computing and quality-assuring renewable-volume-obligation inputs; preparing controlled reporting inputs, workpapers, and supporting evidence for the accountable filer; performing operational preparation, data-entry, workflow, or submission-support tasks only where a documented delegation assigns those tasks to the trading organization, while the accountable filer retains filing and attestation accountability; and tracking reporting-calendar completion. Boundary per the EHS & Government Reporting locked rule and the batch 13 Q3 decision: periodic RFS compliance reports, attestations, audit responses, and RVO demonstrations are filed and attested by EHS & Government Reporting (or the formally designated accountable filer per the operating model); per-transaction EMTS submissions are owned by CM-1-2-3-1-7; this process coordinates, assembles, reconciles, and evidences in support of the accountable filer. Program applicability is jurisdiction-specific; this row does not cover REC, LCFS, emissions-allowance, or other environmental-credit reporting programs. Excludes filing, attestation, and submission accountability (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); per-transaction EMTS and registry submissions (CM-1-2-3-1-7); instrument settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8); position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3). Manage RINs Reporting (CM-1-2-4-3-6) prepares controlled RFS reporting inputs and evidence under the applicable trading-compliance procedures maintained by Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1), while the designated filer retains filing and attestation accountability. When external compliance monitoring checks the applicable RFS reporting obligations, Manage RINs Reporting (CM-1-2-4-3-6) supplies reconciled RFS reporting inputs, workpapers, and reporting-completion evidence that Monitor External Trading Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3-2) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Assemble and quality-assure RIN transaction, position, and RVO data | reconcile reportable data to EMTS | prepare controlled reporting inputs and evidence for the accountable filer | perform documented delegated support tasks and track reporting-calendar completion\nOut of scope: Filing, attestation, and submission accountability (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | per-transaction EMTS submissions (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | instrument settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8) | position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3) | REC, LCFS, and other environmental-credit reporting programs (their accountable owners)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-7"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/governedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized registry events and positions (CM-1-2-3-1-7)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "EMTS records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "RFS reporting calendars and requirements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "blending and production volumes"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Controlled, reconciled RFS reporting inputs and evidence for the accountable filer"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure that RFS reporting attributable to trading activity is complete, reconciled, controlled, and evidenced for the accountable filer."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-7",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20807"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage compliance exceptions through their lifecycle — logging and classifying identified violations, control failures, and approved deviations, assessing severity and regulatory implications, coordinating waiver, remediation-plan, and closure approvals under the documented compliance delegation, tracking remediation to closure, and evidencing the record — so every compliance gap is visibly owned, decided, and closed."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Coordinate-not-approve (the decision owner, batch 16): this process manages the exception workflow, evidence, and closure; the authorized approver is whoever the documented compliance delegation designates. Compliance-exception lifecycle only: risk-limit exceptions (batch 12) and credit exceptions (batch 11) stay with their documented procedures; the exception partition mirrors the dispute partition discipline (batch 10). Pattern analysis closes the loop to CM-1-2-4-3-1. APQC 20807 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; it is an IT-architecture candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.3.7"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Exceptions"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the compliance exception lifecycle across the cluster: intake from the monitoring processes (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4/-5) and self-reports; classification and severity assessment including regulatory-notification implications assessed with Legal; coordination of waiver, remediation-plan, and closure approvals under the documented compliance delegation — the authorized approver may be Compliance leadership, Legal, risk governance, management, or a committee per the delegation, and this process manages the workflow, evidence, and closure rather than approving every waiver itself; remediation planning, tracking, and closure; and pattern analysis feeding policy and control improvement (CM-1-2-4-3-1). Regulatory notification decisions and enforcement interaction are owned by Legal & Corporate Communications; risk-limit and credit exceptions follow their own documented procedures (CM-1-2-2-3, CM-1-2-2-1, batches 11-12); disciplinary action is management and HR. Excludes regulatory notification decisions and legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications); risk and credit exception procedures (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3); disciplinary action (management, HR); fixing the underlying controls (control owners, tracked here to closure). Manage Exceptions (CM-1-2-4-3-7) provides compliance exception patterns, recurring control gaps, and improvement findings to inform Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1); the recipient retains its own decisions.\nIn scope: Log and classify violations, control failures, and deviations | assess severity and regulatory implications with Legal | coordinate waiver, remediation-plan, and closure approvals under the documented delegation | track remediation to evidenced closure and analyze patterns\nOut of scope: Regulatory notification and proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications) | risk and credit exceptions (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | disciplinary action (management, HR) | control remediation execution (control owners)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Escalations from the monitoring processes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "compliance procedures and delegations (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "self-reports"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Decided, tracked, evidenced compliance exceptions closed through remediation"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ensure no compliance gap dies in a log — every one is decided under the right authority, remediated, and evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/triggeredBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-2"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-3"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-3-5"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20682"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Demurrage & Claims Management"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Laytime Claims"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage outbound and inbound claims for demurrage, despatch, detention, and other contractually defined delay or capacity-usage compensation by evaluating verified time, notice, and event evidence under the governing agreement; establishing entitlement or liability; creating, assessing, negotiating, documenting, invoicing, and tracking agreed claims; and escalating unresolved matters through the applicable commercial and legal processes."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process per the batch 08/13/14 variant test — one claim lifecycle in outbound and inbound directional variants. Dispute-partition placement principle (the decision owner, batches 10/17, approved cross-branch): what did the parties agree — Manage Confirm Disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5); what was delivered, received, measured, or performed — Manage Actualization Disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8); what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied — Manage Settlement Disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17); is delay, laytime, capacity usage, detention, demurrage, despatch, or similar compensation payable under the governing agreement — here; what legal remedy, enforcement, arbitration, or litigation applies — Legal & Corporate Communications. A single incident can raise questions in more than one category; the processes exchange evidence and referrals rather than assuming a rigid sequence. Detention guardrail (Q2): demurrage relates to time beyond laytime, despatch to early completion, and detention may arise outside laytime mechanics or under distinct agreements — the contractual wording decides whether a delay charge is demurrage, detention, despatch, an accessorial, or a liquidated-damages claim, and only contract-governed claims route here. Terminology per BIMCO Laytime Definitions 2013 (laytime, demurrage, despatch, Notice of Readiness); the governing charter party controls over any standard definition. APQC 20682 is an unrelated IT candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Demurrage Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the two-directional claim lifecycle: identifying potential claims from actualized time, notice, and event facts (Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-4, per the batch 13 handoff); creating, documenting, and pursuing outbound claims; receiving, assessing, and negotiating inbound claims; and preparing and submitting authorized receivable and payment requests through the designated finance processes per the batch 14 payment-authority rule. Under the batch 10 dispute partition, this cluster answers 'was delay or capacity usage compensable'. Includes marine demurrage and despatch under charter parties and contracts of affreightment, and detention or delay/capacity-usage claims only where the governing charter party, terminal agreement, or other commercial agreement treats them as a claim managed through this process; rail car-hire and detention charges settle with Settle Rail (CM-1-2-4-1-13) and truck detention-type accessorials with Settle Trucks (CM-1-2-4-1-4) where their transport agreements govern them, per the batch 14 carve-outs. Claims may be resolved by contractual determination, surveyor or terminal evidence, settlement agreement, credit note, set-off, arbitration, or legal process — not only bilateral negotiation; arbitration and litigation are conducted by Legal & Corporate Communications. Laytime, demurrage, and related terms are calculated under the governing agreement; industry definitions (BIMCO Laytime Definitions for Charter Parties 2013) provide terminology, not contract terms. Excludes freight and voyage-charge settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-5); the underlying time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4); confirmation, actualization, and settlement disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-3-1-8, CM-1-2-4-1-17); legal proceedings and arbitration conduct (Legal & Corporate Communications); AR/AP booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-4, CM-1-2-4-2-9); chartering and voyage operations (marine logistics processes). Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4) precedes the claim-specific posting activities within Manage AR/AP & Actuals (CM-1-2-4-2-4) by providing the calculated, validated, and authorized demurrage amount and supporting references. Subsequent claim revisions are reflected through controlled accounting adjustments; unrelated AR/AP activity proceeds independently.\nIn scope: Determine potential claims from actualized time and event facts under governing terms | create, document, and pursue outbound claims | receive, assess, and negotiate inbound claims | prepare and submit authorized receivable and payment requests and track claims to evidenced closure\nOut of scope: Freight settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-5) | time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | rail and truck detention-type charges under transport agreements (CM-1-2-4-1-13, CM-1-2-4-1-4) | other dispute quadrants (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-3-1-8, CM-1-2-4-1-17) | legal proceedings and arbitration (Legal & Corporate Communications) | AR/AP booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized time, notice, and event facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "charter party, contract of affreightment, and terminal terms (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty claims and correspondence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Resolved demurrage claims — pursued, defended, negotiated, invoiced, and evidenced"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Recover and defend delay compensation correctly — no entitlement missed, no invalid claim paid, every claim evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10188"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Screen completed voyages and terminal operations for potential demurrage, despatch, and detention entitlements and exposures — comparing used against allowed laytime from actualized notices, statements of fact, and timesheets under the governing terms, computing preliminary claim values in both directions, and identifying the contractual deadlines and evidence needed to preserve or defend each claim — so every potential claim is identified before rights lapse."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the batch 13 handoff: actualization supplies the facts, this process makes the potential-claim determination. Contract-first rule (the decision owner, batch 17): notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements vary by agreement and are identified per contract, not assumed uniform. APQC 10188 'Identify potential customers' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; false lexical match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Identify Potential Demurrage"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers claim identification: screening completed movements using the actualized time, notice, and event facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4); performing preliminary laytime calculations under the governing charter party, contract, or terminal terms; identifying both outbound entitlements and inbound exposures; identifying relevant contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements and flagging the deadlines and evidence needed to preserve or defend the claim; and routing identified potential claims to creation (outbound, CM-1-2-4-4-2) or anticipating inbound receipt (CM-1-2-4-4-3). Excludes the time-fact actualization itself (CM-1-2-3-1-4); claim creation and pursuit (CM-1-2-4-4-2, -5); inbound assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4).\nIn scope: Screen completed movements against allowed laytime under governing terms | compute preliminary claim values in both directions | identify contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements and flag deadlines and evidence needed | route identified potential claims\nOut of scope: Time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | claim creation and negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-2, -5) | inbound assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Actualized voyage and terminal time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "contractual notice and time-limit provisions"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governing laytime terms (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Identified potential claims with preliminary calculations, deadlines, and evidence requirements flagged"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Catch every compensable delay — in either direction — before claim rights expire."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Outbound leg of the two-directional lifecycle (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Create Outbound Delay Compensation Claim' queued for the naming pass. Legal-boundary rule (the decision owner, batch 17): high-risk, high-value, or disputed claims route to legal review per threshold; time-limit consequences are contract-specific (may prejudice, limit, or bar recovery), never assumed. APQC 10147 is a budgeting candidate, a false match. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Create Claim' to 'Create Outbound Delay Compensation Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10147"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Create Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Build outbound demurrage, despatch, and detention claims — performing the definitive laytime calculation under the governing terms, assembling the supporting evidence (notices of readiness, statements of fact, timesheets, port and terminal logs), verifying contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements are met, obtaining the designated internal review, and issuing the claim to the responsible party — so outbound claims are complete, timely, and defensible."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Create Outbound Delay Compensation Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers outbound claim preparation and issuance: definitive laytime and quantum calculation under the governing charter party, contract, or terminal terms including the exceptions and interruptions the terms allow; evidence assembly; verification of contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements; the designated internal review per the documented delegation, including legal review or privileged advice where required by the governing agreement, claim value, dispute status, or escalation threshold; and formal claim issuance to the counterparty, charterer, terminal, or other responsible party. A missed contractual time limit or required document may prejudice, limit, or bar recovery under the governing agreement; the claim process identifies and escalates such conditions promptly. This process routes legal questions; it does not itself make legal conclusions about enforceability. Excludes identification (CM-1-2-4-4-1); negotiation of the issued claim (CM-1-2-4-4-5); claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7); inbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-3/-4/-6/-8); legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications).\nIn scope: Perform definitive laytime and quantum calculations under governing terms | assemble notices, statements of fact, and supporting evidence | verify contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit compliance and obtain designated review including legal review where required | issue claims to responsible parties\nOut of scope: Identification (CM-1-2-4-4-1) | negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7) | inbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-3/-4/-6/-8) | legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Identified potential claims (CM-1-2-4-4-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "actualized time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governing terms and time limits (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Issued outbound claims with complete supporting evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Issue outbound claims that are complete, timely, and hard to reject."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound leg intake (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Receive Inbound Delay Compensation Claim' queued. Receipt-stage observations are recorded without prejudging their legal effect (the decision owner, batch 17) — assessment and legal/commercial review determine the response position. APQC 20096 'Receive warranty claim' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; warranty analogy, comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Receive Demurrage Claim' to 'Receive Inbound Delay Compensation Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20096"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Receive Demurrage Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Register inbound demurrage, despatch, and detention claims — logging received claims, acknowledging where the governing terms require, recording receipt-stage time-limit and documentation observations, and routing claims to assessment — so inbound claims enter a controlled process with their receipt-stage observations captured."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Receive Inbound Delay Compensation Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers inbound claim intake: registration and logging of claims received from owners, counterparties, terminals, and other claimants; acknowledgement where contractually required; receipt-stage time-limit and documentation observations recorded and made available for assessment, legal/commercial review, and the response position; and routing to Assess Inbound Claim (CM-1-2-4-4-4). Receipt-stage observations inform the response position; their legal effect is assessed downstream, not prejudged at intake. Excludes merit and quantum assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4); negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6); invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8); outbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-1/-2/-5/-7).\nIn scope: Register and log received claims | acknowledge where required | record receipt-stage time-limit and documentation observations for assessment and review | route claims to assessment\nOut of scope: Merit and quantum assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4) | negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8) | outbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-1/-2/-5/-7)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Inbound claims and supporting documents"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governing terms and time limits (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Registered inbound claims routed to assessment with receipt-stage observations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Give every inbound claim a controlled, recorded entry with receipt-stage observations captured at the door."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound assessment leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Assess Inbound Claim' queued. Independent-recalculation discipline mirrors the batch 11 validate-not-accept pattern. Contract-first rule: no cause is automatically exculpatory or compensable — the governing agreement decides. Dispute-partition routing: a disagreement about what physically happened goes to CM-1-2-3-1-8; a disagreement about compensability stays here. APQC 20105 is a claim-closure candidate, comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Assess Claim' to 'Assess Inbound Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20105"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess the merit and quantum of inbound claims — recalculating laytime independently from the organization's own actualized records under the governing terms, applying contractual exceptions, interruptions, allocation provisions, notices, and responsibility rules as the governing agreement provides, and determining an evidenced accept, counter, or reject position — so inbound claims are paid only to the extent actually owed."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess Inbound Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers inbound claim assessment: independent laytime and quantum recalculation from the organization's own actualized time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4) rather than the claimant's figures; application of contractual exceptions, interruptions, allocation provisions, notices, and responsibility rules as the governing agreement provides — specific causes such as weather or berth availability have whatever effect the contract gives them, no more; comparison against the claimed amount; and determination of an evidenced accept, counter, or reject position with the designated review per the documented delegation. Where the underlying physical or measurement facts are themselves disputed, the actualization dispute is handled by Manage Actualization Disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) per the batch 10 partition. Excludes intake (CM-1-2-4-4-3); negotiation of the position (CM-1-2-4-4-6); physical-fact disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8); legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications).\nIn scope: Recalculate laytime and quantum independently from own records | apply contractual exceptions, interruptions, allocation provisions, notices, and responsibility rules as the agreement provides | determine evidenced accept, counter, or reject positions with designated review\nOut of scope: Intake (CM-1-2-4-4-3) | negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | underlying physical-fact disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) | legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-3-1-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Registered inbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governing terms, exceptions, and responsibility rules"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "own actualized time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Evidenced accept, counter, or reject positions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay only what is actually owed, on an evidenced recalculation, never on the claimant's arithmetic."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Outbound negotiation leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Negotiate Outbound Claim' queued. Authority limitation (the decision owner, batch 17): claim resolution can create a financial concession — compromises, waivers, credits, set-offs, and write-downs beyond the documented delegation require the designated approval; this process never silently owns a Finance write-off or legal settlement authority. Resolution modes extend beyond bilateral negotiation. APQC 10046 'Communicate mission' is an unrelated candidate. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Communicate and Negotiate Claim' to 'Negotiate Outbound Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10046"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Communicate and Negotiate Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pursue issued outbound claims to resolution — presenting the claim and its evidence, responding to counterarguments and counter-calculations, negotiating commercial resolution within delegated claim-settlement authority, obtaining additional approval where a proposed compromise, waiver, credit, set-off, or write-down exceeds that authority, and agreeing resolution or escalating unresolved claims — so outbound entitlements convert to agreed, collectible amounts."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Negotiate Outbound Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers outbound claim negotiation and resolution: claim presentation and follow-up; evidence exchange and response to counterpositions; negotiation of commercial resolution within the documented delegation of claim-settlement authority, with additional approval obtained where a proposed compromise, waiver, credit, set-off, or write-down exceeds delegated authority — a resolution creating a financial concession routes through Finance or the designated authority; agreement of the settled amount; and escalation of aging, material, or deadlocked claims per procedure. Resolution may also come by contractual determination, surveyor or terminal evidence, settlement agreement, credit note, set-off, arbitration, or legal process; arbitration and litigation are conducted by Legal & Corporate Communications. Time-limit-preserving communications are maintained where negotiation extends near contractual limits. Excludes claim creation (CM-1-2-4-4-2); inbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6); claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7); write-off approval beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority); arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications).\nIn scope: Present claims and exchange evidence | respond to counterarguments and counter-calculations | negotiate commercial resolution within delegated authority and obtain additional approval for concessions beyond it | escalate aging, material, or deadlocked claims\nOut of scope: Claim creation (CM-1-2-4-4-2) | inbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7) | write-offs and concessions beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority) | arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Issued claims and evidence (CM-1-2-4-4-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "counterparty responses"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "delegated claim-settlement authority"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-7"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Agreed outbound claim resolutions or controlled escalations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Convert issued claims into agreed, collectible amounts without surrendering entitled value or exceeding authority."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound negotiation leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Validate and Negotiate Inbound Claim' queued. Same authority limitation as the outbound leg: concessions beyond the documented delegation route to the designated approval. APQC 12671 'Validate warranty claim' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; warranty analogy, comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Validate and Negotiate Claim' to 'Validate and Negotiate Inbound Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12671"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validate and Negotiate Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Resolve inbound claims from the assessed position — communicating the assessed position with its evidence, negotiating, accepting, partially accepting, countering, or rejecting the claim within delegated authority, routing concessions, waivers, or formal settlement agreements through the required approval path, and agreeing resolution or escalating unresolved claims — so inbound exposure closes at the evidenced amount, not the claimed one."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validate and Negotiate Inbound Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers inbound claim negotiation and resolution: communicating the assessed position (CM-1-2-4-4-4) and its recalculation evidence to the claimant; negotiating, accepting, partially accepting, countering, or rejecting the claim within the documented delegation of claim-settlement authority; routing concessions, waivers, or formal settlement agreements through the required approval path — a resolution creating a financial concession routes through Finance or the designated authority; and escalating material, aging, or deadlocked claims per procedure. Resolution may also come by contractual determination, surveyor or terminal evidence, settlement agreement, credit note, set-off, arbitration, or legal process; arbitration and litigation are conducted by Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes the assessment itself (CM-1-2-4-4-4); outbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5); invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8); concessions beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority); arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications).\nIn scope: Communicate assessed positions with evidence | negotiate, accept, partially accept, counter, or reject within delegated authority | route concessions, waivers, and settlement agreements through the required approval path | escalate material or deadlocked claims\nOut of scope: Assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4) | outbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8) | concessions beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority) | arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assessed positions and evidence (CM-1-2-4-4-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "claimant responses"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "delegated claim-settlement authority"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-8"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Agreed inbound claim resolutions or controlled escalations"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Close inbound exposure at the evidenced amount, under the right authority."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-7",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Outbound invoicing leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Invoice Agreed Outbound Claim' queued. Batch 14 Q3 rule applied: this process prepares and submits; Accounting books and applies — closure is confirmed by Accounting, not assumed here. Agreed-then-unpaid transitions to the settlement-dispute quadrant. APQC 20105 is a claim-closure candidate, comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Send Claim Invoice' to 'Invoice Agreed Outbound Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20105"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Send Claim Invoice"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Invoice agreed outbound claims — issuing the claim invoice and settlement statement for the negotiated amount with its supporting calculation, submitting the receivable into the designated finance processes, tracking expected receipt, and obtaining confirmation from Accounting that the receivable is booked and cash applied — so agreed entitlements are collected and evidenced."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.7"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Invoice Agreed Outbound Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers outbound claim invoicing: generating the claim invoice and supporting settlement statement for the agreed amount; issuing to the paying party; submitting the receivable into the designated finance processes for booking (Manage AR/AP & Actuals, CM-1-2-4-2-4) and receipt application (Manage Cash Application, CM-1-2-4-2-9) per the batch 14 payment-authority rule; tracking expected receipt and coordinating follow-up; obtaining confirmation from Accounting that the receivable has been booked and cash applied; and evidencing closure. A payment disagreement after agreement is a settlement dispute (CM-1-2-4-1-17) per the batch 10 partition. Excludes negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5); AR booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-4, CM-1-2-4-2-9); post-agreement payment disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Generate claim invoices and settlement statements for agreed amounts | issue to paying parties and submit receivables to the designated finance processes | track expected receipt and coordinate follow-up | obtain booking and cash-application confirmation from Accounting and evidence closure\nOut of scope: Negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | AR booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-4, CM-1-2-4-2-9) | post-agreement payment disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Agreed resolutions (CM-1-2-4-4-5)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "claim calculations and evidence"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "receivables process references"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Issued claim invoices with tracked receipt and Accounting-confirmed closure"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Collect agreed claim amounts with a complete evidence trail."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4-8",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound invoicing leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Process Invoice for Agreed Inbound Claim' queued. Batch 14 Q3 rule applied: this process prepares the request; the designated payment authority approves and releases. Pay-what-was-agreed control: the invoice validates against the negotiated resolution, not the original claim. APQC 20096 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; warranty analogy, comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — directionality-missing Batch 4 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Receive Claim Invoice' to 'Process Invoice for Agreed Inbound Claim'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20096"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-bimco-ld-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Receive Claim Invoice"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Process invoices for agreed inbound claims — validating the received invoice against the negotiated resolution and its evidence, preparing and submitting a payment request supported by the agreed resolution and required approvals to the designated Accounts Payable/Treasury process, and evidencing closure — so the organization pays exactly what was agreed, once, with a complete trail."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.4.4.8"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Process Invoice for Agreed Inbound Claim"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers inbound claim invoice processing: validating received claim invoices against the agreed resolution (CM-1-2-4-4-6) and its recalculation evidence; rejecting or querying invoices that do not match the agreement; preparing and submitting a payment request supported by the agreed resolution and required approvals to the designated Accounts Payable/Treasury process per the batch 14 payment-authority rule — the designated payment authority approves and releases payment, not this process; and evidencing closure. Duplicate or mismatched invoicing is surfaced back through negotiation or the settlement-dispute quadrant (CM-1-2-4-1-17). Excludes negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6); AP booking, payment approval, and payment execution (CM-1-2-4-2-4, Treasury/banking, designated payment authority); post-agreement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).\nIn scope: Validate received invoices against agreed resolutions and evidence | reject or query mismatched invoices | prepare and submit payment requests with required approvals to the designated AP/Treasury process | evidence closure\nOut of scope: Negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | AP booking, payment approval, and payment execution (CM-1-2-4-2-4, Treasury/banking, designated authority) | post-agreement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Agreed resolutions (CM-1-2-4-4-6)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "payables process references"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "received claim invoices"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated invoices with submitted, approved payment requests and closure evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pay agreed inbound claims exactly and once, with the trail to prove it."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude/feed supply chain management consists of processes required to plan, organize, coordinate & control crude/feed supply & processing at the refinery. This includes crude/feed transportation, slate, and supply & demand management."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude/Feed Supply Chain Management"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 3
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/17498"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-doe-sprassay-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock Quality & Slate Development"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The capability to keep feedstock selection running on trustworthy quality and economic information — maintaining validated crude and feedstock quality data, reviewing the integrity of the evaluation models that consume it, and developing the quality-screened term and spot procurement slates that feed the feedstock recommendation and trading execution."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12/15 test — data stewardship, model review, and slate development are heterogeneous concurrent functions. PTC-002 honored: production slate decided by CM-1-1-4; procurement slates recommended here. The L4 label understates the slate-development content; naming-pass note recorded. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude/Feed Quality Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the feedstock quality-information capability: crude and feedstock assay and quality-data stewardship (CM-1-2-5-1-1); integrity review of the LP and evaluation model representations that consume that data (CM-1-2-5-1-2); and development of the term (CM-1-2-5-1-3) and spot (CM-1-2-5-1-4) procurement slates, quality-screened against refinery capability. Under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed), the binding production slate and run rate are set by Refinery Planning and Optimization (CM-1-1-4); this cluster's slates are procurement recommendations. Crude and feedstock evaluation — suitability and economic value of individual crudes — is owned by Evaluate Crude & Feedstock (CM-1-1-4-7-2, batch 02). Excludes the binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); crude evaluation conclusions (CM-1-1-4-7-2); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3); enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion); laboratory testing operations (Refining/laboratory functions). Crude/Feed Quality Management (CM-1-2-5-1) provides quality-screened term and spot procurement recommendations and quality considerations to inform Coordinate Feedstock Trading (CM-1-2-5-2-1); the recipient retains its own decisions.\nIn scope: Steward validated crude and feedstock quality data | review evaluation-model input integrity | develop quality-screened term and spot procurement slates | feed the feedstock recommendation and trading execution\nOut of scope: Binding production slate and run rate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion) | laboratory testing (Refining/laboratory)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/capability"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude assays and quality data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market availability and pricing"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "refinery capability and constraint data (CM-1-1-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated quality data, model-integrity findings, and term/spot procurement slates"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Make every feedstock decision run on validated quality data, sound models, and quality-screened procurement options."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Assay stewardship in the SPR Crude Oil Assay Manual sense: standardized characterization data maintained current and validated for planning and evaluation use; implementation-level work should cite the actual contractual assay clauses, refinery lab standards, inspector procedures, and applicable API/ASTM methods for the specific attribute governed (the decision owner, batch 18). The row name's lowercase 'feedstock' is a typographical artifact — corrected capitalization queued for the naming pass. APQC: no meaningful candidate. Step 3d naming pass — normalization-only mechanical Batch 6 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Manage feedstock Data Quality' to 'Manage Feedstock Data Quality'. Prior label NOT retained as skos:altLabel: it differs from the new label by case only, which the validator flags as an altlabel-collision (queue rule: case/punctuation-only changes carry no alt label); preserved as prior_name in the identity/migration map."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11749"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-doe-sprassay-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Steward the crude and feedstock quality data used in evaluation, planning, and trading — maintaining current, validated assays and quality records, tracking assay age and representativeness, incorporating new laboratory and cargo data under controlled update procedures, and distributing consistent quality data to the consuming models and processes."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.1.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Feedstock Data Quality"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers feedstock quality-data stewardship: maintaining the assay and quality-data library used by evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2), LP models, slate development, and trading; validating and versioning updates from laboratory analyses, inspection results, and cargo experience; tracking assay currency and representativeness against actual received quality; and controlled distribution so all consumers work from the same data. Laboratory testing itself is performed by Refining and laboratory functions; cargo inspection data arrives through actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); enterprise master-data governance frameworks are excluded per the batch 05 precedent. Excludes laboratory operations (Refining/laboratory); inspection execution (independent inspectors, CM-1-2-3-1 evidence); model structures (CM-1-2-5-1-2 reviews, model owners own); enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion). Manage Feedstock Data Quality (CM-1-2-5-1-1) maintains the validated assay and feedstock-quality data library that Develop Term Slate (CM-1-2-5-1-3) needs to operate its defined process path.\nIn scope: Maintain validated, versioned assay and quality records | track assay currency and representativeness against received quality | incorporate laboratory, inspection, and cargo data under controlled updates | distribute consistent quality data to consumers\nOut of scope: Laboratory testing (Refining/laboratory) | inspection execution (CM-1-2-3-1 evidence chain) | model ownership (model owners) | enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Laboratory assays and analyses"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "inspection and cargo quality results (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "received-quality experience"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Current, validated, versioned feedstock quality data"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One current, validated set of quality data behind every feedstock decision."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/priorPreferredLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage feedstock Data Quality"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19977"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-lfmm-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Review whether the feedstock data, yield representations, operating constraints, capacities, market assumptions, and other decision inputs used by feedstock-evaluation and planning models remain current, complete, internally consistent, and representative of the refinery and market conditions; document findings and escalate required corrections to accountable data and model owners."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (the decision owner): fitness-of-representation review distinguished from model ownership, methodology approval, and formal model validation — four responsibilities, four owners (data stewardship at CM-1-2-5-1-1; model architecture with Refinery Planning/designated owner; methodology approval and independent validation with model governance; representation review here). Review-and-escalate, never own (batch 11/12 patterns). LP modeling context per the refinery planning literature. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.1.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Review LP Model Integrity"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers input-and-representation integrity review: periodic verification that the LP and evaluation models consuming feedstock data reflect current validated assays (CM-1-2-5-1-1), actual refinery configuration, constraints, and capacities, and current price and market assumptions; back-checking model predictions against actual outcomes where available; documenting findings; and escalating stale, inconsistent, or incomplete representations to the accountable data and model owners for correction. This process does not independently certify model validation, approve model methodology, alter model code, change LP constraints, or approve planning conclusions. Model methodology, structure, and ownership remain with their accountable owners: the refinery planning LP with Refinery Planning and Optimization (CM-1-1-4) and its tooling; model-methodology approval and independent validation with the applicable model governance and validation functions (batch 12 rule). Excludes model validation certification and methodology approval (model governance/validation functions); model ownership and implementation (model owners, CM-1-1-4); evaluation conclusions (CM-1-1-4-7-2); data stewardship (CM-1-2-5-1-1).\nIn scope: Verify model inputs against validated quality data and current configuration | check price and assumption currency | back-check predictions against outcomes where available | document findings and escalate corrections to accountable data and model owners\nOut of scope: Model validation certification and methodology approval (model governance/validation) | model ownership, code, and constraints (model owners, CM-1-1-4) | evaluation conclusions (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | data stewardship (CM-1-2-5-1-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated quality data (CM-1-2-5-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "model input sets and predictions versus actuals"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "refinery configuration and constraint data"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Documented model-integrity findings escalated to accountable owners"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep the models behind feedstock decisions honest about the refinery and the market as they are today."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10941"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop the recommended term feedstock procurement slate — the portfolio of crudes and feedstocks proposed for supply under agreements with multi-period or continuing commercial commitments, screened for refinery quality compatibility and evaluated for sustained economic attractiveness — so term procurement and the feedstock recommendation rest on a quality-assured base-load supply plan."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision: 'term slate' read as the term procurement portfolio recommendation — base-load supply anchored by continuing commitments — not the production slate (PTC-002 forecloses that reading). Glossary rule (the decision owner, batch 18): term supply is feedstock sourced under an agreement with a multi-period or continuing commercial commitment; spot supply is assessed or acquired for a discrete, near-term market opportunity or short-duration requirement; no fixed duration threshold is assumed — classifications vary by company, market, and contract. Quality screening justifies the Quality Management placement. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.1.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Term Slate"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers term procurement-slate development: identifying candidate term supplies, screening them against refinery quality capability using validated data (CM-1-2-5-1-1) and current evaluations (CM-1-1-4-7-2), assessing sustained economic attractiveness and supply reliability, and recommending the term slate that anchors base-load feedstock supply. Term supply means feedstock sourced under an agreement with a multi-period or continuing commercial commitment; the term-versus-spot classification follows the organization's own commercial definitions, not a fixed contractual duration. The output is a procurement recommendation: under PTC-002 the binding production slate is Refinery Planning and Optimization's (CM-1-1-4), and term-contract negotiation and execution belong to the contract and trading processes (CM-1-2-1-2, CM-1-2-1-3). Excludes the binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); contract negotiation and trade execution (CM-1-2-1-2, CM-1-2-1-3); spot procurement (CM-1-2-5-1-4).\nIn scope: Identify and screen candidate term supplies against refinery capability | assess sustained economics and supply reliability | recommend the term procurement slate | feed the feedstock recommendation and contract processes\nOut of scope: Binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | contract negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-2, CM-1-2-1-3) | spot slate (CM-1-2-5-1-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-4-7-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated quality data (CM-1-2-5-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "crude evaluations (CM-1-1-4-7-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market availability and term pricing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Recommended term procurement slate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Anchor base-load feedstock supply on quality-compatible, economically sustained term choices."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10428"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop the recommended spot feedstock procurement slate — the quality-screened spot supply candidates that address residual demand, supply disruption, quality constraints, timing needs, or economic opportunities after considering committed term supply, ranked by current economic attractiveness and availability — so spot procurement decisions start from a vetted, refinery-compatible option set."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision with the decision owner's wording correction: spot is not only deficit-filling — it addresses residual demand, disruption replacement, quality constraints, timing needs, and economic opportunity. Glossary rule per CM-1-2-5-1-3 note: no fixed duration threshold. Faster cadence than the term slate reflects spot market rhythm. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.1.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Spot Slate"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers spot procurement-slate development: quantifying the requirement against current feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2) and committed term supply, screening available spot cargoes and grades for refinery quality compatibility, ranking candidates on current economics, logistics feasibility, and timing, and recommending the spot slate to the feedstock recommendation and trading processes. Spot supply means feedstock assessed or acquired for a discrete, near-term market opportunity or short-duration requirement — spot purchases may fill residual demand, replace disrupted term supply, resolve quality constraints, optimize economics, or create flexibility. The output is a procurement recommendation: the binding production slate is Refinery Planning and Optimization's (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); spot deal negotiation and execution belong to trading (CM-1-2-1-3). Excludes the binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); term slate (CM-1-2-5-1-3); arrival and logistics planning (CM-1-2-5-3). Develop Spot Slate (CM-1-2-5-1-4) provides quality-screened spot-slate procurement recommendations to inform Coordinate Feedstock Trading (CM-1-2-5-2-1); the recipient retains its own decisions.\nIn scope: Quantify the spot requirement against demand and committed term supply | screen spot candidates for quality compatibility | rank candidates on economics, logistics feasibility, and timing | recommend the spot slate\nOut of scope: Binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | term slate (CM-1-2-5-1-3) | logistics planning (CM-1-2-5-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock demand and committed term supply (CM-1-2-5-2-2, CM-1-2-5-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "spot availability and pricing"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "validated quality data (CM-1-2-5-1-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Recommended spot procurement slate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Give spot procurement a vetted, ranked, refinery-compatible option set at market speed."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10222"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock Demand Planning"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Translate the refinery production plan into feedstock supply action — developing the feedstock demand signal, recommending the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning and Optimization, and coordinating the resulting requirements with the trading processes — so feedstock supply is planned against a validated demand and an economically informed recommendation."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one demand-to-coordination planning cycle (develop, recommend, coordinate), like the batch 03 Produce Demand Forecast precedent. PTC-002 honored at cluster level: this cluster advises and coordinates; Refinery Planning decides. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude/Feed Demand Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the feedstock demand cycle: developing projected feedstock requirements by grade, quality, quantity, and timing from the production plan (CM-1-2-5-2-2); recommending the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning and Optimization under PTC-002 (CM-1-2-5-2-3); and coordinating the resulting purchase, sale, and exchange requirements with the trading processes (CM-1-2-5-2-1). Under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed): Refinery Planning and Optimization decides the binding slate and run rate; this cluster develops demand, recommends, and coordinates — it decides none of the plan. Trading decides the deal (batch 05 decide-versus-advise pattern). Excludes the binding production plan, slate, and run rate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4). Crude/Feed Demand Management (CM-1-2-5-2) provides validated feedstock requirements and the resulting purchase, sale and exchange requests to inform Trading Management (CM-1-2-1); trading decides the deal.\nIn scope: Develop the feedstock demand signal from the production plan | recommend the economically attractive slate and run rate to Refinery Planning and Optimization | coordinate resulting requirements with trading | keep demand, recommendation, and coordination consistent\nOut of scope: Binding slate, run rate, and production plan (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Refinery production plan (CM-1-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market conditions"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock demand, slate/run-rate recommendation, and coordinated trading requirements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Keep feedstock supply planned against a validated demand and an economically informed, advisory recommendation."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19527"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate feedstock supply requirements with the trading processes — translating demand, slate recommendations, and schedule imbalances into requested purchases, sales, or exchanges with their grade, quality, quantity, timing, and delivery requirements, evaluating proposed trades for plan and schedule fit, and incorporating agreed volumes back into the supply plan."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Declared feedstock mirror of CM-1-1-3-5-2 (batch 05): this node specifies the need and consumes the result; Supply And Trading owns the trade. Decide-versus-advise pattern applied. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.2.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate Feedstock Trading"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the feedstock-side interface to trading, mirroring Coordinate Product Trading (CM-1-1-3-5-2, batch 05): specifying what the feedstock plan needs — grade, quality, quantity, timing, location, delivery basis; requesting trading action; evaluating proposed deals for refinery compatibility, schedule feasibility, and plan fit; and incorporating agreed volumes into the supply plan and downstream logistics planning (CM-1-2-5-3). Trading decides the deal; planning decides the plan (decide-versus-advise, batch 05); the binding plan is Refinery Planning and Optimization's (PTC-002). Excludes trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); the binding production plan (CM-1-1-4); transportation arrangement (CM-1-2-5-3-1); exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1). Coordinate Feedstock Trading (CM-1-2-5-2-1) supplies feedstock requirements and plan-fit assessments that inform the pre-trade analysis within Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3).\nIn scope: Specify feedstock requirements for trading action | request purchases, sales, or exchanges | evaluate proposed deals for plan, schedule, and refinery fit | incorporate agreed volumes into the supply plan\nOut of scope: Trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | binding production plan (CM-1-1-4) | transportation arrangement (CM-1-2-5-3-1) | exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
