 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10235"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop the projected feedstock requirement — quantity, grade, quality, and timing — from the refinery production plan and current inventory and supply positions, maintaining it as plans and positions change, so procurement, slate development, and trading coordination work from one validated demand signal."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Derived-not-decided: demand is computed from the binding plan and positions; the plan is Refinery Planning's. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.2.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Feedstock Demand"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers feedstock demand development: deriving requirements by grade family, quality envelope, quantity, and timing window from the binding production plan (CM-1-1-4) net of committed supply, in-transit volumes, and inventory positions (CM-1-2-5-4-2); maintaining the demand signal through plan revisions; and publishing it to slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4), the recommendation (CM-1-2-5-2-3), and trading coordination (CM-1-2-5-2-1). The demand derives from the plan; it does not set the plan (PTC-002). Excludes the production plan itself (CM-1-1-4); product demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, batch 03 constraint honored); procurement and trading action (CM-1-2-5-2-1, CM-1-2-1-3).\nIn scope: Derive feedstock requirements from the production plan net of committed supply and inventory | maintain the demand signal through plan revisions | publish one validated demand to slate development, recommendation, and coordination\nOut of scope: Production plan (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | product demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3) | procurement and trading action (CM-1-2-5-2-1, CM-1-2-1-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Binding production plan (CM-1-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "committed supply and in-transit volumes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "feedstock inventory positions (CM-1-2-5-4-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated feedstock demand by grade, quality, quantity, and timing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One validated feedstock demand signal behind every supply decision."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). PTC-002 implementation (Option 1, the decision owner, closed 2026-09-18, quoted): 'Refinery Planning decides; Supply And Trading advises. CM-1-1-4 sets the binding crude slate and run rate. CM-1-2-5-2-3 is the trading-side recommendation of an economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate, feeding the plan without setting it.' The row label overstates the mandate ('Plan Optimal') and, per the decision owner's batch 18 direction, the normalized label 'Recommend Feedstock Slate And Run Rate' is strongly queued for the naming pass to protect the PTC-002 decision from later misreading. Decide-versus-advise pattern (PTC-002 is its founding instance). APQC: no meaningful candidate. Step 3d naming pass, authority-risk category (2026-09-21, the decision owner's approval): renamed 'Plan Optimal Feedstock Slate And Run Rate' -> 'Recommend Feedstock Slate And Run Rate' — the trading-side concept recommends an economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate; it feeds the plan without setting it (decide-versus-advise, PTC-002 pattern)."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10215"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-snf-refps-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Plan Optimal Feedstock Slate And Run Rate"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Recommend the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning and Optimization — combining the demand signal, procurement slates, market prices, and supply economics into a trading-side recommendation that feeds the binding plan without setting it — so the refinery plan is informed by the best current supply-market view."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.2.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Recommend Feedstock Slate And Run Rate"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the trading-side slate and run-rate recommendation under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed 2026-09-18): assembling the economically attractive feedstock slate and run-rate case from the demand signal (CM-1-2-5-2-2), term and spot procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4), current market prices and differentials, and supply logistics; submitting the recommendation to Refinery Planning and Optimization; and iterating as market conditions change. Refinery Planning and Optimization (CM-1-1-4) decides the binding crude slate and run rate; this process advises and never sets, approves, or revises the plan. Crude evaluation is owned by Evaluate Crude & Feedstock (CM-1-1-4-7-2, batch 02). Excludes the binding slate and run-rate decision (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3). Recommend Feedstock Slate And Run Rate (CM-1-2-5-2-3) recommends the feedstock slate and run rate that inform Refinery Planning and Optimization (CM-1-1-4), which sets the binding plan.\nIn scope: Assemble the economically attractive slate and run-rate case from demand, slates, and market economics | submit the recommendation to Refinery Planning and Optimization | iterate the recommendation as markets move\nOut of scope: Binding slate and run-rate decision (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/monthly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-2-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/enabledBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-1-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market prices, differentials, and logistics costs"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "term and spot slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Trading-side slate and run-rate recommendation to Refinery Planning"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Put the best current supply-market case in front of the planner who decides."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10269"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock Logistics Planning"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Plan and manage the transportation of purchased crude and feedstocks to the refinery — coordinating movements across pipeline, marine, rail, and truck modes against the supply plan, scheduling deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows, and planning arrivals with the refinery and terminal operations owners — so purchased feedstock arrives when, where, and how the refinery can receive it."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one per-movement transport-planning lifecycle (coordinate, schedule, plan arrival), the batch 13 variant test. Inbound-feedstock counterpart of the product-side distribution planning in CM-1-1-3 under the locked SCM boundary. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude/Feed Transportation Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers feedstock transportation planning: coordinating and monitoring movements for purchased volumes under the applicable carrier and capacity arrangements (CM-1-2-5-3-1); scheduling deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows (CM-1-2-5-3-2); and planning arrival timing and sequence with the refinery and terminal operations owners (CM-1-2-5-3-3). Operates against carrier contracts administered under Manage Carrier Contracts (CM-1-2-1-2-9) and transportation deals captured under CM-1-2-1-3-9; movement actuals are recorded by Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1); transportation charges settle under the mode settlement processes (CM-1-2-4-1); delay-compensation claims follow Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4); physical movement execution belongs to carriers and operators. Excludes carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4); physical movement execution (carriers, operators); product distribution (CM-1-1-3, locked SCM boundary).\nIn scope: Coordinate feedstock movements across modes against the supply plan | schedule deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows | plan arrivals with refinery and terminal operations owners | adjust movements as plans and conditions change\nOut of scope: Carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4) | physical execution (carriers, operators) | product distribution (CM-1-1-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-1-2-9"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Agreed purchases and supply plan (CM-1-2-5-2-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "carrier terms and capacity (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "refinery receipt constraints and tankage positions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Planned, scheduled feedstock movements and arrival plans"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Get purchased feedstock to the refinery gate at the right time, mode, and cost."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12923"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate and monitor the commercial and logistical arrangements required to move agreed feedstock volumes under available carrier contracts, capacity rights, nominations, fixtures, and transport agreements — selecting modes and routings, tracking movement status, and resolving in-transit issues with carriers and operators — so every purchased cargo has a managed path to the refinery."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Coordinate-not-execute (the decision owner, batch 18): this process requests and coordinates under existing authority; charter negotiation, contracting, physical transport, discharge, and actualization stay with their owners — matching the Trade Capture, Contract Management, and nominations boundaries. Whether it holds authority to request nominations/fixtures directly or only coordinates already-authorized actions is an operating-model follow-up carried in the batch 18 PR. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.3.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Feedstock Transportation"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers movement coordination and monitoring: mode and routing selection within the supply plan and carrier arrangements; requesting or coordinating nominations, fixtures, bookings, and carrier actions under contracts administered at CM-1-2-1-2-9 and deals captured at CM-1-2-1-3-9; movement status tracking; and in-transit issue resolution with carriers and operators, escalating schedule impacts to CM-1-2-5-3-2/-3. The process may request or coordinate nominations, fixtures, bookings, and carrier actions, but it does not execute charter-party negotiation, carrier contracting, physical transport, refinery discharge operations, or movement actualization. Excludes charter-party negotiation and chartering execution (marine logistics processes); carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9); delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2); arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1).\nIn scope: Select modes and routings within the supply plan | request or coordinate nominations, fixtures, bookings, and carrier actions | track movement status | resolve in-transit issues and escalate schedule impacts\nOut of scope: Charter-party negotiation and chartering execution (marine logistics) | carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2) | arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-1-2-9"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Agreed purchases (CM-1-2-5-2-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "carrier contracts and capacity (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "movement status from carriers"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinated, tracked feedstock movements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A managed, tracked path to the refinery for every purchased cargo."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20882"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-cmu-refplan-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Schedule feedstock deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows by sequencing confirmed movements against available berth, pipeline-receipt, tankage, quality-segregation, and refinery-receipt constraints, coordinating and confirming proposed receipt windows with the relevant refinery and terminal scheduling and operations owners, and maintaining the delivery schedule as movements and refinery conditions change."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Proposes-and-confirms, does not decide feasibility: refinery/terminal operations owners retain authority over operational receipt feasibility (the decision owner, batch 18; batch 06 hierarchy). APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.3.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Schedule Feedstock Delivery"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers delivery scheduling: sequencing coordinated movements (CM-1-2-5-3-1) into agreed refinery receipt windows against berth availability, pipeline receipt capacity, tank space, and quality-segregation needs; coordinating and confirming proposed receipt windows with the relevant refinery/terminal scheduling and operations owners, who retain authority over operational feasibility; and maintaining the schedule under movement delays and refinery changes. Refinery-side unit and tank operations scheduling belongs to Refining and the production-scheduling processes under the approved planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy (batch 06); this process schedules the deliveries, not the refinery. Excludes refinery unit and tank operations scheduling (Refining, CM-1-1-7-2, batch 06 hierarchy); receipt-window operational approval (refinery/terminal operations owners); movement coordination (CM-1-2-5-3-1); arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3); discharge operations (terminal/refinery operators).\nIn scope: Sequence confirmed movements into agreed receipt windows against constraints | coordinate and confirm proposed receipt windows with refinery/terminal scheduling and operations owners | maintain the delivery schedule under changes\nOut of scope: Refinery operations scheduling (Refining, CM-1-1-7-2) | receipt-window operational approval (refinery/terminal operations owners) | movement coordination (CM-1-2-5-3-1) | arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3) | discharge operations (operators)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinated movements (CM-1-2-5-3-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "berth, receipt, and tankage constraints"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "refinery schedule interfaces (CM-1-1-7-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Confirmed, maintained feedstock delivery schedule"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Deliveries land in windows the refinery has actually agreed it can receive."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10943"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Plan individual feedstock arrivals — coordinating laycans, arrival timing, and berth and discharge sequencing with the terminal and refinery, recommending or coordinating proposed tank destinations with refinery/terminal operations subject to their operational authority, and adjusting the arrival plan as vessels, pipelines, and refinery conditions develop — so each arrival is prepared for timely, safe, and quality-compatible receipt, subject to operational conditions and the governing agreements."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Prepares-not-guarantees (the decision owner, batch 18): weather, congestion, force majeure, and operational constraints sit outside this process; it prepares each arrival for timely, safe, quality-compatible receipt subject to conditions and the governing agreements, and operators keep operational authority (batch 06 boundary). APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.3.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Plan Feedstock Arrival"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers per-arrival planning: coordinating laycan and arrival timing with carriers; planning berth assignment and discharge sequence with terminal and refinery operations; recommending or coordinating proposed tank destinations with refinery/terminal operations, subject to their operational authority over safe discharge, line-up, and receipt feasibility; monitoring approach status and replanning under delay; and providing arrival facts context that downstream actualization and demurrage processes consume. Time, notice, and event facts are recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) and demurrage determination follows CM-1-2-4-4 (batch 17); physical berth and discharge operations belong to terminal and refinery operators. Excludes discharge operations and tank-destination operational decisions (terminal/refinery operators); time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2). Plan Feedstock Arrival (CM-1-2-5-3-3) provides the planned arrival schedule that informs Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4), which records actual quantities from movement documents.\nIn scope: Coordinate laycans and arrival timing per cargo | plan berth and discharge sequence with operators | recommend or coordinate proposed tank destinations subject to operational authority | monitor approach, replan under delay, and provide arrival context downstream\nOut of scope: Discharge operations and tank-destination operational decisions (terminal/refinery operators) | time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Delivery schedule (CM-1-2-5-3-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "berth, tankage, and segregation constraints"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "vessel/pipeline approach status"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Per-cargo arrival plans maintained to discharge"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Every arrival prepared for receipt — timed, sequenced, and quality-compatible — under the operators' authority."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12960"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock Supply Position Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The capability to keep the feedstock supply position sound — monitoring and controlling feedstock inventory positions against operating requirements, and assessing and managing the price, supply, and working-capital risk those positions carry — so the refinery is supplied without avoidable risk or trapped capital."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12/15 test — two concurrent control functions, mirroring the batch 04 Inventory capability precedent. Receiving end of two bindings: the batch 04 reciprocal (finished products/intermediates there, feedstock here) and the batch 11 inventory-risk exclusion. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Crude/Feed Supply Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the feedstock supply-position capability: feedstock inventory position monitoring and control (CM-1-2-5-4-2, the feedstock counterpart of the batch 04 finished-products row under its reciprocal constraint) and feedstock inventory risk assessment and management (CM-1-2-5-4-1, the receiving row of the batch 11 Credit Risk exclusion). Inventory policy — including crude and feedstock policy — is owned by the batch 04 policy rows (CM-1-1-3-7-1); reconciliation by CM-1-1-3-7-5; refinery tank operations by Refining. Excludes inventory policy design (CM-1-1-3-7-1); book-to-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); tank operations (Refining); trading-book market risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3, batch 12); counterparty credit risk (CM-1-2-2-1). Crude/Feed Supply Management (CM-1-2-5-4) supplies controlled feedstock inventory and supply-position records that Develop Feedstock Demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Monitor and control feedstock inventory positions | assess and manage feedstock inventory risk | keep supply-position information current for planning and trading\nOut of scope: Inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1) | reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | tank operations (Refining) | trading-book risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3) | counterparty credit (CM-1-2-2-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/capability"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock positions, movements, and commitments"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market prices"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "supply plan and demand (CM-1-2-5-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Controlled feedstock positions with assessed, managed risk"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A supplied refinery, a controlled position, a bounded risk."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12942"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ccro-002a"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess the price, supply-availability, disruption, quality, concentration, and working-capital risk associated with feedstock inventory positions; recommend inventory, supply, operational, and hedging mitigations to accountable owners; and submit approved hedging requirements to the authorized trading process so risk is visible, understood, and managed within approved appetite."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q5 decision (the decision owner): receiving row of the batch 11 exclusion; request-not-initiate — this process submits approved hedging requirements to the authorized trading process; Trading Strategy Management, Trade Approval, and Trade Capture govern strategy, authorization, and execution (batch 07/08 chain); booked hedges are measured by Market Risk (batch 12 rule). Risk categories are illustrative, not a mandated metric set — measurement methodology is governance-controlled and company-specific. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.4.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess & Manage Inventory Risk"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers feedstock inventory risk: assessing price exposure from inventory valuation and forward prices; supply and disruption risk from delayed cargoes, outages, unavailable grades, or constrained logistics; quality and segregation risk from off-spec, incompatible, or constrained positioning; concentration risk from excessive exposure to one grade, source, geography, supplier, route, or facility; and working-capital risk from inventory value, dwell time, and cash tied up in stock; recommending inventory, supply, operational, and hedging mitigations to the accountable owners; and submitting approved hedging requirements or mitigation requests to the authorized trading process — Trading Strategy Management, Trade Approval, and Trade Capture govern strategy, authorization, and execution (batches 07-08). Receiving row of the batch 11 exclusion: inventory risk here, counterparty credit risk at CM-1-2-2-1. Boundary with Market Risk (batch 12): once a hedge or position is booked into the trading book, its measurement, limits, and P&L belong to Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3); non-trading inventory exposure is assessed here. Risk appetite, limit approval, and risk-measurement methodology remain with risk governance (batches 11-12). Excludes hedge strategy, authorization, and execution (CM-1-2-1-4, CM-1-2-1-3); trading-book measurement (CM-1-2-2-3, batch 12 rule); counterparty credit (CM-1-2-2-1); risk-appetite, limit, and methodology approval (risk governance); inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1). Assess & Manage Inventory Risk (CM-1-2-5-4-1) recommends inventory, supply and hedging mitigations that inform Trading Management (CM-1-2-1); approved hedging requirements are submitted to the authorized trading process, which decides execution.\nIn scope: Assess price, supply, disruption, quality, concentration, and working-capital risk of feedstock positions | recommend inventory, supply, operational, and hedging mitigations to accountable owners | submit approved hedging requirements to the authorized trading process | keep inventory risk visible against requirements\nOut of scope: Hedge strategy, authorization, and execution (CM-1-2-1-4, CM-1-2-1-3) | trading-book risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3) | counterparty credit (CM-1-2-2-1) | risk-appetite, limit, and methodology approval (risk governance) | inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Feedstock positions (CM-1-2-5-4-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market prices and forward curves"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "refinery requirements (CM-1-2-5-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "working-capital parameters"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assessed inventory risk with recommended mitigations and submitted hedging requirements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "No feedstock inventory risk that nobody has measured, owned, and decided to carry."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12942"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-api-mpms-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-5-4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Monitor and control actual and projected crude and feedstock inventory positions — identifying operating-limit, availability, quality, location, and balance exceptions against refinery requirements and the supply plan, coordinating timely response, and providing reliable current-position information to planning, trading, and logistics."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Receiving row of the batch 04 reciprocal: CM-1-1-3-7-12 holds finished products and intermediates so this row holds feedstock — the mirrored wording is deliberate. Identifies-and-escalates: feedstock sourcing, purchase, exchange, and replenishment decision rights belong to the applicable supply-planning, Refinery Planning, and authorized trading processes (the decision owner, batch 18) — replenishment stays aligned to the binding Refinery Plan, never independent of it. EI HM 32 daily stock-control practice context. APQC: no meaningful candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.5.4.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Feedstock Inventory"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers feedstock position stewardship, the feedstock counterpart of Monitor and Control Inventory Positions (CM-1-1-3-7-12) under the batch 04 reciprocal constraint: recurring review of physical and book feedstock positions, movements, in-transit volumes, and commitments; minimum, maximum, safety-stock, and operating-limit exceptions against refinery run requirements; short-term availability and quality-segregation risks; escalation and coordination of corrective actions through the accountable owners; and maintenance of the position view that demand development (CM-1-2-5-2-2), arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3), and inventory risk (CM-1-2-5-4-1) consume. Excludes inventory policy design (CM-1-1-3-7-1); measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4); book-to-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); finished-product and intermediates positions (CM-1-1-3-7-12, batch 04 reciprocal); tank operations (Refining); feedstock sourcing, purchase, exchange, and replenishment decision rights, which belong to the applicable supply-planning, Refinery Planning and Optimization, and authorized trading processes.\nIn scope: Monitor physical, book, and in-transit feedstock positions against requirements | identify and escalate operating-limit, availability, and quality exceptions | coordinate corrective action through accountable owners | maintain the current-position view for planning, trading, and logistics\nOut of scope: Inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1) | measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4) | reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | finished-product/intermediates positions (CM-1-1-3-7-12) | tank operations (Refining) | sourcing, purchase, exchange, and replenishment decisions (supply-planning, Refinery Planning and Optimization, and authorized trading processes)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/daily"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Tank gaugings and book positions"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "commitments and receipts"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "in-transit movements (CM-1-2-5-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "refinery run requirements"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Controlled feedstock position view with escalated exceptions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A current, reliable feedstock position with every exception owned and answered."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Refined product supply chain management consist of processes that are required to maintain an optimal supply network. This covers the generation of the demand forecast, associated customer allocations, the demand & supply balancing process & the execution of terminal replenishment."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Refined Product Supply Chain Management"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 3
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one periodic analysis cycle (segment, evaluate, assess, recommend). Q5 decision: supply-economics analysis, distinct from Marketing's commercial segmentation; analyze-and-recommend with decisions at the accountable owners. Normalized label 'Supply Network Participation Analysis' queued — the current label sounds like network design/operations ownership (the decision owner, batch 19). APQC candidates are WEAK generic matches. Step 3d naming pass, authority-risk category (2026-09-21, the decision owner's approval): renamed 'Network Management' -> 'Supply Network Participation Analysis' — the concept analyzes supply-network participation; the old label sounded like network design/operations ownership."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/12899"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-lnl-t2s-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Network Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Analyze the refined-product supply network's market participation — segmenting customers and markets on supply economics, evaluating market attractiveness for supply-network participation, and assessing cost-to-serve performance — and recommend where and whom the network should serve profitably, informing the network and participation decisions of the accountable commercial and planning owners."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Supply Network Participation Analysis"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the market-participation analysis cycle: supply-economics customer and market segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1), market attractiveness evaluation (CM-1-2-6-1-2), and cost-to-serve assessment (CM-1-2-6-1-3), combined into recommendations on network participation, service levels, and supply-envelope choices. Analyze-and-recommend (batch 11 pattern): supply, production, inventory, and allocation decisions are owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2, batch 03 rule); commercial and brand segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy by the Marketing processes (CM-1-3) where defined; network-investment decisions by their accountable owners. Excludes supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); commercial segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy (CM-1-3); network investment decisions (accountable owners); demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, CM-1-1-1-1). Supply Network Participation Analysis (CM-1-2-6-1) recommends where and whom the network should serve, which informs Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); the integrated plan decision remains with Regional Optimization. Supply Network Participation Analysis (CM-1-2-6-1) recommends where and whom the network should serve, which informs Refined Product Demand Management (CM-1-2-6-2).\nIn scope: Segment customers and markets on supply economics | evaluate market attractiveness for supply-network participation | assess cost-to-serve performance | recommend network participation and service choices to accountable owners\nOut of scope: Supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | commercial segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy (CM-1-3) | network investment decisions (accountable owners) | demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, CM-1-1-1-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Sales, volume, and margin history"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market and competitor intelligence"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "supply and logistics cost data"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Network participation and service recommendations with segmentation and cost-to-serve evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Know which markets and customers the supply network serves profitably — and recommend accordingly."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19968"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-lnl-t2s-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Segment the customers and demand points served by the refined-product supply network on supply-economics dimensions — volume, product mix, location, delivery mode, service requirements, and margin contribution — so participation, service, and cost-to-serve analysis work from an economically meaningful segmentation."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Four-lens rule (the decision owner, batch 19, consistent with the Party-plus-Role customer-domain model): the same customer can legitimately exist in supply-economics segmentation (here), Marketing/commercial segmentation (CM-1-3), credit segmentation (credit and order processes), and the party/account hierarchy (customer domain/MDM) — no one segmentation is universal, and this row claims only the supply-economics lens; parallels the batch 11 relationship-based credit split. APQC 'Validate with customers' is a WEAK unrelated candidate."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.1.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Segment Customers"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers supply-economics segmentation: grouping customers and demand points by the dimensions that drive supply cost and value — volume and mix, location and logistics reach, delivery mode and service requirements, and contribution; maintaining the segmentation as the network and market change; and supplying it to attractiveness evaluation (CM-1-2-6-1-2) and cost-to-serve assessment (CM-1-2-6-1-3). Commercial, brand, and marketing segmentation for customer strategy, pricing, and offer design is owned by the Marketing processes (CM-1-3) where defined — the same customer may appear in both segmentations for different purposes. Excludes commercial and marketing segmentation (CM-1-3); customer strategy and pricing (CM-1-3); order and credit management (CM-1-3-7).\nIn scope: Group customers and demand points on supply-economics dimensions | maintain the segmentation as network and market change | supply the segmentation to attractiveness and cost-to-serve analysis\nOut of scope: Commercial and marketing segmentation (CM-1-3) | customer strategy and pricing (CM-1-3) | order and credit management (CM-1-3-7)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Customer volume, mix, location, and delivery data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "logistics and service records"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "margin contribution"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Maintained supply-economics segmentation"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "An economically meaningful customer segmentation behind every network analysis."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10107"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-steo-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-lnl-t2s-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Evaluate market attractiveness for supply-network participation using accessible demand, realizable supply margin, logistics reach and cost, supply reliability, specification and regulatory constraints, and network fit — so participation recommendations rest on a consistent supply-network economic evaluation."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Supply-network-centered evaluation (the decision owner, batch 19), not generic market-entry strategy — the question is what the network can serve profitably. Evaluate-and-rank, decide-elsewhere (batch 11 pattern); jurisdiction and specification requirements vary by market. APQC candidate is a WEAK generic match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.1.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Evaluate Market Attractiveness"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers market attractiveness evaluation centered on the supply-network question: assessing markets and segments on accessible demand, realizable supply margin from the network's supply points, logistics reach and cost, supply reliability, specification and regulatory constraints, and fit with the existing and planned network; ranking participation options; and feeding the evaluation into network recommendations (CM-1-2-6-1) and demand management (CM-1-2-6-2). This is not generic market-entry strategy: participation, supply, and allocation decisions stay with the accountable owners (CM-1-1-2, commercial leadership); market-entry strategy and marketing programs with CM-1-3. Excludes participation and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2, accountable owners); marketing strategy and programs (CM-1-3); demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3).\nIn scope: Assess markets on accessible demand, realizable margin, logistics reach, reliability, and constraints | rank participation options consistently | feed evaluations into network recommendations and demand management\nOut of scope: Participation and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2, accountable owners) | marketing strategy (CM-1-3) | demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "logistics cost and access data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market demand and margin intelligence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Ranked market attractiveness evaluations for network participation"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A consistent economic answer to 'is this market worth serving from our network?'"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10314"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-lnl-t2s-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess the full cost of serving customers, segments, and markets from the refined-product supply network — supply, storage, handling, primary and secondary logistics, and service costs against realized margins — identifying where the network serves profitably, marginally, or at a loss, so participation and service recommendations rest on evidenced economics."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Assessment on governed data at an agreed allocation basis — consumes Accounting and logistics costs, never creates allocation or transfer-pricing rules (the decision owner, batch 19); plan-variance backcasting remains with Distribution Backcasting under its locked rule (batch 03). APQC candidate is a WEAK production-performance match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.1.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers cost-to-serve assessment: assembling supply, storage, throughput, handling, transportation, and service costs by segment, customer group, and market from the accountable cost owners' data; setting them against realized volumes and margins; identifying profitable, marginal, and loss-making service; and feeding results to segmentation, attractiveness evaluation, and network recommendations. Costs are reported at an agreed allocation and attribution basis; cost-to-serve analysis does not itself create accounting cost allocations or transfer-pricing rules — those remain with Accounting, Finance, and the applicable legal-entity policy. Uses governed cost and margin data from Accounting and the logistics processes; it does not produce cost accounting (CM-1-2-4-2), set prices (CM-1-3), or decide service changes (accountable owners). Excludes cost accounting production and allocation rules (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance); transfer pricing (Finance/legal-entity policy); pricing (CM-1-3); service and participation decisions (accountable owners); distribution cost-performance backcasting (CM-1-1-6, locked rule).\nIn scope: Assemble cost-to-serve by segment, customer group, and market from governed data at the agreed allocation basis | set costs against realized volumes and margins | identify profitable, marginal, and loss-making service | feed evidenced findings to network recommendations\nOut of scope: Cost accounting production and allocation rules (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance) | transfer pricing (Finance/legal-entity policy) | pricing (CM-1-3) | service and participation decisions (accountable owners) | distribution backcasting (CM-1-1-6, locked rule)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-4-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governed cost data (CM-1-2-4-2, logistics processes)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "realized volumes and margins"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Evidenced cost-to-serve assessments by segment and market"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Know what serving each customer and market truly costs — and show it with evidence."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19697"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-steo-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Operational Product Demand Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage refined-product demand on the supply side — maintaining the operational demand picture the supply chain runs on, forecasting location-level product demand for supply operations, and managing product-exchange volumes as a balancing lever — so supply operations, replenishment, and trading coordination work from one current, operational demand view."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one operational demand cycle. Batch 03 three-way forecast partition implemented: enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1), trading intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2), operational SCM-context forecasting here. APQC candidate is an unrelated documentation match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Refined Product Demand Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the operational demand cycle: managing the current demand picture against supply capability (CM-1-2-6-2-1), operational location-level demand forecasting for the supply-chain context (CM-1-2-6-2-3, under the batch 03 constraint), and operational management of product-exchange volumes as a supply and demand balancing lever (CM-1-2-6-2-2). The enterprise published demand forecast is owned by Produce Demand Forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) and consumed here as an input; trading's forward-view intake is Receive Demand Forecast (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery-planning demand basis is Capture Product Demand Requirements (CM-1-1-4-7-1). Excludes the enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, batch 03); trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1); order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7); supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2).\nIn scope: Maintain the operational demand picture against supply capability | forecast location-level demand for supply operations | manage exchange volumes as a balancing lever | supply one operational demand view to supply operations and trading coordination\nOut of scope: Enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2) | refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1) | order capture (CM-1-3-7) | supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-1-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Published enterprise forecast (CM-1-1-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "exchange positions and entitlements"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "orders and lifting patterns"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "supply capability"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Operational demand picture, location-level forecasts, and exchange-utilization plans"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One current operational demand view behind supply operations, replenishment, and trading coordination."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-2-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16630"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage the operational refined-product demand picture — consolidating current orders, lifting patterns, committed volumes, and forecast demand by product and location, comparing it against available supply, and surfacing imbalances for supply, replenishment, and trading action — so shortfalls and surpluses are visible and acted on before they become failures."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Recommends-and-routes (the decision owner, batch 19): the process actively recommends supply actions and contingencies while the batch 03/04 decision rights stay with their owners — allocation with Regional Optimization, replenishment with CM-1-1-3-7-9. APQC candidate is an unrelated image-management match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.2.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Refined Product Demand"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers operational demand management: consolidating the current demand view from orders, rack liftings, contract commitments, and the operational forecast (CM-1-2-6-2-3); comparing demand against available and planned supply by product and location; identifying imbalances, allocation pressure, and demand risks; and routing actions to the accountable owners — replenishment requirements to the batch 04 decision owner (CM-1-1-3-7-9), trading needs through Coordinate Product Trading (CM-1-1-3-5-2), exchange utilization to CM-1-2-6-2-2. It may recommend supply actions and contingency options, but allocation decisions remain with Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2, batch 03 rule); order commitments with Order Management (CM-1-3-7). Excludes allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7); replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3).\nIn scope: Consolidate the current operational demand view by product and location | compare demand against available and planned supply | identify imbalances and recommend supply actions and contingency options | route actions to the accountable owners\nOut of scope: Allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7) | replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Orders and lifting data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "exchange positions (CM-1-2-6-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "operational forecast (CM-1-2-6-2-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "supply availability"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Current demand-supply picture with recommended actions routed to owners"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "No demand shortfall or surplus that nobody saw coming."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-2-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16630"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-orp-dpsc-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Coordinate operational utilization of product-exchange entitlements to support approved demand and supply requirements by monitoring available exchange positions, planning proposed lifting and delivery use by location and period, and communicating utilization requirements to the relevant agreement, trading, supply, and scheduling processes."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision with the decision owner's caution applied: operational visibility and proposed utilization here; contractual imbalance calculation, commercial decisions, administration, and settlement at CM-1-2-7-1-1 (batch 08 'not product exchange agreements' and batch 14 imbalance-treatment bindings honored). Use-versus-administer split parallels the batch 11 set-versus-apply pattern. APQC candidate is an unrelated image-management match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.2.6.2.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Refined Product Exchanges"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the operational use of product exchanges: monitoring available positions, entitlements, and imbalance status under existing exchange agreements for operational visibility; planning proposed lifting and delivery utilization by location and period against demand needs (CM-1-2-6-2-1) and supply plans; communicating utilization requirements to the agreement owner (CM-1-2-7-1-1), trading coordination, supply operations (CM-1-2-6-3), and the scheduling processes; and flagging utilization or imbalance concerns to the agreement owner. Operational visibility of imbalance status does not constitute contractual imbalance calculation, commercial decision, agreement administration, or settlement — exchange agreements, terms, and imbalance treatment are owned by Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1, batch 08/14 bindings); 'exchange' here means product exchange arrangements, not futures exchanges (CM-1-2-1-3-4, batch 08). Excludes exchange agreement administration, contractual imbalance calculation, and imbalance treatment (CM-1-2-7-1-1); futures-exchange trading (CM-1-2-1-3-4); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6). Manage Refined Product Exchanges (CM-1-2-6-2-2) uses exchange entitlements within the terms of the agreements administered by Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1).\nIn scope: Monitor available exchange positions and entitlements for operational visibility | plan proposed lifting and delivery utilization by location and period | communicate utilization requirements to agreement, trading, supply, and scheduling processes | flag utilization and imbalance concerns to the agreement owner\nOut of scope: Exchange agreement administration and contractual imbalance calculation and treatment (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | futures-exchange trading (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/cadence/weekly"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
