   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-2"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-2-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-3"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-3-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-4-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-5"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-5-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Governed findings from the five analyses"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "consumer decision calendars"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "metric definitions in use"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Decision-ready integrated insight and stewarded marketing-analysis metrics"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One integrated, measurable evidence base under every marketing decision."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Terminal Loading consist of processes such as monitoring customer sales activity and implementing control strategies based upon supply, customer credit and/or contract limitations. Allocation decisions are based on evaluating deals and transaction data to determine which product at the terminal should be made available to the customers. It is responsible for sending locks and unlocks to the terminal when it determines that availability should change."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Terminal Loading"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 3
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 41 (2026-09-21). Record-versus-decision wording, authorization lifecycle, and Tax/Legal-approved status wording adopted verbatim from review; Loading-Authorized Party is a governed role/relationship (CM-1-3-7-1-1), not merely an access-control flag. Receiving end of the onboarding/credit/KYC chain (b28/b31/b34). Naming queue: preferred label 'Set Up and Maintain Terminal Customer Authorization'. APQC 'Maintain IT customer contracts' is unrelated; comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — generic-operational Batch 5 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Setup and Maintain Customer In Terminal' to 'Set Up and Maintain Terminal Customer Authorization'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20637"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-fmcsa-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-irs-p510-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Setup and Maintain Customer In Terminal"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Set up and maintain authorized customers, carriers, and drivers in terminal loading systems: create lifting-authorization records in the terminal automation system from governed commercial, credit, and master data; assign loading credentials and permitted terminals, products, and supply positions; keep authorizations current as commercial, credit, and tax status change; and deactivate access when eligibility ends."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.10.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Set Up and Maintain Terminal Customer Authorization"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "This process maintains terminal-loading authorization and credential records from approved upstream decisions. It may record carrier, driver, vehicle, terminal, product, supply-position, tax-status, and credential eligibility attributes where required by the terminal operating model. It does not determine safety qualification, driver licensing, carrier eligibility, hazardous-materials compliance, tax status, credit status, or master identity; it consumes and applies the authoritative outcomes from their owning processes. Commercial eligibility is decided by the onboarding processes under the Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule, credit standing and holds by Manage Credit & Risk, due-diligence outcomes by Perform KYC Due Diligence, and the governed customer record by Maintain Customer and Commercial Account Master Data. Loading-Authorized Party is a governed role/relationship, not merely an access-control flag: setup resolves the approved customer-domain role decided under the governed customer model (Maintain Customer and Commercial Account Master Data) and never redefines it. Authorization lifecycle: approved customer/carrier/driver/vehicle/product/supply-position data leads to terminal authorization/credential configuration, then authorization activation, ongoing monitoring of upstream status changes, suspension/revocation on loss of eligibility, and an audit trail with reactivation only on approved restoration. Fact-dependent statement: where applicable to a US terminal/rack transaction, the terminal commercial setup process consumes Tax-approved registration, certificate, position-holder, notification, and exemption-status outcomes required by the relevant federal, state, and contractual framework (SRC-IRS-P510-001 context). Tax and Legal determine applicability, legal status, and required evidence; setup records and applies the approved outcomes to terminal-loading authorization controls. Physical terminal operation belongs to the terminal operator (Terminal Operations Management, where company-operated per the locked Midstream rule).\nIn scope: Lifting-authorization and credential record creation and maintenance in terminal loading systems | authorization activation | loading credential issuance, update, and revocation | permitted terminal, product, and supply-position mapping | carrier, driver, and vehicle authorization records (record-only, from accountable owners) | linkage of credit and allocation controls into loading authorization | recording of Tax-approved status outcomes | ongoing monitoring of upstream status changes | suspension, revocation, and audit-trailed reactivation on approved restoration\nOut of scope: Commercial eligibility and onboarding decisions (Onboarding owners under the Governed Submission and Stewardship Rule) | credit decisions and holds (Manage Credit & Risk) | safety qualification, driver licensing, carrier eligibility, and hazardous-materials compliance (Safety and Carrier-management owners) | tax status determination (Tax and Legal) | customer master record ownership (Maintain Customer and Commercial Account Master Data) | security and identity frameworks (Security owners) | physical terminal operations (Terminal Operations Management) | deal creation (Capture Terminal Sales Deal)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-7-1-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-7-4-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Governed customer master records (CM-1-3-7-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "KYC outcomes (CM-1-3-7-4-6)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Tax-approved status outcomes"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "carrier/driver/vehicle qualification outcomes from accountable owners"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "credit standing and controls (CM-1-3-7-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "executed deals (CM-1-3-10-4)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The controlled terminal-loading authorization record that enforces approved decisions about who may lift what, where, and against which supply position."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Only eligible, credit-cleared, tax-valid parties can lift product, and access ends when eligibility does."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 41 (2026-09-21). Customer-intake counterpart of shipper-side Plan & Execute Nominations per the b33 commercial/physical split; nomination-cycle wording and 'confirmed commercial lifting allocation/outlook, not a physical loading schedule' correction adopted verbatim from review. Naming queue: preferred label 'Process Customer Lifting Forecasts and Nominations'. APQC 'Process returns' is unrelated; comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — generic-operational Batch 5 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Process Forecast and Nominations' to 'Process Customer Lifting Forecasts and Nominations'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20094"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-doe-rppl-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Process Forecast and Nominations"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Receive and process customer lifting forecasts and nominations at terminals: validate nominated volumes against supply positions, allocations, and terminal capability for the nomination period; confirm, adjust, or decline nominations within approved rules; manage in-cycle revisions; and maintain the confirmed commercial lifting outlook that supply, replenishment, and allocation processes consume."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.10.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Process Customer Lifting Forecasts and Nominations"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Customer-facing intake side of the nomination relationship, honoring the commercial/physical split (b33): this process handles lifting forecasts and nominations received from terminal customers; nominations the company itself submits to pipelines, vessels, rail, and terminal operators as shipper are owned by Plan & Execute Nominations. A confirmed customer lifting nomination is a confirmed commercial lifting allocation/outlook, not a physical loading schedule; physical scheduling remains with the operational scheduling processes. Customer lifting nominations may be monthly, weekly, daily, or event-driven by channel, agreement, and terminal practice; the process operates on the applicable nomination cycle and supports in-cycle revisions. Confirmed customer lifting nominations and the confirmed commercial lifting outlook feed the operational demand view (Manage Refined Product Demand) and the lifting-history-based forecast (Forecast Operational Refined Product Demand, CM-1-2-6-2-3), and set the volumes Manage Terminal Lifting Allocation enforces. A confirmed nomination is not a sales contract (Capture Terminal Sales Deal) and not a commercial order (Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment).\nIn scope: Customer forecast and nomination intake and validation | feasibility checks against supply position, allocation, and terminal capability | confirmation, adjustment, and decline decisions within approved rules | in-cycle revision management | communication of confirmed customer lifting nominations | nominated-versus-lifted performance tracking\nOut of scope: Shipper-side carrier and terminal nominations (Plan & Execute Nominations) | supply-side demand forecasting (Forecast Operational Refined Product Demand, CM-1-2-6-2-3) | allocation enforcement (Manage Terminal Lifting Allocation) | physical loading scheduling (Operational scheduling processes) | order capture and fulfillment (Capture And Manage Order Fulfillment)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/constrainedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-1"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Customer forecasts and nominations"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "allocation limits (CM-1-3-10-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "deal terms (CM-1-3-10-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "supply positions and replenishment plans"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "terminal capability and constraints"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-3-6-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Confirmed customer lifting nominations and the confirmed commercial lifting outlook consumed by supply, replenishment, and allocation."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Customer lifting intent is validated and confirmed against what the supply system can actually serve."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 41 (2026-09-21). Named home of 'rack and terminal loading allocation enforcement' delegated by CM-1-1-2-11 (b04); title/rights limitation and four-way control separation (allocation, credit, tax, terminal/safety — a successful allocation does not imply a successful loading authorization) adopted verbatim from review. Naming queue: preferred label 'Manage Terminal Lifting Allocation'. APQC 'Manage routings' is unrelated; APQC 'Manage rack allocations' is the closer partial match (comparative only, per the b20 note on CM-1-2-7-2-3). Step 3d naming pass — generic-operational Batch 5 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Manage Allocation' to 'Manage Terminal Lifting Allocation'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11743"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ei-hm32-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Allocation"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Enforce approved allocation controls at terminals and loading racks: translate plan-level allocation decisions and confirmed customer lifting nominations into customer, product, and period lifting limits in terminal loading systems; monitor liftings against limits; apply authorized proration and shortage procedures; and adjust controls as supply positions and confirmed nominations change."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.10.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Manage Terminal Lifting Allocation"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Enforcement, not decision — the same rule as Allocate & Release Order (b33): plan-level assignment of constrained supply to markets, channels, terminals, and commitments is owned by Allocate Finished Products, which delegates rack and terminal loading allocation enforcement here; order-level allocation and release is owned by Allocate & Release Order; this process applies the approved rules at the point of lifting, documents allocation actions, and escalates conflicts it cannot resolve within them. Shortage and disruption procedures are applied as authorized by the accountable commercial and supply leadership, never invented at the rack. Terminal allocation enforcement applies commercial supply-position and lifting limits at the rack. It does not determine title transfer, tax liability, physical custody, product quality, driver safety, terminal sequence, or physical load authorization beyond the approved commercial/credit/tax/allocation controls. Control separation: allocation control asks whether product is available to this customer and supply position under approved allocation; credit control whether the customer is eligible to lift under approved financial exposure rules; tax control whether the transaction is eligible under approved tax status; terminal/safety control whether the carrier, driver, vehicle, and site are authorized and operationally eligible. A successful allocation does not imply a successful loading authorization if another control fails. Continuous control with event-driven adjustment as positions change.\nIn scope: Allocation limit configuration in terminal loading systems | lifting monitoring against limits | authorized proration and shortage procedure application | allocation adjustment on supply or nomination changes | allocation action documentation and conflict escalation\nOut of scope: Plan-level allocation decisions (Allocate Finished Products) | order-level allocation and release (Allocate & Release Order) | credit limit and hold decisions (Manage Credit & Risk) | title transfer, tax liability, custody, and product quality determinations (Their accountable owners) | physical loading execution (Terminal Operations Management)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-1"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/governedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-1-2-11"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Plan-level allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2-11)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "authorized shortage and proration procedures"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "confirmed nominations (CM-1-3-10-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "credit controls (CM-1-3-7-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "supply positions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/continuous"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Enforced lifting limits at the rack — liftings held within allocated volumes, with documented allocation actions."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Constrained supply is lifted only within the volumes the business decided each customer should get."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 41 (2026-09-21). Dual book-ownership/system-of-record boundary with Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3), legal-status wording, captured-content list, and controlled-correction wording adopted verbatim from review; the boundary is recorded as scope text because no controlled relationship type expresses a boundary. Naming queue: preferred label 'Capture Terminal Sales Deal' — high-priority name normalization. APQC 'Analyze deal options' (M&A) is unrelated; comparative only. Step 3d naming pass — generic-operational Batch 5 (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Capture Deal' to 'Capture Terminal Sales Deal'. Prior label retained as altLabel."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16795"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-gloss-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-pmm-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Capture Deal"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-10"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Record concluded terminal sales arrangements — rack and spot sales, branded and unbranded supply arrangements, and terminal-specific pricing arrangements — completely and promptly in the governed commercial system: customer, product, terminal, volume basis, price and price mechanism, duration, and terms references — so authorized deals are available to customer setup, nomination processing, allocation, pricing application, and billing."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.10.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Capture Terminal Sales Deal"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Boundary with Trade Capture (dual test, batch 41 decision): Terminal Sales Deal Capture records customer-facing terminal/rack commercial arrangements in the governed commercial/O2C pricing system when those arrangements authorize lifting, price commercial rack/spot supply, and feed terminal authorization, customer lifting nominations, allocation enforcement, billing, and customer service. Trade Capture records transactions governed in the trading book — trading-book physical purchases/sales, exchange, storage, financial, structured, transportation, and transmission deals — with their associated trading controls, confirmations, risk, actualization, settlement, and trading-accounting lifecycle. Where an arrangement has both terminal-commercial and trading-book representation, the authoritative record, system/interface, and reconciliation responsibility must be explicitly defined; neither process may create duplicate uncontrolled deal records. A deal belongs here only where the approved operating model identifies the commercial/O2C terminal sales record as the system of record for the customer-facing arrangement. If a trading-book deal also creates terminal lifting rights, the terminal authorization record must reference the governed trading/commercial deal identifier and consume the approved terms through controlled integration rather than re-create the deal. The process records the legal/commercial status assigned by the approved quote, agreement, contract, or order framework. It does not independently determine whether a document is binding, negotiate terms, approve a commercial arrangement, or establish price/discount master data. Captured deal content includes, as applicable: customer/supply account, terminal/rack, product/grade, supply position, contract/arrangement reference, price basis/mechanism, effective dates, volume commitment or lifting right, tax status, credit/authorization prerequisites, channel/brand status, terms references, deal/document legal status, and version/audit lineage. Price and discount conditions are maintained in governed price master data by their owning processes; this process links deals to them and never invents prices, honoring the Billing Integrity Rule's requirement for governed commercial inputs. Capture corrections are controlled commercial-record events that preserve the original record, reason, authorizing source, effective date, and downstream impact assessment. They do not silently overwrite lifting, billing, price, tax, or contractual history.\nIn scope: Deal entry and validation against execution evidence | completeness checks on commercial terms | linkage to customer, supply account, terminal, product, supply position, price mechanism, and terms | recording of deal/document legal status and version/audit lineage | controlled correction of capture errors | making deal data available to setup, nominations, allocation, pricing application, and billing\nOut of scope: Deal negotiation and approval (Commercial owners under the delegation of authority) | binding-status determination (Approved quote, agreement, contract, or order framework owners) | trading-book transaction capture (Trade Capture) | price and discount master data (Manage Commercial Master Data Stewardship owning processes) | billing execution (Manage Customer Invoicing and Billing)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-7-1-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Executed deal terms and evidence"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "credit eligibility (CM-1-3-7-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governed customer master (CM-1-3-7-1-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "governed price and terms data (CM-1-3-7-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The governed terminal deal record — the commercial authority that setup, nominations, allocation, pricing, and billing consume."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Every terminal lifting traces to a completely recorded, authorized commercial arrangement."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Strategy process establishes target markets to work in and, at a micro level, provides the strategy for where store locations should be established. For B2B this considers supply envelopes & establishes target geographic markets."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Strategy"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 3
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-2-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11095"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-pmpa-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop and maintain the approved brand strategy for the company's downstream fuels and products marketing — brand positioning, brand architecture, and where and how the brand competes — through a recurring, hypothesis-driven strategy cycle grounded in the governed marketing evidence base and the applicable enterprise delegation and approval process, so brand investments and branded-channel commitments follow deliberate, evidenced, approved direction."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one hypothesis-driven strategy lifecycle (batch 08/21 variant pattern at the strategy layer). Lifecycle order: Establish Brand Objectives and hypothesis → Formulate and Evaluate Strategic Operating Alternatives → Develop & Test Brand Hypothesis → Develop / update Brand. Review governance rule: strategy develops, recommends, and maintains approved direction under the applicable delegation of authority — it never unilaterally alters franchise, trademark, or investment commitments. PMPA applicability is fact-dependent (statement verbatim in scope note); Legal owns all franchise legal analysis. Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop KM strategy' is a false lexical match ('strategy' senses differ)."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.2.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Brand Strategy"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the brand-strategy lifecycle: establishing brand objectives and testable hypotheses (CM-1-3-2-1-2), formulating and evaluating strategic operating alternatives for how the brand goes to market (CM-1-3-2-1-1), developing and testing brand hypotheses against evidence (CM-1-3-2-1-3), and developing and updating the approved brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1-4). Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a branded motor-fuel franchise, changes to trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal arrangements may be constrained by the PMPA and applicable state law. Legal classification, interpretation, notice requirements, termination/non-renewal analysis, and franchise documentation remain the responsibility of Legal & Corporate Communications. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Excludes brand standard enforcement and identity execution (CM-1-3-6-7); marketing communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5); trademark and licensing legal work (Legal, locked rule; CM-1-3-4-4); campaign planning and execution (CM-1-3-5, CM-1-3-6); the analyses consumed (CM-1-3-1). Brand Strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) sets brand positioning and where the brand competes, which informs Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy (CM-1-3-2-4). Marketing Communications Strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) carries the approved brand strategy set by Brand Strategy (CM-1-3-2-1) to its audiences.\nIn scope: Run the recurring brand-strategy cycle | develop and maintain approved brand positioning and architecture | develop and maintain approved direction on where and how the brand competes, including branded versus unbranded participation | recommend brand investment priorities under the applicable approvals\nOut of scope: Brand standard enforcement (CM-1-3-6-7) | marketing communications strategy (CM-1-3-3-5) | trademark/licensing legal work (Legal locked rule; CM-1-3-4-4) | campaign planning/execution (CM-1-3-5/6) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1) | pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
