   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-1-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Template row (sibling rows CM-1-3-3-2-5 and CM-1-3-3-3-5 by design). Review terminology decision: this is a domain measurement framework, not KPI governance — 'KPI Framework' in the locked name is easy to misread as KPI approval authority; preferred label 'Define Offer Measurement Framework' queued prominently for the naming pass. Standard measurement-framework statement verbatim in scope note; routing via Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Offer Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Offer)' recorded in the identity/migration map."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19521"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define the measurement framework for customer offers — the metric definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities by which offer performance will be managed — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.1.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define Offer Measurement Framework"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers offer measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate offer, pricing, or channel design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Offer measures span uptake, mix, margin contribution, target-group penetration, and retention effect, aligned with the marketing-analysis metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); performance measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5); the metrics stewardship capability (CM-1-3-1-6).\nIn scope: Define offer measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | align with stewarded marketing-analysis metrics | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance\nOut of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | measurement execution (CM-1-3-5-5) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "KPI Store classification rules"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Validated offers (CM-1-3-3-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/tactical"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The offer measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Every offer launched with its scorecard already defined."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/priorPreferredLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define KPI Framework (Offer)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define KPI Framework"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/10123"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-pmm-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ftc-rp-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Establish and manage the pricing strategy for downstream marketing — the strategic pricing approach, the integrated pricing strategy and approved price parameters and guardrails across products, channels, and customer groups, and the piloting and performance feedback that keep it evidenced — within the applicable price-related legal frameworks and approvals, so pricing execution operates against governed, deliberate pricing direction."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one strategy-pilot-feedback lifecycle; horizon strategic. Lifecycle per review: Define Strategic Pricing Approach → Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy → Define Pricing Measurement Framework → Conduct Pricing Pilots and/or deploy through execution → Analyze Pricing Performance → feed back to Integrated Pricing Strategy; pilots and performance analysis are evidence sources into the strategy, not a rigid chain. Review governance rule verbatim in scope note: price architecture/rules/guardrails under commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals; owns approved price parameters and guardrails, never specific posted prices or contract rates. Robinson-Patman applicability fact- and jurisdiction-dependent (verbatim); competition-information rule standardized (verbatim). APQC 'Define pricing strategy' STRONG, no validator — genuine comparative anchor."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pricing Strategy Management"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the pricing-strategy lifecycle: defining the strategic pricing approach (CM-1-3-3-2-1), developing the integrated pricing strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2), defining the pricing measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-2-5), conducting pricing pilots (CM-1-3-3-2-4), and analyzing pricing performance (CM-1-3-3-2-3), with pilots and performance analysis operating as a feedback loop into the integrated strategy rather than a rigid sequence. The receiving side of the batch 22 price-role boundary. Develops and maintains approved price architecture, pricing rules, differential logic, index-linkage principles, discount/terms guardrails, and exception criteria under the applicable commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals. Approved parameters include price corridors, floors, and ceilings where governed, price-index and timing conventions where applicable, and discount/rebate guardrails — never rebate administration, specific posted prices, or contract rates. Execution stays out: transaction and sales pricing at CM-1-3-6-3; quote and contract pricing at CM-1-3-7-2. Where applicable, pricing strategy must be designed and applied with regard to price-discrimination risk in sales to competing purchasers, applicable defenses, contract structures, product comparability, competitive effects, state motor-fuel below-cost/unfair-sales laws, and other jurisdictional requirements. Legal and Compliance determine applicability and legal sufficiency. No pricing strategy, pilot, or performance analysis may use non-public competitor price, cost, capacity, customer, or future commercial information obtained through improper channels. Public, licensed, independently gathered, and legally permissible observations must be traceable to source and permitted use. Excludes transaction/sales pricing execution (CM-1-3-6-3); quote/contract pricing (CM-1-3-7-2); trading and supply pricing (CM-1-2, its processes); rebate design administration (CM-1-3-6-4); legal ownership of pricing law compliance (Legal, compliance framework); enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream). Sales Pricing Management (CM-1-3-6-3) sets prices within the approved parameters, corridors and rules established by Pricing Strategy Management (CM-1-3-3-2). When Customer Offer Definition designs price or discount terms within an offer, Pricing Strategy Management (CM-1-3-3-2) maintains approved pricing architecture, index-linkage principles, corridors, and discount guardrails within which Customer Offer Definition (CM-1-3-3-1) operates; the recipient retains its other decisions and authorities.\nIn scope: Run the pricing-strategy lifecycle | develop and maintain approved price architecture, rules, parameters, and guardrails under the applicable approvals | pilot and analyze pricing as a feedback loop | operate within price-related legal frameworks | issue governed pricing direction to execution\nOut of scope: Transaction/sales pricing (CM-1-3-6-3) | quote/contract pricing (CM-1-3-7-2) | trading/supply pricing (CM-1-2) | rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4) | pricing-law ownership (Legal, compliance) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream) | specific posted prices and contract rates (execution)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Approved strategy direction (CM-1-3-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market and competitor findings (CM-1-3-1-1/-5, legitimate sources)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "pricing performance evidence"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/strategic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The governed pricing strategy: approved architecture, rules, parameters, and guardrails"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Deliberate, evidenced, lawful pricing direction under every posted and negotiated price."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19949"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ftc-rp-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define the strategic pricing approach for downstream marketing — the pricing postures (value, cost-plus, index-linked, market-following, premium) by product, channel, and customer group, consistent with the strategic role of price set by the strategy branch."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). First step of the pricing lifecycle. APQC 'Define the strategic vision' is a WEAK generic match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.2.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define Strategic Pricing Approach"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers pricing-approach definition: translating the strategic role of price (batch 22 boundary) into pricing postures by product, channel, and customer group; setting the principles the integrated strategy must satisfy, including the legal guardrails (price-discrimination risk, below-cost statutes, competition-information rule); and framing the pricing-strategy cycle. Excludes the integrated strategy itself (CM-1-3-3-2-2); price parameters and rules (CM-1-3-3-2-2); price levels (execution); legal assessment (Legal, Compliance).\nIn scope: Translate the strategic price role into pricing postures | set principles including legal guardrails | frame the pricing-strategy cycle\nOut of scope: The integrated strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | price parameters/rules (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | price levels (execution) | legal assessment (Legal, Compliance)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Strategic price role (CM-1-3-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "legal guardrail advice (Legal)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "market findings (CM-1-3-1-1)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/strategic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The strategic pricing approach by product, channel, and customer group"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A stated pricing posture before any price is set."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11095"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ftc-rp-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop the integrated pricing strategy — the approved price architecture, differential logic, index-linkage principles, corridors and guardrails, and pricing rules that connect postures across products, channels, and customer groups into one coherent, legally defensible system — issued as governed direction to the pricing execution processes."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Core development step of the pricing lifecycle; review governance wording verbatim in scope note. Feedback relations: pilots and performance analysis inform this process — the decision owner's relation rule ('informs: Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy' from pilots) is vocabulary-missing, applied as informed-by on this receiving row per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent. APQC 'Develop KM strategy' is a FALSE lexical match (third occurrence of the knowledge-management 'strategy' confusion — carried forward); comparative only."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.2.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers integrated pricing-strategy development. Develops and maintains approved price architecture, pricing rules, differential logic, index-linkage principles, discount/terms guardrails, and exception criteria under the applicable commercial, Finance, Legal, Compliance, and delegation-of-authority approvals. Includes price corridors, floors, and ceilings where governed; price-index and timing conventions where applicable; discount/terms guardrails and exception criteria — not rebate administration, posted prices, or contract rates. Ensures cross-channel coherence so branded, unbranded, and direct channels are priced on defensible, documented logic — material where competing purchasers buy at different prices; defenses such as cost justification and meeting competition are Legal's assessment. Maintains the strategy from pilot and performance feedback (the lifecycle's feedback loop). Sets architecture, parameters, and rules, never transaction prices (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2). Excludes transaction price setting (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2); rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4); legal defense assessment (Legal); the pilots and performance analysis (CM-1-3-3-2-4/-3). Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2) revises the pricing strategy informed by the performance findings of Analyze Pricing Performance (CM-1-3-3-2-3).\nIn scope: Develop approved price architecture, differential logic, and index-linkage principles | set corridors, floors, ceilings, and discount/terms guardrails with exception criteria under the applicable approvals | keep cross-channel pricing coherent and documented | maintain the strategy from pilot and performance feedback\nOut of scope: Transaction prices (CM-1-3-6-3, CM-1-3-7-2) | rebate administration (CM-1-3-6-4) | legal defense assessment (Legal) | pilots and performance analysis (CM-1-3-3-2-4/-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pricing approach (CM-1-3-3-2-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "cost-to-serve and channel economics"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "pilot and performance evidence (CM-1-3-3-2-4/-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/strategic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-4"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The integrated pricing strategy: approved architecture, parameters, guardrails, and rules"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "One coherent, documented price system instead of a thousand unconnected prices."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16500"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-eia-pmm-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Analyze the performance of deployed pricing — realization against architecture, margin and volume effects, competitive position, and rule adherence — producing the recurring evidence that keeps the pricing strategy honest."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). The recurring feedback loop of the pricing lifecycle; legal-escalation boundary per review verbatim in scope note. Pilots also inform this analysis (vocabulary-missing 'informs', applied as informed-by here per the batch 10 precedent). APQC 'Analyze performance' scored 0.8 STRONG — genuine match. EIA PMM price statistics are aggregate external context within their b21 limitations."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.2.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Analyze Pricing Performance"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers pricing performance analysis: measuring price realization against the intended architecture; analyzing margin, volume, and mix effects by product, channel, and customer group; assessing competitive price position from legitimate sources only (competition-information rule at CM-1-3-3-2; EIA statistics as external context); monitoring execution's adherence to pricing rules as analytical findings; and feeding findings into the integrated strategy (the lifecycle's feedback loop). Findings concerning potential legal, competition, discriminatory-pricing, below-cost, or unfair-sales issues must be escalated to Legal/Compliance; this process does not make legal determinations. Excludes price correction execution (CM-1-3-6-3); improper price intelligence (prohibited); legal determinations (Legal/Compliance); enterprise performance reporting (its owners); the strategy updates themselves (CM-1-3-3-2-2). Analyze Pricing Performance (CM-1-3-3-2-3) analyzes the posted and transaction prices determined by Sales Pricing Management (CM-1-3-6-3). Analyze Pricing Performance (CM-1-3-3-2-3) produces pricing performance findings that inform Develop Integrated Pricing Strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2).\nIn scope: Measure realization against the intended architecture | analyze margin, volume, and mix effects | assess competitive position from legitimate sources | report rule-adherence findings, escalate potential legal issues, and feed the strategy loop\nOut of scope: Price corrections (CM-1-3-6-3) | improper price intelligence (prohibited) | legal determinations (Legal/Compliance) | enterprise reporting (its owners) | strategy updates (CM-1-3-3-2-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-6-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-7-2-5"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "EIA price statistics (context)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Transaction and realization data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "legitimate competitive price data"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-2-5)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/periodic"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pricing performance findings feeding the next strategy cycle"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pricing strategy corrected by evidence, not anecdote."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/13169"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-ftc-rp-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Conduct authorized pricing pilots — controlled, time-bounded tests of pricing changes in defined markets or channels — measuring effects before strategy-wide deployment, within the same legal guardrails as deployed pricing."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Evidence-source step of the pricing feedback loop; pilot-control safeguard per review verbatim in scope note. Relation rule (the decision owner, review): pilots 'inform' the integrated strategy and performance analysis rather than strictly preceding them — 'informs' is not in the relationship vocabulary, so it is recorded as informed-by on the receiving rows (CM-1-3-3-2-2, CM-1-3-3-2-3) per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent, with this rule recorded verbatim: pilots may run before or after limited deployment depending on the control model, may generate immediate results, and post-deployment performance analysis may continue long after pilots end. APQC 'Conduct pricing analysis' STRONG, no validator — comparative anchor."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.2.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Conduct Pricing Pilots"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers pricing pilots: designing controlled tests of proposed pricing changes; coordinating pilot execution through the pricing execution processes (CM-1-3-6-3 executes; this process designs and evaluates); measuring effects against the defined measures; and recommending adoption, adjustment, or abandonment to the integrated strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2). A pricing pilot requires documented scope, eligible population/channel, time period, success/failure thresholds, legal/compliance review where required, approval authority, customer/partner communication requirements, data collection plan, rollback conditions, and post-pilot decision owner. Pilots operate under the same legal guardrails as deployed pricing; pilot pricing is still real pricing. Excludes pilot price execution (CM-1-3-6-3); the adoption decision (CM-1-3-3-2-2 with the applicable approvals); legal assessment (Legal).\nIn scope: Design controlled pricing tests with the required documented controls | coordinate execution through pricing execution processes | measure effects and recommend adopt/adjust/abandon\nOut of scope: Pilot price execution (CM-1-3-6-3) | adoption decisions (CM-1-3-3-2-2) | legal assessment (Legal)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-6-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Proposed pricing changes (CM-1-3-3-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "pilot design parameters and controls"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "pilot market data"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operatingMode": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/operating-mode/event-driven"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pilot results with an adopt/adjust/abandon recommendation"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Price changes tested small before they are deployed large."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-5",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/provenance": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Template row (see CM-1-3-3-1-4); review terminology decision: domain measurement framework, not KPI governance — preferred label 'Define Pricing Measurement Framework' queued prominently. Standard measurement-framework statement verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Define objectives' is a WEAK generic match. Step 3d naming pass (2026-09-21): renamed from 'Define KPI Framework' to 'Define Pricing Measurement Framework' — critical semantic-collision resolution. Prior label retired from ontology aliases (a shared generic altLabel would recreate the collision); scoped historical alias 'Define KPI Framework (Pricing)' recorded in the identity/migration map."
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/19521"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define the measurement framework for pricing — realization, margin, differential-integrity, and competitive-position measures with their definitions, baselines, targets, and measurement responsibilities — as evidence-layer measures with governed routing toward enterprise KPIs."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.2.5"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define Pricing Measurement Framework"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers pricing measurement design. Defines domain-level measures, baselines, targets, measurement responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence needed to evaluate offer, pricing, or channel design outcomes. The framework may nominate measures as candidate KPIs but does not approve, publish, version, calculate, certify, or govern enterprise KPIs. Those activities follow the Enterprise Measurement Catalog, Candidate KPI Register, Enterprise KPI Catalog, and KPI Store governance process. Pricing measures span price realization, margin capture, differential integrity, discount leakage, and competitive position, supplying the framework Analyze Pricing Performance measures against and aligning with the metrics stewarded at CM-1-3-1-6. Excludes enterprise KPI definition and KPI Store governance (KPI workstream); the measurement itself (CM-1-3-3-2-3); metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6).\nIn scope: Define pricing measures, baselines, targets, responsibilities, data sources, and review cadence | supply the framework for pricing performance analysis | nominate candidate KPIs into KPI Store governance\nOut of scope: Enterprise KPI approval, publication, and governance (KPI Store workstream) | performance measurement (CM-1-3-3-2-3) | metrics stewardship (CM-1-3-1-6)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-1-6"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Integrated pricing strategy (CM-1-3-3-2-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "KPI Store classification rules"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "stewarded metric definitions (CM-1-3-1-6)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/tactical"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-2-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The pricing measurement framework (evidence-layer, KPI Store-routed)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Pricing measured against a framework, not against opinions."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/priorPreferredLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define KPI Framework (Pricing)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define KPI Framework"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16819"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-pmpa-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Design the sales and distribution channel system that implements the approved channel strategy — segmenting channels, defining the channel portfolio, creating channel-specific offerings, and developing channel structure, size, and engagement models — so channel management executes a designed system rather than an accumulation of arrangements."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one channel-design lifecycle; horizon tactical. Lifecycle order: Segment Channels → Define Channel Portfolio → Create Channel Offering & Value Proposition → Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model → Define Channel Measurement Framework. Middle layer of the batch 22 three-layer rule; PMPA applies fact-dependently per the batch 22 statement (referenced, not restated); partner-selection exclusion per review verbatim in scope note. APQC returned NONE — no meaningful candidate; 'Design for manufacturing' is a false match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Sales & Distribution Channel Design"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers the channel-design lifecycle: segmenting channels (CM-1-3-3-3-3), defining the channel portfolio (CM-1-3-3-3-2), creating channel offerings and value propositions (CM-1-3-3-3-1), developing channel structure, size, and engagement models (CM-1-3-3-3-4), and defining the channel measurement framework (CM-1-3-3-3-5). Designs within the approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) and value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3); design changes affecting existing branded dealer or distributor arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement, with all franchise legal analysis reserved to Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes selection, onboarding, negotiation, and management of individual channel partners, dealers, jobbers, distributors, or marketers; those processes follow Channel & Partner Management and applicable Contract Management/Legal processes. Retail network design (site-level) sits at CM-1-3-3-4 (batch 24); secondary distribution operations at CM-1-1-3-8 (locked rule). Excludes channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4); retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4); distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule); franchise legal work (Legal, locked rule); pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3). Channel & Partner Management (CM-1-3-6-1) executes the engagement models designed by Sales & Distribution Channel Design (CM-1-3-3-3). Network Design (Retail) (CM-1-3-3-4) develops the retail-network design from the channel system designed by Sales & Distribution Channel Design (CM-1-3-3-3).\nIn scope: Run the channel-design lifecycle | segment channels and define the channel portfolio within approved strategy | create channel-specific offerings from approved propositions | develop structure, size, and engagement models | define channel measurement with KPI Store routing\nOut of scope: Channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4) | retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | individual partner selection, onboarding, negotiation, and management (CM-1-3-6-1, Contract Management/Legal) | distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule) | franchise legal work (Legal) | pricing (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-candidate"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/informedBy": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-2-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "approved propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/tactical"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The designed channel system: portfolio, offerings, structure, and engagement models"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "A channel system that was designed, not accumulated."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 4
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-1",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/11168"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Create the channel-specific offerings and value propositions — what each channel of trade receives from the company and offers onward — implementing the approved customer, marketer, and consumer propositions in each channel's terms."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Implements, never redefines, the approved propositions (batch 22 boundary); review distinction verbatim in scope note — channel-model proposition here, customer-group offer at CM-1-3-3-1. APQC 'Define offering and customer value proposition' STRONG, no validator — comparative anchor."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.3.1"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Create Channel Offering & Value Proposition"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers channel-offering creation: specializing the approved value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) into channel-model propositions and standardized channel offer components (supply terms shape, brand and program access, support and service elements) per channel segment; keeping channel offerings consistent with each other and with the propositions; and coordinating price-term structure with Pricing Strategy Management. This process designs channel-model propositions and standardized channel offer components; it does not configure customer-specific offers, negotiate individual partner terms, or establish individual contract prices. The design object here is the channel operating proposition — what a channel participant receives, carries, or commits to as part of the channel model; concrete offers for target customer groups are Customer Offer Definition's (CM-1-3-3-1). Excludes proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3); customer-specific offer configuration (CM-1-3-3-1); individual partner negotiation (CM-1-3-6-1, CM-1-3-7-2); price architecture and rules (CM-1-3-3-2); contract prices (execution).\nIn scope: Specialize approved propositions into channel-model propositions and standardized offer components | keep offerings consistent across channels and with the propositions | coordinate price-term structure with pricing processes\nOut of scope: Proposition strategy (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | customer-specific offers (CM-1-3-3-1) | partner negotiation (CM-1-3-6-1, CM-1-3-7-2) | price architecture/rules (CM-1-3-3-2) | contract prices (execution)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Approved propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "channel portfolio (CM-1-3-3-3-2)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "channel segments (CM-1-3-3-3-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/tactical"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-4"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Channel-model propositions and standardized channel offer components"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Each channel knowing exactly what it gets and what it carries."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-2",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16403"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-pmpa-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define the channel portfolio — which channel segments the company serves through which channel types, with what portfolio balance — implementing the approved channel participation and target mix as a designed portfolio."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Portfolio step of the channel-design lifecycle; capacity-validation caveat per review verbatim in scope note — a strategically approved portfolio may be operationally infeasible. APQC 'Define portfolio governance' is a WEAK partial match (business-portfolio governance, different scope)."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.3.2"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Define Channel Portfolio"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers portfolio definition: translating the approved channel participation, roles, and target mix (CM-1-3-2-4) into the designed channel portfolio; balancing coverage, economics, and risk across channel types; identifying portfolio gaps and overlaps; and recording the portfolio design rationale. Portfolio design is validated against supply, logistics, terminal/fleet, retail-network, channel-partner, technology, and service-delivery capacity through the accountable operating and design processes. Portfolio changes affecting existing branded arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement (franchise legal analysis with Legal). Excludes channel strategy and mix approval (CM-1-3-2-4 and its approvals); individual partner selection (CM-1-3-6-1); operating-capacity ownership (the accountable operating processes); franchise legal analysis (Legal).\nIn scope: Translate approved participation and mix into the designed portfolio | balance coverage, economics, and risk | validate against operating and delivery capacity through the accountable processes | identify gaps and overlaps with rationale\nOut of scope: Strategy and mix approval (CM-1-3-2-4) | partner selection (CM-1-3-6-1) | operating-capacity ownership (accountable processes) | franchise legal analysis (Legal)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/no-source"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Approved channel strategy (CM-1-3-2-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "channel economics"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "channel segments (CM-1-3-3-3-3)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "operating-capacity validation inputs"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/tactical"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-1"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The designed, capacity-validated channel portfolio with rationale"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "The approved channel mix made concrete as a feasible, designed portfolio."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-3",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/20001"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Segment the channel landscape for design purposes — grouping channels and channel participants by role, economics, capability, and service needs — producing the segmentation the portfolio and engagement models are designed against."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). First step of the channel-design lifecycle; four-lens rule applied — design-lens segmentation only; enterprise taxonomy governs (batch 22 caveat). APQC 'Determine channels to be supported' is a WEAK adjacent match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.3.3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Segment Channels"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers channel segmentation: grouping channels of trade and participants within them by strategic role, economics, capability, and service requirements, using governed counterparty findings (CM-1-3-1-4) and cost-to-serve evidence (CM-1-2-6-1-3); maintaining segment definitions for design use. The design instance of the four-lens discipline (batch 19/21): channel segmentation for design here; marketing-analysis, supply-economics, credit, and party-hierarchy segmentation stay with their lenses; the enterprise's approved channel taxonomy governs classification (batch 22 caveat). Excludes the underlying analyses (CM-1-3-1-4, CM-1-2-6-1); enterprise taxonomy ownership (its owner); portfolio decisions (CM-1-3-3-3-2).\nIn scope: Group channels and participants by role, economics, capability, and service needs | use governed counterparty and cost-to-serve evidence | maintain design-segment definitions\nOut of scope: The analyses (CM-1-3-1-4, CM-1-2-6-1) | enterprise channel taxonomy ownership (its owner) | portfolio definition (CM-1-3-3-3-2)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/conceptKind": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/concept-kind/process"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/dependsOnOutputOf": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-2-6-1-3"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/keyInputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Counterparty findings (CM-1-3-1-4)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1)"
   },
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "cost-to-serve (CM-1-2-6-1-3)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planningLevel": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/planning-level/tactical"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/precedes": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-2"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryOutputFlow": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Channel design segments with definitions"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/primaryPurpose": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Channels grouped so design decisions fit real differences."
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/responsibleDomain": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Commercial & Marketing"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/taxonomyLevel": [
   {
    "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
    "@value": 5
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3-4",
  "@type": [
   "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/references": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/apqc-pcf-7.2.2/element/16592"
   }
  ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/source": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/src-apqc-pcf-001"
   },
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/source/step3c-definition-workbook"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/process/CM-1-3-3-3"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop the structure, size, and engagement model for each channel segment — coverage and capacity, roles and responsibilities between company and channel, and the engagement cadence and support model — completing the designed channel system."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Batch 23 (2026-09-20). Structure step of the channel-design lifecycle; review headcount/organization boundary verbatim in scope note — protects batch 24 Operating Model Design and future Sales Planning; organizational neutrality per b10/15/16/20. APQC 'Develop omni-channel policies and procedures' is a WEAK adjacent match."
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation": [
   {
    "@value": "CM 1.3.3.3.4"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model"
   }
  ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote": [
   {
    "@language": "en",
    "@value": "Covers structure and engagement design: sizing coverage and capacity per channel segment against the portfolio; defining company-versus-channel roles and responsibilities; designing the engagement model (account coverage, support cadence, escalation, program delivery) the partner-management processes will execute; and maintaining the design as the portfolio evolves. The model can define desired roles, coverage, support, service cadence, and capacity assumptions. It does not set HR headcount, operating organization, or individual account assignments; those are addressed through Sales Planning, Operating Model Design, HR, and Channel/Partner Management. Organizational/delivery-model neutrality applies (b10/15/16/20). Structural changes to existing branded arrangements inherit the batch 22 fact-dependent PMPA statement. Excludes partner management execution (CM-1-3-6-1); sales force planning (CM-1-3-5-1); operating model and organizational design (CM-1-3-3-6, HR); individual account assignments (CM-1-3-6-1); franchise legal analysis (Legal). Develop Channel Structure, Size & Engagement Model (CM-1-3-3-3-4) supplies the designed channel coverage, engagement cadence, support model, and company/partner roles that Channel & Partner Management (CM-1-3-6-1) consumes in its defined work.\nIn scope: Size coverage and capacity per segment | define company-versus-channel roles | design the engagement and support model for execution | maintain the design as the portfolio evolves\nOut of scope: Partner management execution (CM-1-3-6-1) | sales force planning (CM-1-3-5-1) | operating model/organizational design and headcount (CM-1-3-3-6, HR) | individual account assignments (CM-1-3-6-1) | franchise legal analysis (Legal)"
   }
  ],
  "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqcMappingDecision": [
   {
    "@id": "https://w3id.org/lsc/ontology/modules/core/apqc-mapping-decision/review-link"
   }
  ],
